\par\par \b \uc2 MONUMENT SECURITIES: Test For Japan's Bank Policy  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,343 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2003\par 17:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Stephen Lewis \par    Of Monument Securities \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Mr Tanigaki, the Japanese minister of finance, after talks with his French counterpart, Mr Mer, has reiterated that the Japanese authorities are ready to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the foreign exchanges to stem rapid or excessive moves in the yen. This is good news for the US bond market, since speculative pressures appear to be building up against the US \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . If Mr Tanigaki backs up his words with deeds, the Bank of Japan (BoJ) could well be set to accumulate dollars on a substantial scale. These would likely be recycled into the US Treasury and agency bond markets. Mr Tanigaki and Mr Mer pledged to co-operate on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  matters but there is not much the French finance minister can do for his part. The treaty setting up the ECB was clear on the exchange rate policy that the euro zone was to pursue. The primary aim of that policy was to be price stability. The implicit assumption was that, in normal times, whatever euro exchange rate emerged from the process of setting monetary policy, with a view to keeping prices stable, would be appropriate. Even so it was recognised that, exceptionally, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  misalignments might occur. Then, the council of finance ministers might issue a 'general orientation' to the ECB indicating how it should conduct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Consequently, in the current situation, euro zone finance ministers would first have to agree that the euro/dollar rate was misaligned and then give some indication to the ECB on how energetic it should be in its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Since most euro zone finance ministers are still maintaining there is no problem with the euro's present level, it would probably be very difficult to find a consensus to take the first step in the process. With \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prop the dollar while the euro zone desists, the dollar's weakness will more likely show up in its rate against the euro than against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Nevertheless, even the BoJ may have to give a little ground in the days ahead as it battles the upward pressures on the yen's exchange rate. The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  seems to have had a relatively easy time recently in holding down the yen, but conditions could be changing. International funds with year-ends on 30 November have been taking profits on Japanese holdings that had sported large gains over the levels at which these investors would have moved into the market in the spring. The flow of funds out of Japan has probably weighed heavily on the yen. This is now completed. Though more profit-taking and outflows are due from funds with December year-ends, this is unlikely to develop until later next month. In the meantime, the yen's performance could well reflect more closely the strong fundamentals of Japan's current account surplus and economic recovery hopes.  \par \par \uc2 Recent data has been just about good enough to keep alive those hopes for a sustained economic upswing. The 2003 Q3 GDP figures relied for the real growth they revealed on questionable estimates of the deflators. However, industrial production numbers for October showed a monthly increase of 0.8%, continuing an upward trend that has persisted since May. METI has tended to over-estimate production in its projections recently, but if output comes anywhere near matching the ministry's latest forecasts, of a 3.1% increase in November to be followed by a 0.9% fall in December, the fourth-quarter contribution of manufacturing to overall growth in the economy should be substantial. But there is a question whether service industries will show much growth. Household spending is still contracting year-on-year, with the stronger showing of discount store sales suggesting that consumers are trading down. A weakening trend in machinery orders may mean that even industrial output will suffer a sudden loss of momentum during the winter ahead. Further, the Japanese authorities face a test in the resolution of the banks' remaining problems that could have a significant bearing on business and household confidence, going forward.  \par \par \uc2 The latest episode in the Japanese bad loan saga centres on the regional Ashikaga Bank. This institution has delayed releasing its figures, though there has been an official statement from the Financial Services Agency (FSA) indicating that its liabilities exceed its assets. An injection of public funds seems imminent but the question is on what terms is the assistance to be granted. The Resona bail-out in June was widely reckoned to be favourable to the banks' shareholders and caused scarcely a ripple in the economy. However, it is possible, now that the Lower House election is past, that tougher terms, more careful of the public purse, will be applied in Ashikaga's case. If so, the rescue could well be taken as an indication that bank shareholders, not least funds in the insurance sector, have been too complacent about prospects, in basing their general view on the Resona example. Needless to say, Ashikaga Bank has several weak borrowers dependent on it; some of these also have shareholdings in the group controlling the bank. The authorities will need to be careful in unravelling these relationships if they are to avoid inflicting economic distress in the Tochigi prefecture, north of Tokyo, where the bank's business is concentrated. Action is likely within the next few days.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    **   For further information, Stephen Lewis: Tel (0)20 7338 0179,   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 or E-mail: sjlewis@monumentsecurities.com ** This document is for information only and is for use of the recipient. It is not to be reproduced, copied or made available to others. This document neither constitutes, or is to be construed as an offer to buy or sell investments. The information and opinions expressed herein are based on sources we believe to be reliable but we do not represent that they are accurate or complete. Any information herein is given in good faith, but is subject to change without notice. No liability is accepted whatsoever by Monument Securities Ltd, employees and associated companies for any direct or consequential loss arising from this document. Monument Securities Ltd is authorised and regulated by the FSA and is a member of the London Stock Exchange, LIFFE and ISMA.Under the Financial Services Authority (FSA) Conduct of Business Rules we are required to classify entities for whom we undertake any ancillary activity relating to designated investment business. Accordingly we have classified you as an Intermediate Customer. In particular any investments or services mentioned herein are not available to private customers. As an Intermediate Customer you are afforded certain protections given by the FSA Conduct of Business Rules. If you do not agree with this classification please contact us immediately. Further if at any time you intend to conduct 'designated investment business' with us please contact us in order that we send you the appropriate terms of business in accordance with the FSA rules. The contents of this notice are in addition to any written agreement that you may have in place with us and any such agreement will take precedence over these terms.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Stephen Lewis: 44 20 7338 0179: analy