\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 1- \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\uc2  Nov  \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention \highlight\uc2  1.5996 trln yen - MOF.  \b0\par\par\uc2 307 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 November 2003\par 11:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Nov 28 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent 1.5996 trillion yen ($14.65 billion) in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in November in an attempt to halt further strengthening of its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The October figure covers the period from October 30 to November 26, during which the dollar fell to a three-year low of 107.52 yen  \par \par \uc2 Traders said the figure was not a surprise and reflected the MOF's firm stance that it would \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the dollar fell towards 107 yen.  \par \par \uc2 "The market's wariness towards \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will increase should the dollar fall towards the mid-108 yen level," said Kenji Kobayashi, senior manager of the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and treasury division at Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi.  \par \par \uc2 Calculated from both Bank of Japan (BOJ) and MOF data, Japanese authorities have spent around 17.8 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so far this year.  \par \par \uc2 The heavy selling by Japanese authorities to try to stem the yen's rise reflects their fear that a strong yen could hurt exports, hindering the economy's tentative recovery and weakening share prices, which are key to the health of the banking system.  \par \par \uc2 In October, Japanese authorities spent 2.723 trillion yen in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  after selling a record 4.4573 trillion yen in September.  \par \par \uc2 On September 30, the MOF \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the New York Federal Reserve, breaking its recent silence on market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The disclosure of the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the unusual move through the New York Fed came little more than a week after the Group of Seven industrial nations called for more flexible exchange rate regimes, which triggered a fall in the dollar from around 114 yen.  \par \par \uc2 As of 1015 GMT on Friday, the dollar was around 109.23/26 yen.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031128dzbs00277\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
