\par\par \b \uc2 OECD: Japan Annual GDP Growth Likely 1.8% In 2004, 2005  \b0\par\par\uc2 874 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2003\par 11:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan looks set to enjoy a modest export-led economic rebound at least through 2005 on the back of accelerating global growth, the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development said Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 But the Paris-based group warned that Tokyo must forge ahead with vital reforms - including a cleanup of banks' huge pile of bad loans - if it hopes to beat deflation and bring the country out of its decade-long slump once and for all.  \par \par \uc2 The OECD also sounded the alarm over the yen's sharp rise and appeared to endorse Japan's aggressive campaign to rein in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, saying market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  had "an important impact" in protecting \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recovery.  \par \par \uc2 In its annual economic outlook, the OECD predicted Japan's gross domestic product would grow 1.8% a year in 2004 and 2005. That would mark a slowdown from projected 2.7% growth this year, but would still beat the paltry 0.5% average expansion in the five years to 2002.  \par \par \uc2 "The rebound in export growth that began in mid-2003 is likely to accelerate through 2004, thanks to a pick-up in the world economy," the OECD said.  \par \par \uc2 But capital spending, the other key engine of Japanese growth this year, is likely to cool "in line with the long-run declining trend in the investment rate," cutting into overall growth.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par            Deflation To Remain Entrenched \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The report highlighted a number of lingering weaknesses in Japan's economy, including the wide gap between export-oriented manufacturers - toughened by years of exposure to global competition - and the inefficient, debt-laden firms that make up much of the domestic economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The recovery is narrowly based in certain manufacturing industries, while the non-manufacturing sector, where restructuring is less advanced, is still constrained by excess debt," the OECD said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The upturn, it added, "is unlikely to be strong enough either to reduce unemployment significantly or to end deflation."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Unemployment is likely edge lower to 5.2% next year and 5.0% in 2005, compared with this year's projected average of 5.3%, the OECD said. Consumer prices, meanwhile, are likely to slip 0.2% in each of the next two years, the same rate estimated for 2003.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The OECD encouraged the Bank of Japan to hold fast to its quantitative easing policy, in which the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  keeps short-term interest rates at virtually zero and keeps the banking system flush with excess cash.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This should remain in place until positive inflation is achieved on a sustained basis and the risk of deflation is negligible," the OECD said, echoing the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's own policy target.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Easy credit alone won't fix Japan's problems, the OECD said. It noted that price falls as measured by the GDP deflator remain severe - the deflator is predicted to stand at -1.3% in 2004 and -0.8% in 2005 - as bank lending continues to fall.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In any event," the report said, "the effectiveness of any monetary policy measures hinges on the health of the banking system."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The OECD noted recent progress in reducing Japanese banks' glut of non-performing loans, but said cutting the ratio of bad loans to around 4.0% of total lending by March 2005 - as the government has pledged to do - will be "a challenge" unless banks take further steps to improve profitability.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The OECD urged Tokyo to halt banks' practice of seeking capital from weak clients in exchange for loans; to scale back lending by quasi-public financial institutions; and to ease rules requiring banks to reserve a minimum share of loans for small firms.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Moreover, any further injections of public funds into the banking system should be highly selective and subject to strict restructuring conditions," it said.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX Intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Has Supported Profits \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The report seemed to bolster \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's case for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets. The yen has appreciated roughly 10% against the dollar this year, with most of its the gains coming since Group of Seven rich countries called in September for more flexibility in exchange rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A sharp rise in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could slow growth while strengthening deflationary pressure," the OECD said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The report listed Japan's estimated Y17 trillion campaign to hobble the yen this year among the policies that have helped underpin the recovery, alongside tax cuts and the BOJ's easy credit stance.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Active \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market has also had an important impact by preventing a significant appreciation of the yen until mid-September, effectively supporting profits and further boosting the monetary base," the OECD said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Repeating a warning that has become a staple of international reports on Japan, the OECD said the country's massive public debt - at over 150% of GDP, more than twice the average OECD ratio - posed an additional threat to future growth.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The government has pledged to balance core expenditures and revenues by the early 2010s, a goal the OECD said "will require substantial cuts in discretionary expenditures, while limiting the rise in mandatory spending by reforming the pension and health care systems."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Jonathan Soble, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; jonathan.soble@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Alastair Gale  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031126dzbq000gx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
