\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Nov 26  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,098 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 26 November 2003\par 10:05\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 =AUSTRALIA WATCH: Monetary policy is probably the wrong tool to combat Australia's housing debt bubble, but the right tool, tax policy, is unlikely to be used. ("=AUSTRALIA WATCH: Monetary Policy Wrong Cure For Housing" ran at 0341 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 GUANGZHOU, China: The shiny new US$2.5 billion airport going up outside Guangzhou is trying to attract more international airlines, in what could be a challenge to nearby Hong Kong's status as the major air hub in southern China. ("=Guangzhou's New Airport To Challenge HK For Intl Flights" ran at 0012 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: A Japanese government bailout of Ashikaga Financial Group Inc., so far denied by regulators and the regional bank, would send a powerful signal on banking reform and support the yen, many \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market participants say. But traders are wary of finance ministry market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to cap any significant yen gains. (\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia column will run around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has plenty of funds to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market if needed, Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki said Wednesday, countering reports that Tokyo is running short of ammunition in its campaign to curb the yen's rise. ("Japan Tanigaki-2: Denies Reports MOF Running Low On Funds" ran at 0706 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Fuhwa Bank recently received approval from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to launch Taiwan's first money market fund. But the fund may be in for a slow takeoff, with investors holding back amid low interest rates. (INTERVIEW will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Factories in Singapore produced more semiconductors, pharmaceuticals and chemicals in October, further evidence the trade-driven economy has recovered and should grow around 1% this year. ("=WRAP: Singapore Oct Output Surge To Drive '03 Econ Growth" ran at 0701 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Construction activity across Australia weakened in the third quarter, raising the prospect that economic growth forecasts for the period will be downgraded in coming days. ("=WRAP: Weak Australian Construction To Shave 3Q GDP" ran at 0406 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DEBT: Moody's Investors Service Wednesday jacked up Thailand's long-term foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rating two notches in a strong vote of confidence in the country's economic prospects - but warned a downgrade for the Philippines is imminent as uncertain politics have begun to affect the government's financial position and the economy. ("=ASIA DEBT: Moody's Hikes Thailand Ratings, Reviews Philippines" will run by 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: International ratings agency Standard & Poor's Wednesday assigned public information ratings to eight mainland China banks, assessing them all at junk grade status. ("=WRAP: S&P Rates China Listed Banks, State-Run Ag Bank" ran at 0518 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Growth in China's producer price index slowed to 1.2% on year in October, the first easing since June, official figures issued Wednesday showed. ("China's Producer Price Index Rises 1.2% On Yr In October" ran at 0540 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Toyota Finance New Zealand issued NZ$100 million of bonds Tuesday to get more long-term funding that better matches its assets, the company's accounting and Treasury Manager Brett Carrington said Wednesday.("INTERVIEW: Toyota NZ Sells NZ$100M Bonds To Fund Growth" ran at 0415 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Christchurch International Airport's credit rating upgrade by Standard & Poor's Corp. came as a complete surprise to the company's Chief Financial Officer Ian Walker, who says it should improve funding costs for the planned domestic terminal replacement. ("=INTERVIEW: NZ Christchurch Airport Rtg Upgrade Surprises" ran at 0553 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: A year out from the next election, Australian major opposition Labor Party leader Simon Crean's future again appears under a cloud. ("Fresh Doubts Emerge Over Fate Of Australian Labor's Crean" ran at 2231 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand business confidence slipped back a touch in November, according to a National Bank Of New Zealand Ltd. monthly Business Outlook survey released Wednesday. The survey also reported a doubling of expectations for higher interest rates over the next year, compared with the previous survey. ("NZ Businesses Less Confident; More Expect Rate Hike -Poll" ran at 1200 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: U.S. funds continued to buy the dollar for yen in Asia Wednesday after strong U.S. data overnight, but Japanese exporter selling capped the gains. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Dlr Up After Strong US GDP, But Exporters Cap" ran at 0542 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Two of China's three largest television manufacturers Wednesday said they are ready to face inspection teams to be sent from the U.S. Commerce Department to determine whether to uphold a preliminary dumping ruling against them. ("China's Major TV Makers Ready To Face US Dumping Probe" ran at 0059 GMT; WRAP will run around 0945 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Lured by state government incentives, Australian flag carrier Qantas Airways said Wednesday its budget airline will be based in Melbourne and start challenging local rival Virgin Blue from next May. ("=WRAP: Qantas Lured To Melbourne For Key Budget \b \highlight22\uc2 Operation\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0707 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Vodafone Group's Australian unit said Wednesday it will commence the first phase of its third-generation services without a partner after discussions with the country's two largest telecommunications companies fell through. ("=INTERVIEW: Vodafone Australia To Set Up 3G Svcs Alone" ran at 0548 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's Coca-Cola Amatil may have to settle for driving growth from its existing businesses after the nation's competition regulator barred it from purchasing unlisted juice company Berri. ("=WRAP: Australian Regulator Blocks C-C Amatil's Berri Buy" first take ran at 0603 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australia's Coles Myer Wednesday joined other major retailers in predicting better sales in the crucial Christmas trading period, with shoppers apparently not discouraged by rising interest rates. ("=WRAP: Australia's Coles Myer Wishes For Bumper Christmas" ran at 0637 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Shares of Yamanouchi Pharmaceutical and Fujisawa Pharmaceutical are looking more attractive as the two discuss the possibility of expanding their alliance - a move that would generate synergies and help them compete with bigger rivals. ("=Asian Stock Focus:Tie-Up Talks Boost Yamanouchi,Fujisawa" ran at 0004 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Shares in major Japanese banks lagged a broader market rally Wednesday, underscoring worries about lenders' ability to continue generating strong profit growth in the face of deflation and weak underlying businesses. ("=WRAP: Japan Banks Lag Tokyo Shr Rally Despite Strong 1H" ran at 0645 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai cement maker TPI Polene has decided to postpone an issue of 300 million new shares indefinitely due mainly to unfavorable market sentiment, an underwriter for the issue said Wednesday. The company was earlier scheduled to announce the offer price Wednesday, ahead of the share subscription between Nov. 27 and Dec. 2. ("Thai TPI Polene Postpones Share Offering Indefinitely" ran at 0100 GMT; WRAP will run around 0915 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Honda Motor's auto sales in Japan rose for the first time in more than a year, in a sign its domestic business might be starting to pick up and support its earnings that have been fueled so far this year by brisk sales in the U.S. (WRAP will run around 0930 GMT, to include sales and production data of other major auto makers)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Creditors of South Korea's Hynix Semiconductor may sell the company's non-memory \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a fund owned by Citigroup for about $600 million, an official at one of Hynix's creditor banks told Dow Jones Newswires Wednesday. ("S Korea Hynix Creditor" ran at 0701 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Ashikaga Financial Group, a Japanese regional bank which has delayed releasing its latest earnings report due to an ongoing inspection by regulators, is required by law to file an audited financial statement to the government by the end of December. ("Ashikaga 1H Results Date Not Set; Faces Dec 31 Deadline" ran at 0701 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Game software distributor Digicube, which is traded on the Osaka Securities Exchange's Hercules market, says it has filed for bankruptcy with the Tokyo district court, leaving behind debts totaling Y9.5 billion. ("Digicube -2: Shares Listed On Hercules Market" ran at 0750 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Matsushita Electric Industrial says it hopes to abolish its seniority-based pay scheme for domestic employees and expand merit-based compensation, a radical move for the conservative electronics company that could spur a broader shift to such pay schemes by corporate Japan. ("Matsushita Aims To Abolish Seniority-Based Salary System" ran at 0431 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Malaysia-based budget airline AirAsia's first international service will fly on Dec. 8 between Kuala Lumpur and Thailand's southern seaside resort island of Phuket, the company's top executive said Wednesday. ("Malaysia AirAsia Intl Flight To Phuket To Debut Dec 8" ran at 0745 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Hitachi Ltd. has reached an agreement with Henan Xinfei Electric for the Chinese home appliance maker to produce Hitachi-brand small refrigerators for the Japanese market, the companies said Wednesday. ("China's Henan Xinfei To Produce Small Fridges For Hitachi" ran at 0317 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Ports of Auckland, New Zealand's main seaport, Wednesday said a 13% surge in container volumes for October doesn't represent a sudden turnaround in fortunes. ("=INTERVIEW: NZ Auckland Port Oct Container Vols Distorted" ran at 0238 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Australasian insurer Tower Ltd. made a small profit in the second half thanks to cost cutting and improved investment markets, but the market isn't convinced the company has turned the corner. ("=WRAP: NZ Tower Makes 2H Profit But Still Found Wanting" ran at 0132 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian wagering group Tab Ltd. will increase its estimate of synergies from a planned merger with Queensland state-based counterpart Unitab, improving the appeal of the transaction, according to a person familiar with the situation. Due diligence on the A$2.8 billion Tab-Unitab merger was completed late Tuesday. ("=Australia's Tab To Increase Unitab Deal Synergies-Source" ran at 0632 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Tony Bell, managing director of Southern Cross Broadcasting, said Wednesday that it makes sense to split some back-office costs with arch rival Macquarie Radio Network even though the two remain fierce competitors. ("=INTERVIEW:Australia's Southern Cross Forms JV With Rival" ran at 0253 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: COSCO Pacific said that two of its units sold their stakes in two industrial paint manufacturers to a sister company, COSCO International, for CNY41.0 million. ("HK COSCO Pacific Sells Assets To Sister Co For CNY41M" ran at 0057 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Digital China said it posted a net loss of HK$91.0 million for its first half, compared with a net profit of HK$88.8 million a year earlier, due to heavy provisions made in the first quarter amid the SARS outbreak. ("HK Digital China/Results -2: Loss Due To Provisions In 1Q" ran at 0641 GMT; WRAP will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FUND VIEW: Hongkong & Shanghai Banking Corp. is counting on higher income from wealth management services to help improve its profitability in coming years. ("=Asia Fund View:Hongkong Bk Eyes Stronger Wealth Mgmt Ops" ran at 0503 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China Pharmaceutical Group, a China-based drug maker with shares listed in Hong Kong, said Wednesday its net profit more than doubled to HK$450.7 million for the nine months ended Sept. 30, from HK$152.3 million a year earlier. ("HK China Pharmaceutical/Earnings -2: Revenue Up 69%" ran at 0632 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Publisher Ming Pao said that China Strategic Holdings sold 23 million shares in the company for HK$1.45 each. The sale reduced China Strategic's stake in Ming Pao to 4.14% from 9.99%. ("HK Ming Pao Says China Strategic Sold 23M Shrs At HK$1.45" ran at 2356 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Power shortages this summer in China prompted many factories to buy diesel-based power generators to produce electricity for their own use, causing a squeeze in China's diesel supply, China-based industry officials said Wednesday. ("Diesel-Based Pwr Generation Causes China Supply Squeeze" ran at 0653 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Australian agribusiness GrainCorp will return to profit this fiscal year, but its performance will still be hurt by the lingering impact of drought, managing director Tom Keene said Wednesday. ("Drought To Hurt Australia GrainCorp's Pfts Again This FY" ran at 0139 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031126dzbq000en\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
