\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar softens, but yen contained by BOJ wariness.  \b0\par\par\uc2 501 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2003\par 04:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  (Reuters offers a scrolling news service for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  professionals who want to follow market developments minute by minute. Click here for the latest \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  news and views   \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Nov 20 (Reuters) - The dollar was softer on Thursday on geopolitical concerns and worries about the U.S. economy, eroding gains it had made from record lows against the euro and three-year troughs against the yen set a day earlier.   \par \par \uc2  Still, wariness of yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese authorities cushioned the decline, keeping trading in relatively narrow ranges.   \par \par \uc2  "I would not say it is falling (against both the euro and yen) anymore. Movement has become much more horizontal," said Mitsuru Sahara, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at UFJ Bank.   \par \par \uc2  "There is not a lot of selling going on," he said. "They (traders) are afraid of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."   \par \par \uc2  The dollar was down by about a quarter yen on the day against the Japanese unit at 0310 GMT but clung near 109 yen - almost 1.5 percent off three-year lows around 107.50 set on Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2  The euro rose about a quarter cent against the dollar to around $1.1915/20 within two-thirds of a cent from Wednesday's record high of $1.1978.   \par \par \uc2  On Wednesday, the Japanese authorities were believed to have stepped into the market to stem the yen's renewed rise. A report in the Nihon Keizai Shimbun financial daily said they sold an estimated one trillion yen ($9.16 billion) for dollars.   \par \par \uc2  JAPANESE EXPORTS   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent about 16 trillion yen on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first 10 months of the year on concern that its export-reliant economic recovery would be choked off by a further rise in its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  revealed on Thursday that Japan's overall trade surplus for October rose to 20.4 percent from a year earlier to 1.0739 trillion yen ($9.83 billion).   \par \par \uc2  Peter Morgan, chief economist at HSBC Securities Japan, said the current exchange rate levels don't seem to be inflicting much damage to Japan's export economy.   \par \par \uc2  "The current level is still okay. If it breaks through 100 (yen to the dollar), it's more of a significant problem," he said.   \par \par \uc2  But Japan's persistent trade surplus was a yen-boosting factor in itself.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said the dollar was under pressure from the expectation that Japanese exporters would be converting dollars earned overseas to the yen.   \par \par \uc2  However, selling was not as brisk as some traders had expected, Sahara said.   \par \par \uc2  Because of this, he said the dollar should settle between 108 yen and 110 yen through the month, despite continued conflict in Iraq and worries about the fallout from Washington's plans to impose import quotas on Chinese textiles.   \par \par \uc2  "Of course, such factors will weaken the dollar, but they are more long-term concerns," Sahara said. "For the time being, people's positions are short, and there will be a lot of room for short covering." ($1=109.16 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031120dzbk000un\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
