\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Investors Look For Bargains  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,890 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2003\par 07:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY: General Electric, Hewlett-Packard Add Cheer \par  \par DJIA          9690.46    gain 66.30     up 0.7% \par NASDAQ        1899.65    gain 17.90     up 1.0% \par S&P 500       1042.44    gain 8.29      up 0.8% \par Dow Future    9699.00    gain 13.00     up 0.1% \par NASDAQ Future 1378.00    gain 2.50      up 0.2% \par S&P Future    1042.50    gain 1.50      up 0.1% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.22% up 0.08 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--A report on housing construction and a bullish outlook from General Electric Co. soothed Wall Street Wednesday and gave investors an incentive to pick up some bargains after four days of declines. Prices of Treasurys fell.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 STOCKS: "You had the record housing numbers, which were encouraging," said John Caldwell, chief equity strategist for McDonald Financial Group. "After several days of declines you have some buyers stepping in.  \par \par \uc2 "The market is a little bit extended, so we're not looking for major moves in either direction," he added. "This market can hopefully tread water through the end of the year."  \par \par \uc2 Michael Palazzi, managing director of equity trading at SG Cowen Securities, agreed. "We've seen the market consolidate over the last four trading days. The market has been very defensive," he said. "What kicked it out of the trend was one, a slightly oversold condition, and two, the announcement by General Electric saying the economy will pick up. It's definitely a market leader."  \par \par \uc2 General Electric rose $1.03 to $29.47 after the company said it will likely increase its quarterly dividend by a penny to 20 cents a share next year, citing in part optimism about an improving economy.  \par \par \uc2 The Commerce Department reported Wednesday that housing construction rose in October to 1.96 million units at a seasonally adjusted annual rate, representing a 2.9% increase from September. It was the highest level of activity seen in 17 years and beat analysts' estimates.  \par \par \uc2 In addition, after the close Hewlett-Packard said its fourth-quarter earnings inched past Wall Street estimates as the computer giant reported strong sales in its traditional printing market, but executives remained cautious about growth in 2004.  \par \par \uc2 For the three months ended Oct. 31, Hewlett Packard earned $862 million, or 28 cents per share, compared with $390 million, or 13 cents per share, in the same period last year.  \par \par \uc2 Excluding special items, HP earned $1.1 billion, or 36 cents per share. That compares with $721 million, or 24 cents per share, in the same period of 2002.  \par \par \uc2 Fourth-quarter revenue was $19.85 billion, up 10 percent from $18.05 billion in the fourth quarter of 2002.  \par \par \uc2 Analysts were expecting HP to earn 35 cents per share cents per share on revenue of $19 billion, according to a survey by Thomson First Call.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Treasurys fell sharply Wednesday as investors booked profits in a market many believed had become overbought while new corporate issuance also helped squeeze prices.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said buyers became more scarce once the yield on the 10-year note reached the 4.12% to 4.14% area earlier in the session, allowing sellers to take charge of market direction.  \par \par \uc2 "Supply is starting to weigh on the market a little bit," said Michael Effron, head of trading at US Bancorp Piper Jaffray in Minneapolis.  \par \par \uc2 Among the issuers, Hong Kong-based conglomerate Hutchison Whampoa priced a $5 billion three-part offering earlier in the session, bringing total corporate supply for the week to around $12.5 billion.  \par \par \uc2 "The market was being whipped around by all that corporate supply" this week, said David Coard, head of fixed income strategy at Williams Capital Group, who believes the higher yields better reflect the economic reality in which output and jobs are gaining traction.  \par \par \uc2 Federal Reserve Bank of St. Louis President William Poole said the Fed's policy outlook depends on data, not on a predetermined timeline, and for the moment U.S. inflation levels are basically stable.  \par \par \uc2 "Given the uncertainty that surrounds the outlook...the (Federal Open Market Committee) members generally view that the committee should not commit itself" to a given policy outlook. Whatever happens with interest rates "will be determined by the outlook, not the passage of time," Poole said.  \par \par \uc2 "I think that the federal funds rate that the Fed sets is where it should be," Poole said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Tokyo Shares Rebound \par  \par USD-Yen             108.87       loss  0.43    dn 0.4% \par AUD-USD             0.7219       gain  0.0007  up 0.1% \par Nikkei 225          9861.67      gain  247.07  up 2.6% \par Hang Seng           11888.13     gain  15.14   up 0.1% \par JGB Yield           1.3500% up 0.0600 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks rose Thursday as investors welcomed Wall Street's overnight gains and the U.S. dollar's recovery against the Japanese yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Technology issues such as Advantest and Tokyo Electron, and banks including Mizuho, Mitsubishi Tokyo and UFJ, gained.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the dollar rebounded after suspected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japanese monetary authorities Wednesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was widely suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  heavily overnight to help lift the dollar up from a fresh three-year low around 107.57 yen. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was estimated by traders to have sold around JPY1 trillion in Tokyo and New York markets on Wednesday to boost the dollar back above 109 yen, Japan's economic newspaper Nihon Keizai reported.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's merchandise trade surplus grew a much wider than expected 20.4% in October from a year earlier to Y1.074 trillion as exports remained firm.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Economists surveyed by Dow Jones estimated on average the surplus would come to Y948.5 billion, up 6.3% from the same month last year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Stocks May Try A Rebound \par                                   +Euro-USD            1.1916      gain  0.0036  up 0.3% \par Stlg-USD            1.6987      gain  0.0010  up 0.04% \par USD-Franc           1.3040      loss  0.0035  dn 0.3%\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European shares are likely to bounce back slightly, while profit-takers trim government bond prices and the euro holds steady just over $1.1900.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European shares are expected to open slightly higher as bargain hunters try to entice big institutional investors back into the market. But that may be an uphill battle, because many major investors are winding down their activity for the year to safeguard their gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London spreadbettor IG Index is calling the FTSE up 9 points to 4336, the DAX up 9 to 3661, and the CAC up 7 to 3350.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks were lower Wednesday for a third consecutive session, as the euro's appreciation against the U.S. dollar acted as an anchor preventing equities from moving ahead.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices are likely to follow the U.S. and Japanese markets lower, as stocks take center stage again.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Euro-zone government bonds were lower Wednesday, on profit-taking from Tuesday's rally and a strong open for U.S. stock markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 France's auction of around EUR3 billion in short and medium-term bonds Thursday is also weighing on the market.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts the session back over $1.1900, ahead of a full plate of economic data, topped off with more Federal Reserve speakers.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar rebounded after hitting record lows against the euro and three-year lows versus the yen Wednesday, helped in large part by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by Japanese monetary authorities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 However, Ed Stapleton, head of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at Fortis Bank New York said he believes the dollar will continue to decline, weighed down by concerns over terrorism and trade.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Right now, with the geopolitical situation and the potential for trade problems between major participants in the global economy, I think the dollar is going to suffer," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Thursday, Nov 20: France GDP; US Jobless; Greenspan \par ET/GMT \par  \par 0200/0700 Germany      3Q Adjusted Final GDP \par 0245/0745 France       3Q Preliminary Gross Domestic Product \par 0330/0830 U.K.         British Bankers Association Lending \par 0330/0830 U.K.         October PSNCR (seen -GBP2.2B) \par 0330/0830 U.K.         Oct M4 Preliminary (seen +0.7% MM) \par 0330/0830 U.K.         October M4 Lending (seen GBP8.8B) \par 0330/0830 U.K.         November Bank Of England Minutes \par 0330/0830 U.K.         October Retail Sales (seen +0.3% MM) \par 0630/1130 Italy        November 12 Cities Consumer Price Index \par 0830/1330 U.S.         Initial Jobless Claims for Nov 15 week \par 0855/135