\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Japan Exporter Sales Pare Dlr's Gains Vs Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Lisa Twaronite  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2003\par 06:34\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese exporter sales pared some of the dollar's overnight gains in Asia Thursday, but the perception that Japanese financial authorities would act again kept dollar bears on a leash.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, when the dollar dropped to a three-year low of Y107.52, the Bank of Japan was said to have sold an estimated Y1 trillion on the Finance Ministry's behalf.  \par \par \uc2 "We saw a ghost yesterday," said a trader at an Asian bank in Tokyo, referring to the unconfirmed but generally acknowledged presence of the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The market remained spooked Thursday, with just a few short-term funds joining exporters in dollar-selling, but many of the usual large fund players were sidelined as they assessed what the latest round of apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  means for the dollar's near-term direction.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "seems to be working, just looking at today's market," says Credit Suisse First Boston strategist Satoru Ogasawara. But in the longer term, he added, "there's not much change in our view that downward pressure on the dollar/yen will continue."  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y108.88, below Y109.34 in New York but above Y108.17 in Tokyo late Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.1920, higher than $1.1888 in New York and $1.1954 in Tokyo. Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y129.78, compared with Y129.96 in New York and Y129.30 in Tokyo Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.6985, compared with $1.6972 in New York and $1.7020 in Tokyo Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Wednesday's apparent \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  underscores \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's resolve to prevent what it has called irregular \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market moves from derailing the nation's ongoing economic recovery.  \par \par \uc2 But early in Thursday's session, the government released data suggesting the impact of yen strength so far has been limited.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's merchandise trade surplus grew 20.4% on year in October to Y1.074 trillion, beating a consensus forecast for a 6.3% rise, as exports remained firm.  \par \par \uc2 Exports rose 5.4% for a fourth straight month of gains, despite an average dollar rate of Y111.41, 10.6% below the average rate of Y123.20 in October 2002.  \par \par \uc2 "Today's trade data suggest exports are doing quite well despite recent yen strength," said Credit Suisse First Boston's Ogasawara.  \par \par \uc2 Japanese exporters also sold euros for yen in addition to dollars Thursday, but in relatively smaller quantities.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro stuck to a narrow Y129.58-Y129.93 range.  \par \par \uc2 The Asian bank trader in Tokyo said he observed "a lot of short-term cash going in and out" of euros for both dollars and yen, but "major funds appear to taking a breather today."  \par \par \uc2 The euro hit an all-time high against the dollar of $1.1978 overnight, but grew heavier and heavier as it approached what market participants described as a formidable options barrier at $1.2000.  \par \par \uc2 In addition to options-defense selling, traders said holders of exotic options with knockouts at that level sold to take profits before the euro touched $1.2000 in spot trading and caused their positions to go out of the money.  \par \par \uc2 The euro got as high as $1.1921 early in the Asian session Thursday, but lacked momentum to go higher in light of the thin activity for other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs.  \par \par \uc2 -By Lisa Twaronite, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; lisa.twaronite@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair Gale  \par \par \uc2 The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \par \par \uc2 Subscribers can find this table:  \par \par \uc2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \par \par \uc2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \par \par \uc2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \par \par \uc2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031120dzbk0006m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
