\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Govt Bonds End Sharply Lower As Stocks Surge Back  \b0\par\par\uc2 751 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 November 2003\par 07:41\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese government bond prices ended sharply lower Thursday as some players actively switched funds to stock futures from bond futures, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 The results of the 20-year JGB auction this session were favorable, keeping falls in cash JGB prices limited in comparison with falls in futures, they added.  \par \par \uc2 Lead December 10-year JGB futures ended 0.44 lower at 137.96, recovering mildly from an intraday low of 137.75. The yield of the newest 10-year cash JGBs was up five basis points to 1.335%. The five-year bond yield rose three basis points to 0.615% while the 20-year bond yield was 3.5 basis points up at 1.845%.  \par \par \uc2 Arbitrage players, possibly foreign hedge funds, started selling bond futures for stocks in the afternoon, traders said. As others followed to cut losses, the fall in bond futures accelerated, traders said. The Nikkei 225 Stock Average ended 2.6% higher at 9865.70.  \par \par \uc2 The much-awaited auction of 20-year bonds was favorable for market sentiment but not enough to stem the selling pressure, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold Y499.3 billion of 1.9% 20-year bonds with the lowest price of 100.80 to yield 1.844%. The lowest price was in line with market expectations, but the bid-to-cover ratio rose to 2.32 from 1.67 at the last month's auction of 20-year bonds.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said short-covering needs at securities houses helped boost demand at the auction. But investors didn't seem to actively buy the issue in the secondary market.  \par \par \uc2 Hedge selling by brokerage houses may also have been a trigger for the futures decline this session, traders added.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Prices as of 0600 GMT: \par  \par    Tiffe Jun 3-Mos Euroyen Price:       99.870  Unchanged \par    SGX Jun 3-Mos Euroyen Price:         99.865  Dn 0.005 \par    TSE Dec 10Y JGB Future Price:        137.96  Dn 0.44 \par    SGX Dec 10Y JGB Future Price:        137.96  Dn 0.31 \par    10-Yr 1.5% JGB No. 255 Yield:        1.335%  Up 0.05 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although the 20-year auction today marked the end of a successful series of key bond auctions, players aren't very optimistic on the near-term direction of the market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We saw very few buyers with the five-year yield below 0.6% and the 10-year yield below 1.3%," said a trader at a major Japanese securities house. "As the market has lost momentum, investors have started to sell."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The bond market had risen recently due to short covering but investors are currently trying to cut losses, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the money market, the Bank of Japan didn't provide any funds for Tuesday. A large deficit is expected for the day.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Market participants think that the BOJ's decision to forego the provision is likely aimed at leaving room for further yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sells yen later today, this would boost the account balances Tuesday when the sale settles. The BOJ will be able to use fund-providing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Friday to make up for any shortfalls, if chooses not to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Separately, the BOJ drained Y800 billion from the money market this session through a sale of discount bills for delivery Friday. The action supported market players view that Japan sold around Y500 billion in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets last night, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Players suspect Japanese authorities were behind the dollar's jump against the yen Wednesday just minutes after it had fallen to a three-year low against the yen. Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki didn't confirm if \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  had \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yen interest rates swaps rose in a knee-jerk reaction to rises in JGB yields but the market was largely quiet, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Two-year swaps rose 0.625 basis point to 0.25125%, five-year swaps were three basis points higher at 0.755%, and 10-year swaps rose 4.875 basis points to 1.3875%.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although the rates rose across the board, players' paying of fixed rates wasn't active.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some players regarded the day's rises as temporary adjustments, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Several U.S. investment bankers, whose fiscal year ends this month, have already finished closing positions in yen rate swaps, traders said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Michiyo Seki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; michiyo.seki@dowjones.com (Hiroshi Miwa contributed to this report.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Bennett Richardson  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1037 4153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031120dzbk0000f\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
