\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Rebounds After BOJ  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 967 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 November 2003\par 22:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar rebounded after hitting record lows against the euro and three-year lows versus yen Wednesday, helped in large part by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese monetary authorities.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was widely suspected of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  heavily overnight to help lift the dollar up from a fresh three-year low around 107.57 yen. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is estimated by traders to have sold somewhere between $5 billion and $10 billion worth of yen to boost the dollar back above 109 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The Ministry has been aggressively trying to keep the yen from appreciating too rapidly and threatening Japan's export-led recovery, selling a record 14.55 trillion yen during the first nine months of this year.  \par \par \uc2 While declining to comment on the suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Japanese Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki Wednesday noted the dollar's "sudden move" lower, and said, "There's no change in our stance that we will take appropriate steps when \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates don't reflect fundamentals."  \par \par \uc2 During the North American session, the dollar continued to climb slowly to just under 109.40 yen, and some traders think the BOJ may have left some additional bids in the market to ensure that the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is successful.  \par \par \uc2 "I believe there's been some covert supportive \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York (Wednesday)," said Joe Francomano, vice president of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Erste Bank in New York. "They accomplished what they set out to do."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has also been able to claw back from fresh all-time lows reached against the euro, which topped out around $1.1979 overnight.  \par \par \uc2 It was also higher against other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in what market players described as a corrective pause in an overall bearish trend for the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "We've had a dramatic move in the dollar across the board, and I think the market's a little tired," said Ed Stapleton, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Fortis Bank New York.  \par \par \uc2 Wednesday afternoon, the dollar was trading around 109.34 yen, well above 108.07 yen late Tuesday in New York. The euro was at $1.1888, down from $1.1960 late Tuesday in New York. It was also at 129.96 yen, above 129.25 yen.  \par \par \uc2 Against the Swiss franc, the dollar was higher at 1.3072 francs from 1.2936 francs. Sterling was lower at $1.6972, compared with $1.7026 Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 Though the New York session was generally quiet, most of the drama was taking place outside of the market as investors tuned in to the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading scam probe that resulted in arrests late Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. authorities announced Wednesday that 47 bankers, brokers and traders had been charged following an 18-month investigation into fraud and other criminal conduct in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market.  \par \par \uc2 One scam allegedly affected thousands of small investors who lost millions of dollars in "boiler-room" type schemes. In another, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders at large banks, including J.P. Morgan Chase & Co. (JPM) and UBS AG (UBS), allegedly engaged in rigged trades in return for kickbacks.  \par \par \uc2 Francomano said he believes there are probably plenty of other scandals that have gone unreported in the largely unregulated market, though the "overwhelming majority" of the business is legitimate.  \par \par \uc2 "I don't think it has any impact on the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market," he added.  \par \par \uc2 After this latest bout of profit-taking, Francomano expects investors to return to selling the dollar despite ongoing indications that the U.S. economy continues down the path of recovery.  \par \par \uc2 On Wednesday, the Commerce Department reported that housing starts unexpectedly jumped 2.9% to a seasonally adjusted 1.960 million annual rate in October. That followed an upwardly revised jump of 4.0% in September.  \par \par \uc2 Yet while Francomano expects the euro to test new highs in coming sessions, he thinks the BOJ has put a damper on investors' urge to sell the dollar against the yen. Thus, the euro will likely rise against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as well, he said.  \par \par \uc2 Stapleton of Fortis believes the dollar will continue to decline across the board, weighed down by concerns over terrorism and trade.  \par \par \uc2 "Right now, with the geopolitical situation and the potential for trade problems between major participants in the global economy, I think the dollar is going to suffer," he said.  \par \par \uc2 China has postponed Wednesday's planned trade mission to the U.S., with The Wall Street Journal citing Chinese officials as saying the move was in response to restrictions on textile imports imposed by the U.S. on Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 In addition, a spokesman at China's Ministry of Commerce said that China regrets the U.S. decision and reserves the right to appeal to the World Trade Organization.  \par \par \uc2 The trade dispute pushed the dollar to a deeper discount against the Chinese yuan in the offshore nondeliverable forward market, which is a proxy for market sentiment on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The yuan's spot price, which the government only allows to trade in a tight band around 8.2770 yen, was unchanged on the day at 8.2767 yen late Wednesday in Shanghai. However, the one-year dollar-yuan nondeliverable forward discount was quoted around 3400-3200, wider than 3200-3000 Tuesday. That implies that market expects the yuan to strengthen about 4.2% against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, the Hong Kong \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep the Hong Kong dollar from appreciating against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . About HK$1.24 billion was sold Wednesday, helping to lift the U.S. dollar to HK$7.7593 from HK$7.7555 Tuesday in Hong Kong.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Colleen DeBaise and Nick Baker contributed to this story.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031119dzbj0005q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw200\pich25\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3000\pichgoal375
