\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Falls to All-Time Low Against Euro   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley and Jamie McGeever   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 987 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2003\par 22:51\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar fell to an all-time low against the euro Tuesday amid a broad-based selloff in the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Market participants came up with a laundry list of reasons behind the euro's surge of over two cents on the day, from concerns about trade and geopolitics to investment outflows. But in general, rising dollar bearishness enabled speculative players to push the market through key technical levels.   \par \par \uc2 In addition to its plunge versus the euro, the dollar fell to fresh six-year lows against the Australian and New Zealand dollars, as well as its weakest level in 3 1/2 years against the South African rand.   \par \par \uc2 "It's difficult to put a finger on the precise news behind" the sharp move lower in the dollar, said Kenneth Agostino, senior dealer at Gain Capital in New Jersey. "There's an overall nervousness about holding U.S. assets."   \par \par \uc2 After a lazy, rangebound session overnight, the selling began in earnest following news the Bush administration has granted U.S. industry requests to impose temporary quotas on some imports of Chinese textiles. That fed into growing concerns the U.S. is edging closer to low-intensity trade wars with its major trading partners, over not just textiles but also steel tariffs and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation.   \par \par \uc2 "In general, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trade flows is a backdoor devaluation," said Ram Bhagavatula, chief economist for North America at Royal Bank of Scotland in New York. "It's an implicit admission that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be weaker."   \par \par \uc2 Perhaps more importantly, the Treasury Department released data showing a big pullback in foreign investment in Treasurys, agency debt and equities in September. The slowdown in net inflows to $4.2 billion has major implications for the dollar because it comes at a time when the ballooning U.S. current-account deficit requires an increasing amount of financing from abroad to keep the dollar from falling.   \par \par \uc2 Alan Ruskin, research director at financial consultancy 4Cast in New York, said the net inflow was the lowest since October 1998. Overall, the Treasury data "were just awful" for the dollar, he noted, adding the "whole quota on Chinese clothing didn't help," either.   \par \par \uc2 The morning news helped propel the euro through key resistance at $1.1860, tripping several stop-loss buy orders. Momentum picked up again in afternoon trading enough to send the euro soaring to around $1.1960, its strongest level since being introduced in January 1999.   \par \par \uc2 Tuesday afternoon, the euro was trading around $1.1960, up sharply from $1.1748 late Monday. Sterling rose to $1.7028 from $1.6917, and the dollar slid to 108.07 yen from 108.93 yen late Monday in New York.   \par \par \uc2 Several traders suspected the Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  covertly by selling yen around 108 yen to keep the dollar from breaking into a free fall.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Still, analysts say the finance ministry could have its work cut out for it, particularly in light of the plunge in foreign-investor interest in U.S. assets during September.     \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's record level of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  that month, buying $40 billion in foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , is also worrisome for the dollar.    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meg Browne, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at HSBC in New York, noted in a research piece that since the BOJ is believed to invest the bulk of its proceeds from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in U.S. government securities, the drop in net purchases of Treasurys to $5.6 billion "raises concern that some central banks are net sellers of bonds."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As a further indication of overall dollar weakness, the trade-weighted dollar index, a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  basket drawn from major U.S. trading partners, hit a new low for its nearly 23-month bear market, at 90.28, according to Stephen Cox, chief technician for Dow Jones Newswires.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The collapse of key technical support for the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  was one factor which prompted perennial dollar bull Citibank to abandon its long dollar positions for now. In a research note, Citibank analysts also cited likely -- if unwarranted -- protectionist fears in the market and the fact that the dollar has failed to benefit from recent upside data surprises.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "While we continue to believe that the U.S. has entered a cyclical rally that will be sustained well into next year, we are concerned that convincing evidence of a sustainable recovery will not be clear enough to support USD gains in the near-term," the Citibank analysts said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The market has largely already priced in a solid U.S. recovery, and is now awaiting signs the Federal Reserve is comfortable enough that the economy has turned the corner to shift into a tightening trend.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hope for an interest-rate hike before March has faded over the past week due to comments from Fed officials, and Federal Reserve Bank of San Francisco President Robert Parry did little to change the market's mind. Citing excess capacity and low inflation, he said, "I think there's still room for some pretty strong growth before the risk of inflationary pressures becomes a primary concern."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Labor Department reported Tuesday that the overall consumer-price index was flat in October, while the nonfood, non-energy core component rose a modest 0.2%.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of all the market-moving news Tuesday, Mr. Parry's "remarkably dovish" comments are "much more powerfully dollar-negative," said Jason Bonanca, director and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Credit Suisse First Boston in New York.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At a time when central banks in the United Kingdom and Australia are beginning to raise rates, "the U.S. is running such a reflationary policy mix," he said.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385; tom.barkley@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Jamie McGeever Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Agnes Crane contributed to this article.)   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031118dzbi00066\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
