\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW:Whiff Of US Protectionism On China Scalds Dlr  \b0\par\par\uc2 926 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2003\par 22:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--While a trade war with China may be a remote possibility, the shots fired across the bows by the Bush Administration Tuesday in placing temporary quotas on certain Chinese imports sure found a target: the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 With the U.S. needing around $500 billion of foreign capital a year to fund its current account deficit, any hint of protectionism or trade war is being perceived as negative for the dollar. In the current climate of extreme dollar bearishness, such uncertainty heightens investor aversion to risk, causing foreign investors to bring their holdings closer to home, only exacerbating the dollar's plight.  \par \par \uc2 "In general, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in trade flows is a back-door devaluation," said Ram Bhagavatula, chief economist for North America at Royal Bank of Scotland in New York. "It's an implicit admission that the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be weaker."  \par \par \uc2 Reports of the temporary quotas on some Chinese textiles had an instant and profound impact on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. They were the catalyst for huge euro buying against the dollar which sent the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a fresh all-time high around $1.1949.  \par \par \uc2 The euro wasn't the only beneficiary - the dollar plunged across the board, hitting six-year lows against the Australian and New Zealand dollars, five-month lows against the Swiss franc, and coming to the brink of new three-and five-year lows against the pound and yen, respectively.  \par \par \uc2 As Meg Browne, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York noted, the dollar suffered a similar fate in March 2001 when the Bush administration imposed tariffs on steel imports. Then, the euro strengthened to $0.8850 from $0.8650 within three days of the tariff imposition.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this month, the World Trade Organization declared these steel tariffs illegal. President George W. Bush said he'll consider lifting them, something which would be seen as a dollar-positive development. But this hasn't happened yet, and some say the Chinese textile quotas show the administration is more concerned with domestic politics one year away from elections than anything else.  \par \par \uc2 "Bush appears set on using trade (and specifically protectionist policy) as a way to increase political support into the November 2004 presidential election," wrote Rebecca Patterson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York, in a note to clients. "Such a policy is likely to weigh on foreign sentiment towards the U.S., and U.S. assets, further. Without those capital inflows to finance the growing (U.S.) current account deficit, the dollar will stay under pressure."  \par \par \uc2 As if right on cue, the importance of these inflows was put under the spotlight Tuesday with the release of dismal Treasury flows data for September. In that month, net securities inflows into the U.S. plunged to $5.6 billion, the lowest level since October 1998 when investor sentiment was severely dented by the collapse of Long Term Capital Management and Russia's debt default.  \par \par \uc2 If it wasn't for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Bank of Japan in September - record purchases of over $40 billion, mostly of U.S. Treasuries - the data would have been even bleaker.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par        Latest China Twist \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Added to this, the Chinese import quotas represent the latest twist in the U.S.-China trade saga that has become a growing focus in U.S. political circles this year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The assumption is that China and other countries could put (trade) restrictions on the U.S.," which will weigh on the dollar, said Sung Won Sohn, chief economist at Wells Fargo in Minneapolis.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Politicians and manufacturers blame the loss of over 2.5 million U.S. manufacturing jobs on China, and specifically its policy of keeping the yuan tightly pegged to the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This means the yuan accompanies the dollar as it slides on the global foreign exchanges, maintaining the competitive advantage of China's exports on world markets, they argue.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Policy makers here have called for China to accelerate moves to abandon the peg, although they admit it will be a long process. And the Group of Seven's September communique on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , which called for more "flexibility" in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy, was seen as a thinly veiled push to get China to let go of the peg.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 September trade data released last week showed the bilateral deficit with China surging to a record $12.69 billion. Imports from China were a record of $14.79 billion, twice their monthly average of just four years ago.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The downside risk for the dollar is that a degree of instability is added to U.S.-China relations, caused by anticipation of how China may react to the quotas, said Naotaka Matsukata, chair of the Strategic International Business Practice of Washington-based law firm Hunton & Williams.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This view was underscored when Citibank - a major presence in the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets - closed all its long dollar positions Tuesday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Citibank's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategists - who have been bullish on the dollar - said in a note that while they remain positive on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the medium term, "it is unreasonable to expect even greater upside surprises that would produce the desired strengthening of the dollar in the near-term."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who reports on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in New York, has previously written about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets from London and Rio de Janeiro.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Robert Flint in New York contributed to this article.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031118dzbi0008q\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
