\par\par \b \uc2 Euro Hits New Record High Vs Dollar;Breaks Above $1.1934  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 779 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 November 2003\par 19:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The euro hit fresh all-time highs against the dollar Tuesday afternoon in New York, breaking above its previous record peak of $1.1934 in a session marked by huge buying of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Having already surged over 1%, broken key resistance barriers and tripped several stop-loss buy orders in the morning session, the momentum picked up again in afternoon trading enough to send the euro soaring to $1.1949.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar plunged across the board, with the New Zealand dollar, for example, making new six-year highs.  \par \par \uc2 The catalyst for the initial pressure on the dollar earlier in the day were reports that the Bush administration has granted U.S. industry requests to impose temporary quotas on some Chinese textile imports. Weak September portfolio flows data - which analysts say showed total foreign investor flows into the U.S. plummeting to their lowest level since late 1998 - added to the gloom surrounding the dollar, and the sheer strength of the momentum took hold.  \par \par \uc2 "There's an overall nervousness about holding U.S. assets," said Kenneth Agostino, senior dealer at Gain Capital in New Jersey.  \par \par \uc2 At 1:40 PM EST, the euro had eased back to $1.1932, but still nearly two full cents up on the day. The dollar was below CHF1.30, touching Y108.00 - within a whisker of three-year lows at Y107.81 - and sterling was firmly above $1.72, again close to making new five-year highs.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar has so far managed to hold around Y108, and some traders suspect the level is being defended through covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , acting on behalf of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "It seems pretty solid," said a trader at a Japanese bank in New York, noting some "rumored semi-official support" at the level. He reckons Y108 will likely hold unless the euro climbs above $1.20.  \par \par \uc2 Another senior dealer at a Japanese bank in New York said a break by the dollar below Y108 could spark more obvious action by the Japanese monetary authorities.  \par \par \uc2 "They have their guns loaded and they have their fingers on the trigger," he said."Once Y108.00 breaks, I think they're really going to do something."  \par \par \uc2 Still, analysts say the finance ministry could have its work cut out for it, particularly in light of the plunge in foreign investor interest in U.S. assets during September. According to financial consultancy 4Cast, the total net inflow of $4.2 billion reported by the Treasury Department Tuesday was the lowest on record since October 1998.  \par \par \uc2 Alan Ruskin, New York-based research director at 4Cast, noted that agency debt showed the second largest outflow ever and also the largest since October 1998. Inflows into Treasurys were dominated by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  - which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  massively in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets during September in a bid to stop a strong yen from derailing a nascent economic recovery. Overall, the Treasury data "were just awful" for the dollar, Ruskin said.  \par \par \uc2 A decrease in foreign investor inflows to the U.S. can have particularly ominous consequences for the dollar, given the U.S. dependance on such flows to finance its burgeoning current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 The tangible effects of these concerns can be seen across the spectrum, with the dollar hitting six-year lows against the Australian and New Zealand dollars. Perhaps most tellingly, the euro is also sustaining its break above the previous record high. A sense that the U.S. might be pursuing more protectionist policies against China only piled further pressure on the dollar. "The whole quota on Chinese clothing didn't help," said Ruskin.  \par \par \uc2 The Bush administration's committee on textile policy voted Monday to invoke the "safeguard" trade sanctions measure on three textile products: knit fabric, bras, and dressing gowns and robes. U.S. firms asked for the trade relief under a provision of China's World Trade Organization accession agreement, which allows temporary quotas if imports are causing "market disruption," Commerce said.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. lawmakers have lobbied the administration to adopt across the board tariffs against China, which they argue is using the virtual peg of the yuan to the dollar to gain global manufacturing market share and eat into U.S. manufacturing jobs.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers say a general increase in risk aversion - amid mounting fears about global terrorism threats - was also weighing on the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 -Jamie McGeever and Grainne McCarthy, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; 201 938 2381; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com; grainne.mccarthy@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Tom Barkley contributed to this article)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031118dzbi0007j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
