\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks To Decline  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,841 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 November 2003\par 06:59\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par US SUMMARY: Economic Reports Add Pressure On Equities \par  \par DJIA          9768.68    loss 69.26     dn 0.7% \par NASDAQ        1930.26    loss 37.09     dn 1.9% \par S&P 500       1050.35    loss 8.06      dn 0.6% \par Dow Future    9714.00    loss 39.00     dn 0.4% \par NASDAQ Future 1402.00    loss 7.50      dn 0.5% \par S&P Future    1044.75    loss 4.25      dn 0.4% \par 10-Yr US Treasury: 4.22% down 0.05 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Wall Street sank Friday as investors sifted through a series of downbeat economic reports. But drug makers including Johnson & Johnson and Merck & Co. got a boost as Congress neared an agreement on Medicare prescription legislation. Prices of Treasurys gained.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 StOCKS: The economic reports included a drop in consumer spending and an uptick in inflation at the producer level during October.  \par \par \uc2 "Advance retail sales weren't so good," said Brian Williamson, an equity trader at The Boston Company Asset Management. "Retailers are a little worried about holiday shopping. They're worried about their profits, so this number confirmed what we saw yesterday."  \par \par \uc2 A Commerce Department report Friday showed that Americans cut their spending in October, depressing retail sales for a second month in a row. The news exacerbated investor concerns after Wal-Mart, the world's largest retailer, said Thursday it was cautious about the outlook for the fourth quarter.  \par \par \uc2 Williamson said investors also were concerned that interest rates could go up faster than expected after a government report suggested inflation was on the rise. The Labor Department said producer prices were up 0.8% in October, the largest increase in seven months. Economists forecast a rise of just 0.2%.  \par \par \uc2 A turnaround in inflation could motivate the Federal Reserve to raise rates.  \par \par \uc2 Also Friday, the Fed reported that industrial production rose by 0.2% in October, after a 0.5% gain in September. October's increase was half the size of the 0.4% increase economists expected.  \par \par \uc2 Some positive news came from a survey by the University of Michigan, which found consumers have become more optimistic about the economy so far this month. The university's consumer sentiment index increased to 93.5 for the first half of November, according to Dow Jones Newswires. Wall Street had expected a mid-November reading of 91.0.  \par \par \uc2 Travelers is in advanced talks to merge with St. Paul, in a deal between the two property-casualty insurers valued at about $10 billion. For Travelers, a deal would be its first big corporate transaction since it was spun off from Citigroup last year.  \par \par \uc2 Morgan Stanley is expected to pay a $50 million civil penalty as part of an agreement with the Securities and Exchange Commission to settle charges relating to the way the firm sold mutual funds, according to people familiar with the matter.  \par \par \uc2 BONDS: Treasurys rallied Friday after more reassuring words from Federal Reserve officials, who indicated that U.S. monetary tightening is a long way off.  \par \par \uc2 It will be "some time" before monetary policy needs to be tightened, Philadelphia Federal Reserve President Anthony Santomero said. "We have an expansionary monetary policy" that is most appropriate for current economic circumstances.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ASIAN SUMMARY: Terror Fear Send Tokyo Shares Over A Cliff \par  \par USD-Yen             109.54       gain  1.24    up 1.2% \par AUD-USD             0.7142       loss  0.0063  dn 0.9% \par Nikkei 225          9777.28      loss  389.78  dn 3.8% \par Hang Seng           11971.51     loss  232.02  dn 1.9% \par JGB Yield           1.3000% down 0.0500 \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Tokyo stocks plunged as much as 4% Monday on fears of terror attacks following reports of an al-Qaida threat.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Meanwhile, foreign selling of Japanese shares caused the yen to fall against the dollar.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Statements attributed to the terrorist network al-Qaida named Japan along with other U.S. allies as potential targets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Prices of U.S. Treasurys were slightly higher in Tokyo trading Monday as Asian investors decided against taking profits on long positions after a rally in the U.S. Friday. Japanese government bond prices were also higher.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Customers are "standing on the sidelines," said one trader. The sharp fall in Japanese share prices and talk of terror attacks is "worrying people."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Markets also discussed the impact of an audio tape purportedly by Saddam Hussein, urging Iraqis to escalate attacks against the Allied occupation and "agents brought by foreign armies."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK: Shares Start Under Pressure \par  \par Euro-USD            1.1794      gain  0.0007  up 0.04% \par Stlg-USD            1.6884      gain  0.0002  up 0.02% \par USD-Franc           1.3256      loss  0.0011  dn 0.09% \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ZURICH (Dow Jones)--European share prices are expected to open lower, with prices of government bonds and the euro slightly higher.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STOCKS: European shares are expected to start lower, with London spreadbettors City Index calling the FTSE down 50 to 4347 and the DAX down 60 to 3740. The "Nikkei has slid below 10,000 in a seriously damaging trading session for the bulls," City Index's Tom Hougaard said. "The Dow fell out of bed Friday night and the weakness has continued into Monday morning. We are now calling the Dow down 50 points already."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stocks ended last week on an upbeat note as pharmaceutical and chemical issues helped push indexes close to fresh highs for the year.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The question now is whether indexes can push through key resistance levels in the run up to Christmas," said Rupert Thompson, strategist at E*Trade.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rolf Elgeti, strategist at Commerzbank, said, "Facing the prospect of a positive return as the end of the year approaches, the desire to lock-in profits amongst equity investors is understandable. However, with the macroeconomic data continuing to strengthen, and corporate news flow supportive, we expect further gains over the coming months."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 BONDS: Euro-zone government bond prices are likely to extend Friday's slight gains.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But analysts said the market may be nearing the end of its short-covering lift.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "My guess is that we are nearing a turning point" of short covering, said Erik Norland, fixed-income analyst at CDC IXIS Capital Markets.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Christoph Rieger, fixed-income analyst at Commerzbank, said "there (is) enough supply (so) that the market is once again long." However, with little due in the way of macroeonomic data this week, he said he expects trading to be erratic.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : The euro starts the session just under $1.1800, higher than its Friday close, but off its overnight high of close to $1.1850.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think that a dollar-bearish sentiment is absolutely appropriate going into this week, but you are starting to come up against some pretty big levels against the euro and the yen," said Lara Rhame, senior economist at Brown Brothers Harriman & Co. in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 She expects pressure to remain on the dollar, but adds, "I just don't know if we're going to see the data or enough market activity to give the euro the momentum to strengthen through its all-time high (around $1.1935) or to give the yen the oomph to push past" the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has been widely suspected of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to defend Y108 in recent sessions.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "You've got rising yields elsewhere in the world, and the Fed is keen to damp yields in the U.S.," said Sean Callow, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at New York-based financial market research firm IDEAglobal, noting recent rate hikes by central banks in the U.K. and Australia.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With interest-rate differentials growing increasingly unfavorable for the dollar, he said that "leaves the U.S. essentially trying to draw capital on the basis of its strong growth and perhaps its equity markets, and less so on its yield attraction."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Several speeches by Fed officials are scheduled for this week, including Fed Chairman Alan Greenspan on Thursday, and they are expected to continue to downplay chances for a near-term rate hike.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Risk aversion could also keep pressure on the dollar, with investors growing increasingly concerned about escalating violence in the Middle East.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold, often a risk-averse investment, is trading just under $400. A break above that key level could lead to further dollar weakness, especially against so-called commodity \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Canada, South Africa, Australia and New Zealand, say analysts. All of the dollar bloc \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  have strengthened to historical levels in recent weeks.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CALENDAR: Monday, Nov 17: U.S. Business Inventories \par 