\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 : Dollar Slides On Poor US Data, Firm Japan GDP  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jonathan Soble  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 465 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2003\par 06:46\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar sank across the board in Asia Friday, continuing a slide sparked overnight by discouraging U.S. data and shrinking prospects for a U.S. interest rate hike early next year.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was also weighed down by slightly better-than-expected Japanese gross domestic product data, although apparent yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  kept the dollar from falling too far.  \par \par \uc2 Several traders said the dollar's biggest enemy was St. Louis Federal Reserve President William Poole, who was quoted in an interview Thursday saying that the Fed can keep short-term interest rates low "well beyond March."  \par \par \uc2 "Pool is known as one of the most hawkish board members, so coming from him, the comments were a real shock," said the head dealer at a European brokerage in Tokyo. "So many people had factored in a rate hike by next spring."  \par \par \uc2 Continued rock-bottom interest rates will make it hard for the U.S. to attract the foreign investment it needs to cover its gaping trade and budget deficits without a sharp fall in the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Data showed Thursday that the trade deficit widened to $41.3 billion in September from an upwardly revised $39.5 billion in August. This was worse than expectations of a $40.5-billion gap. A rise in weekly initial jobless claims added to the bearish tone of the day's figures.  \par \par \uc2 By contrast, the recent turnaround in Japan's economy was highlighted by a 0.6% on-quarter expansion in GDP in the three months to September.  \par \par \uc2 The modest growth, which translates to 2.2% in annualized terms, marked a slowdown from July-September but still beat private analysts' average forecast for a 0.4% on-quarter increase.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar ticked down immediately following the release, but traders said bids by Japanese banks - who often act as conduits for official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  - absorbed most of the sales at around Y108. That kept a solid floor under the U.S. unit and shifted the bulk of its fall to other major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y117.99, below Y108.14 late Thursday in New York. The dollar was at Y108.36 late Thursday in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.1792, higher than $1.1731 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.1645 in Tokyo Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y127.38, up from Y126.86 in New York late Thursday. The euro was at Y126.17 in Tokyo late Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.6892, compared with $1.6852 in New York and $1.6760 in Tokyo Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031114dzbe000av\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
