\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Hurt by U.S. Data, Expectations of Low U.S. Rates   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 1,031 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 November 2003\par 00:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar continued its week-long pullback Thursday, hit by a combination of poor data and diminished expectations that interest rates will start rising anytime soon.   \par \par \uc2 The dollar has been on its back foot since failing to rally on last Friday's jump in U.S. payrolls, and the most recent set of data didn't do much to change increasingly bearish sentiment toward the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. trade deficit widened to $41.3 billion in September from an upwardly revised $39.5 billion in August, worse than expectations of a $40.5 billion gap.   \par \par \uc2 By contrast, third-quarter growth figures out of the euro zone were generally positive, with French gross domestic product coming in slightly higher than expected and Germany moving out of a recession.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. weekly initial jobless claims weren't as rosy as in recent weeks, either, with the number of workers filing first-time applications for unemployment benefits up 13,000 to 366,000 in the week ended Nov. 8. Nevertheless, the claims have stayed below the key 400,000 mark for six straight weeks.   \par \par \uc2 Part of the recent pressure on the dollar is attributable to the market having already priced in a recovery in the U.S., with investors now waiting for a signal that the Federal Reserve will shift into tightening mode.   \par \par \uc2 Any hopes that rate increases are just around the corner were disappointed Thursday by St. Louis Federal Reserve President William Poole, who was quoted in an interview with Bloomberg News as saying that the Fed can keep short-term interest rates low "well beyond March."   \par \par \uc2 "The data today and comments from Poole don't give investors any reason to change dollar positions, so we're continuing to see interest to sell dollars," said Rebecca Patterson, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at J.P. Morgan. "If you have expectations that growth is going to improve, you're going to see U.S. interest rates go up, but you'll also see more foreign buying of U.S. assets, so the dollar will go up."   \par \par \uc2 As the trade gap indicates, the U.S. needs to attract an increasing amount of foreign investment flows in order for the dollar keep from falling. A healthy U.S. recovery isn't likely to be enough to keep the dollar from continuing a steady decline, said market participants.   \par \par \uc2 "Over the longer term, I think most technicians agree that the dollar will still be under pressure based on the deficit," said Enrico Caruso, chief dealer at Tempest Asset Management, a hedge fund based in Irvine, Calif.   \par \par \uc2 Another factor increasingly weighing on the dollar is geopolitical risk, with violence in Iraq escalating in recent weeks. As one indication of growing risk aversion, gold prices hit their highest value in seven and a half years Thursday, peaking just above $398 before sinking back to $394 on profit-taking.   \par \par \uc2 Geopolitical concerns prompted Bank of America analysts to cut their November forecast for the dollar. The analysts ratcheted up their target for the euro to $1.1450 from $1.1200 and raised sterling's forecast to $1.6480 from $1.6000.   \par \par \uc2 "On top of the global terrorist threats, we now have the Iraqi situation spilling over" to the dollar, the analysts said in a research note.   \par \par \uc2 While the euro hit a two-week high of $1.1740, the biggest gainers continue to be the so-called commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Against the Canadian dollar, the U.S. dollar fell to a fresh 10-year low of C$1.2954 after a rise in Canada's trade surplus to C$5.65 billion in September from an upwardly revised C$5.34 billion the month before.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  also hit new six-year lows against the Australian and New Zealand dollars, with the Australian dollar rising to 72.30 U.S. cents and the New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  hitting 63.18 U.S. cents.   \par \par \uc2 Thursday afternoon, the euro was trading around $1.1731, up from $1.1641 late Wednesday in New York. It was also at 126.86 yen, versus 126.61 yen. Against the Swiss franc, the dollar fell to 1.3374 francs from 1.3507 francs. Sterling was higher at $1.6852, from $1.6732 Wednesday. The dollar was also at 108.14 yen, below 108.77 yen Wednesday.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  threatened to fall below 108 yen several times during the session, but was unable to breach that level. Several traders suspected that Japanese monetary officials were in the market for much of the day defending the area above 108 yen. The Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sold a record 13.48 trillion yen in the first nine months of the year trying to keep the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from appreciating too fast and threatening the country's export-led recovery.   \par \par \uc2 "I think they will continue to be there for months to come, at least in a smoothing trend," said Ms. Patterson of J.P. Morgan, referring to the Bank of Japan. "But I don't think they're trying to turn the dollar around or draw a line in the sand at 108 yen."   \par \par \uc2 Japan reports third-quarter gross domestic product on Friday, and a solid number could give investors an excuse to push the dollar to three-year lows below 107.80 yen, market participants say.   \par \par \uc2 In addition, the dollar faces a batch of U.S. data for October on Friday, including the producer-price index, retail sales, industrial production and capacity utilization.   \par \par \uc2 Much of the focus will be on the retail-sales figures, to determine whether consumer demand has held up now that the effects of the tax cuts and ultra-low mortgage rates have mostly warn off, said Ms. Patterson.   \par \par \uc2 "Investors are going to go into the data relatively bearish toward the dollar, so there's going to be more of a reaction if you get a positive surprise to the number," she said.   \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par     tom.barkley@dowjones.com \par     \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031113dzbd0001o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
