\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Official Interest Boosts Euro Foundations  \b0\par\par\uc2 922 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 November 2003\par 20:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A Dow Jones Newswires Column \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Much of the euro's gains on the foreign exchanges recently can be traced to hot money flows, but cooler heads are also lending the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strong underlying support.  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers say Asian central banks, as well as Russian and Middle Eastern financial institutions have all been active in the market recently, quietly buying up the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 This, they say, suggests the euro's longer-term foundations are sound despite the euro zone's relatively weak growth profile. Not only that, but with the dollar seemingly entrenched in a long-term bear market, there's potential for the euro to appreciate further.  \par \par \uc2 Citing "very powerful collective buying interest" for the euro when it dipped below $1.1700 earlier Thursday, a senior dealer at a Japanese bank in New York said there's a lot of talk doing the rounds in the market that official bids are boosting the euro. "I don't know who they are...but that's consistent with what I'm hearing," he said, noting that the euro has been easily able to absorb huge offers in recent weeks.  \par \par \uc2 Tommy Molloy, trader at Bank Leumi in New York, also cited similar dealing floor talk Thursday, that Asian central banks are buying euro possibly for reserve rebalancing purposes, and Middle Eastern accounts for potential "eurofication" of oil revenues.  \par \par \uc2 Asian central banks are the world's largest holders of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves. With around $1.6 trillion in their vaults, they have twice the reserves of all of Europe and the Americas combined. The vast bulk of this is in dollars, but as the dollar weakens on foreign exchanges and the euro cements its credibility as the world's second \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  of choice, some degree of rebalancing is likely.  \par \par \uc2 "Should the dollar's value continue to be unstable, Asian countries will tilt towards the euro in replenishing their \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves," wrote Kwon Tae-Shin, South Korea's deputy finance minister for international affairs in the Financial Times Thursday. This would "threaten" the dollar's global preeminence, he said.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Cloak And Dagger \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such is the secretive nature of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  activity, it's extremely difficult to pin down exact data and activity until official reports are published often months or years later.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some monetary authorities, however, are more transparent than others. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , for example, reports its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  at the end of each month, going into more specific detail at the end of every quarter.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 With around $600 billion, Japan is the world's largest single holder of foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, most of it in dollars. Indeed, this year alone, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has bought nearly $150 billion in market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 interventions\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem the yen's rapid and potentially destabilizing appreciation.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Russian officials have stated that they intend to increase their euro-denominated reserve holdings at the expense of dollars.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 First Deputy Finance Minister Alexei Ulyukayev told Bloomberg news agency earlier this week that, prompted by the dollar's weakness and the importance of Europe as a trading partner with Russia, the country's dollar reserves will be lowered by 3-5 percentage points and the share of euros will grow by the same number.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollars account for 70% of Russia's gold and foreign \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves, 25% are in euros and the remaining 5% in other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  officials later damped speculation this will happen any time soon, much in the same way government officials recently played down speculation that Russia is looking to account oil revenues in euros as opposed to dollars currently.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For sure, euro bulls would be well served by a heavy dose of caution. Since the euro was launched on Jan. 1, 1999, speculation that global central banks - particularly the Asians - would beef up their reserves with euros hasn't really been fulfilled.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Although it's the world's second largest and most traded \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , the euro only accounted for around 15% of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves at the end of last year. Despite the speculation, large reserve holders like China and Taiwan have shown little enthusiasm for taking the plunge any time soon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Getting Ahead Of The Masses \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But change may be afoot.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dealers said Thursday Chinese accounts were among those buying euros under $1.1700, and were also in the market Wednesday. "(I'm) starting to wonder if they're getting more aggressive with reserve adjustments out of dollars into euros for year-end purposes. Beat the crowd mentality, perhaps?" pondered one dealer.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Taking into account the huge sums of money involved - even small shifts of a couple of percentage points - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants are well aware of the impact this could have on exchange rates.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "It's certainly a big issue in the medium term, going into next year," said Ian Stannard, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BNP Paribas in London. "Asian central banks have built up huge dollar reserves recently and they need to rebalance."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Marcel Kasumovich, global head of G-10 strategy at Merrill Lynch in New York, said if central banks are weighing up reserve reallocation decisions, they're likely doing so now.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Now is the time of year when they think about their strategy for next year," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets for Dow Jones Newswires in New York, has previously written about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from London and Rio de Janeiro.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031113dzbd0002v\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
