\par\par \b \uc2 ODJ FOCUS: Dollar May Not Get Lasting Lift From Improving Economy  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,080 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 November 2003\par 15:08\par \uc2 Dow Jones Commodities Service\par \uc2 DJCS\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par   -- Greenback May Benefit In Short Term From Stronger Economic Data \par   -- Dollar Ultimately To Be Checked By Growing Current Account Deficit \par  \par By Allen Sykora \par     Chicago, Nov. 12 (OsterDowJones) - As the U.S. economy continues to \par strengthen, the U.S. dollar may do the same or at least stabilize, but only \par for a while, analysts say. \par     Then one of the bogeymen that has been spooking the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for some \par time may once again take its toll - the massive current account deficit. \par     Yet another factor that could limit the dollar's upward momentum, sources \par said, is the perception that the Bush administration doesn't want to \par the dollar too strong. This is based on a communique adopted by the Group of \par Seven nations this fall. \par     The bottom line is that while some short-term dollar gains are possible, \par analysts are nevertheless looking for the greenback to be softer by the end of \par the year. \par     "We do have a short-term bias for dollar gains, but if you're talking \par about a couple of months to year end, we would expect the dollar to be \par somewhat weaker against the majors," said Sean Callow, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist \par with IDEAglobal. Others expressed similar views. \par     "I think it (the dollar) can pick up next year," said Callow. "But until \par year end, I think it will struggle to attract the foreign capital which it \par needs to finance the deficit." \par     The expectation for Thursday's release of the September trade report is \par for a deficit of around $40 billion, which if maintained year around would \par amount an annual deficit of some $480 billion, said Callow. Meanwhile, the \par annual current-account deficit is in excess of $500 billion. \par     "There's two conflicting stories at play," said David Gilmore, partner \par with \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics, about the dollar's outlook. "One is arguably \par beneficial for the dollar and the other is detrimental. \par     "The beneficial one would be the strong U.S. economy, reflected recently \par in the third-quarter GDP report and the October employment report." \par     The government has reported that gross domestic product grew 7.2% in the \par third quarter. Also, non-farm payrolls rose 126,000 in October, while the \par September increase was revised up to 125,000 from the originally reported \par 57,000. All of this means U.S. yields on fixed-income are likely to rise more \par than most other nations, thus supporting the dollar. \par     "That makes it difficult for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders to dump dollars," said \par Gilmore. "On the other hand, a strong U.S. economy means the U.S. is \par outgrowing the rest of the world and continues to suck in imports, which \par boosts the current-account deficit, which requires more foreign savings with \par more foreign investors buying U.S. assets. So ultimately, that means the \par current-account deficit becomes unwieldly, which arguably it is now, and that \par requires the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to depreciate to improve U.S. competitiveness." \par     Callow said foreign investors are still "lukewarm" about investing in the \par U.S. equity market, despite the gains that have occurred in stocks so far this \par year. In particular, he continued, they are cautious after getting "burned" by \par the bubble in the technology sector a few years ago. \par     "They're likely to make net purchases, trying to benefit from the stronger \par economy," he said. "But it's unlikely to see the rush that we saw in the \par late '90s and early 2001, where the current account widened but there was such \par an inflow of foreign capital trying to get into the tech boom that the dollar \par rose anyway. \par     "The S&P 500 has done well this year, but it has not outperformed a lot of \par the Asian stock markets. It's not the beacon of performance in the world. It's \par rise this year has been pretty much driven by U.S. investors rather than \par driven by foreign capital." \par     Meanwhile, he added, foreign interest in the U.S. bond market is mixed, \par with demand decent for Treasury and corporate products but cool toward agency \par bonds. \par     Callow listed a potential year-end level of around $1.18 to $1.19 for the \par euro against the dollar, while Gilmore forecast around $1.20, all of which \par would be up from its current level of around $1.16. BNP Paribas strategist Bob \par Lynch looks for the euro to initially weaken to around $1.10, before \par recovering back to around the current $1.15 area. \par     Callow and Gilmore said they suspect dollar/yen may fall to around 105 yen \par by the end of the year, while Lynch suggested 106. These forecasts are below \par the current level of around 109 yen. \par     Like the others, Lynch looks for the economy to help the dollar for a \par while. \par     "I think for the next month and a half, there is potential for the dollar \par to hold up well and to rally against the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," he \par said. "Fundamentally, there has been an obvious improvement in the U.S. \par economic data, particularly the labor-market statistics." \par     But by the end of the year, it will likely be slipping again, he said. In \par particular, Lynch suggested the mood in dollar/yen has been altered by this \par fall's G-7 communique, which called on nations to be more flexible about \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market movements. This was seen as encouragement for countries such \par as Japan to stop artificially weakening their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and thus boosting the \par dollar through measures such as \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 . \par     Lynch and Callow pointed out that the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has still been \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  on behalf of the dollar, but appears to be doing so mainly to slow \par the rate of the yen's ascent. \par     "They are not \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to keep dollar/yen as high as it was," said \par Lynch. "They are \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to help curtail a more sizeable decline in the \par \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pair." \par     Dollar/yen has been hurt some by Japanese exporter selling, but the yen \par has also been supported by international investor interest in Japan's stock \par market, Lynch said. Callow pointed out that whereas the U.S. runs a trade \par deficit, Japan has a surplus. \par     "The Bank of Japan and MOF (\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ) are going to have a \par pretty tough task preventing yen appreciation," said Callow. "We think there \par will be more \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  before year end, but more of smoothing rather than \par an attempt to turn the tide." \par  \par    --- \par Allen Sykora, OsterDowJones \par sykora@bendcable.com \par  \par Copyright 2003 OsterDowJones Commodity News (ODJ). All rights reserved. \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJCS000020031112dzbc000a3\par }\page {\p