\par\par \b \uc2 GLOBAL MARKETS-Tokyo stocks fall 3 pct, dollar off 3-yr yen low.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Richard Baum  \par \uc2 731 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 November 2003\par 07:35\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  SINGAPORE, Nov 11 (Reuters) - Tokyo shares slid three percent on Tuesday and bonds rose as Asian technology stocks shadowed falls in U.S. peers, while the U.S. dollar jumped away from three-year lows against the yen on talk of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's Nikkei average hit its lowest close in three months as Softbank Corp, the country's biggest broadband Internet provider, tumbled 10.4 percent on a bigger half-year loss.   \par \par \uc2  MSCI's broadest index of shares elsewhere in the Asia Pacific dropped 1.3 percent by 0600 GMT as contract chip makers sank in Taiwan and Singapore.   \par \par \uc2  "Two straight sessions of falls in U.S. shares were the main factor behind today's weakness," said Lee Sang-jun, an analyst at Bridge Securities in South Korea, where the benchmark index fell 1.1 percent.   \par \par \uc2  Tech stocks retreated on Wall Street after a brokerage house forecast a dip in sales of some Hewlett-Packard Co products. The Dow Jones Industrial Average fell half a percent and the Nasdaq Composite Index sagged 1.5 percent.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar jumped to just above 109 yen as speculation swirled that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  (BOJ) was \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength damaging an economic recovery.   \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials declined to comment on whether they had stepped in to stem the yen's rise. But market sources said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in New York after the dollar matched three-year lows of around 107.85 yen, and continued bidding on Tuesday.   \par \par \uc2  ELECTION LIFTED YEN   \par \par \uc2  The market had been on alert for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as the yen rose following the re-election of the coalition government of Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi at the weekend.   \par \par \uc2  "The market is not longer worrying about whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  - everyone expects \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They are just wondering at what level the BOJ will bid," said Takashi Toyahara, a \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities.   \par \par \uc2  The euro was quoted at $1.1478 versus $1.1473 in late U.S. trade and 125.17 yen against 124.91, well above a 10-month low of 124.18 hit on Monday.   \par \par \uc2  Japanese government bond prices soared. Key 10-year JGB futures hit their highest level in almost two weeks, as the rising yen and falling stocks raised concerns about the country's recovery. U.S. Treasuries inched up in thin trade ahead of a market holiday in the United States.   \par \par \uc2  Gold firmed to $386.50 an ounce from $386 in New York trade as some investors sought safer homes for their money following Sunday's bombing in Saudi Arabia.U.S. oil climbed 13 cents to $31.01 a barrel.   \par \par \uc2  In stock markets, the Nikkei closed down 2.8 percent at 10,207.04, its lowest close since August 19. Softbank fell by its daily 500-yen limit after the company reported on Monday a $710 million loss for the first half.   \par \par \uc2  Resona Holdings Inc, Japan's fifth-biggest bank, slid 8.7 percent on a report that the Japanese government might sell a stake of more than 70 percent that it took in the bank in June.   \par \par \uc2  "The market was initially hit by a rise in the yen and the slide in bank shares," said Masayoshi Okamoto, head of dealing at Jujiya Securities.   \par \par \uc2  "But it was sold far more deeply than what these two factors could have done on the market because Softbank's tumble had stoked fears of massive loss-cutting selling by individual investors."   \par \par \uc2  Mitsubishi Motors Corp fell 4.5 percent after posting a huge interim loss and slashing its full-year forecast. But South Korea's Hyundai Motor Co gained 3.3 percent after reporting an unexpected rise in profits.   \par \par \uc2  Australian stocks fell 0.9 percent to a five-week low after National Australia Bank gave a muted outlook statement, sending shares in the country's biggest bank 2.1 percent lower.   \par \par \uc2  Chartered Semiconductor Manufacturing Ltd slumped 4.7 percent in Singapore on speculation the contract micro chip maker was planning a share sale to raise funds. Singapore's benchmark index was down two percent at midday.   \par \par \uc2  Chartered's larger rival, Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Co, fell 2.9 percent as Philips Electronics reduced its stake. The Taiwan index fell 0.6 percent.   \par \par \uc2  Hong Kong  was 1.7 percent lower at midday.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031111dzbb000c3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
