\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar, Euro Succumb to Yen Strength  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Tom Barkley  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 785 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 10 November 2003\par 22:52\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar fell sharply against the yen Monday in New York, but regained some ground against European \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  following Friday's broad-based selloff of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The yen was the main mover of the day, climbing to its strongest level in 10 months against the euro and retesting last month's three-year highs against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The strength in the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  follows the Japanese parliamentary elections over the weekend, in which Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi's ruling Liberal Democratic Party maintained a slim majority by merging with a coalition party.  \par \par \uc2 However, the implications of the vote on the prospects for economic reform in Japan, as well for the value of the yen, were the subject of debate among \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Jin Saito, vice president of Washington-based advisory service G7 Group, said media reports portraying the outcome as a disappointment for the LDP are mistaken. Predicting that the LDP's majority in the 480-seat lower house could rise to 250 through mergers, Mr. Saito called the election a "clear dominant victory" for the ruling party.  \par \par \uc2 With the dollar unable to garner much lasting strength from the recent string of strong data surprises out of the U.S. -- including a 7.2% surge in third-quarter gross domestic product and Friday's employment report showing 126,000 additional jobs in October -- the LDP's victory gives investors "an excuse to buy more yen," said Mr. Saito.  \par \par \uc2 The yen's rise began overnight against the euro, and several traders said selling of the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could have been driven in part by a large amount of euro-denominated government debt maturities and coupon payments. Japanese investors, thought to be holding a sizable amount of the debt, could be expected repatriate some of that money.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar bounced off 107.86 yen, just above the three-year low of Y107.81 reached Oct. 29. The dollar recovered back above 108 yen in afternoon trading, and some traders noted bids from Japanese banks in activity that could have been covert \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities.  \par \par \uc2 However, the traders said investors may also be covering short dollar positions ahead of Veteran's Day holiday in the U.S. Tuesday. While bond markets will be closed in the U.S., equity markets will remain open.  \par \par \uc2 The Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , has a long history of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to slow the yen's appreciation. The MOF reported Monday it had sold a record-high 7.551 trillion yen ($69.92 billion) in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market in the third quarter, the third-straight quarterly increase. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the first nine months of this year totaled 14.55 trillion yen, topping 1999's record 7.64 trillion yen.  \par \par \uc2 Monday afternoon in New York, the dollar was trading around 108.47 yen, down from 109.30 yen late Friday in New York. The euro was at $1.1496, down from $1.1540 Friday. Sterling was a little weaker at $1.6718 versus $1.6742.  \par \par \uc2 Elsewhere, the dollar fell to its lowest levels in six years against the Australian and New Zealand \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  Monday on expectations that rising interest-rate differentials would continue to support the so-called commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of Australia left little doubt about future rate hikes in Monday's quarterly statement on monetary policy. "A rising exchange rate cannot be relied upon to control inflation over the medium term; that requires domestic pressures to be properly contained," the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said.  \par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar was trading around US$0.7157, up from US$0.7076 late Friday in Sydney. The New Zealand dollar was at US$0.6217, compared with US$0.6147 at Friday's close in Wellington.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators raised their bets on the rising Australian dollar to record levels by last Tuesday, a day before the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  raised interest rates by one-quarter percentage point to 5%.  \par \par \uc2 Noncommercial investors' bets that the Australian dollar will keep appreciating totaled $1.8 billion in the week ended Tuesday, according to latest positioning data from the International Monetary Market on the Chicago futures exchange. It was the eighth-straight week of rising long positions in the Australian \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, speculators' overall bets against the U.S. dollar moderated to $13.7 billion in the latest week, down from $16 billion a week earlier. The speculative community's net-long euro position moderated.  \par \par \uc2 -By Tom Barkley; Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-4385 tom.barkley@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 (Adriana Arai in London contributed to this story.)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031110dzba00010\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex1