\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Slumps Despite Strong U.S. Jobs Data  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 675 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2003\par 23:17\par \uc2 Dow Jones Business News\par \uc2 DJON\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK -- The dollar weakened sharply across the board Friday, completely wiping out gains accrued earlier on the back of surprisingly strong U.S. employment data as rumor-driven trade forced dealers to unwind hefty long dollar positions.  \par \par \uc2 This position-squaring tripped several layers of stop-loss sell orders, which slammed the dollar down from session highs -- six-week highs against the euro and Swiss franc -- to near intraday lows at the close of New York trade.  \par \par \uc2 "Although the numbers were much better than expected, the market was unable to break out," which then forced a turnaround, said Meg Browne, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The excuse given by traders for suddenly dumping dollars was a rumor -- later denied by a government official -- that the U.S. would raise the nation's terror alert status. The rumor caught the market extremely long on dollars and once the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  started to slide, these players were forced to cover their positions, which triggered several stop-loss sell orders.  \par \par \uc2 "I think the terrorist alert change (rumor) spooked people," said a trader based on the West Coast. "Add to that the fact that the market (was) caught long of dollars big time behind improving U.S. data" and it isn't difficult to see why the dollar reversed course, he said.  \par \par \uc2 A U.S. official said later Friday there is no change to the country's terror alert, which remains on "yellow alert, or elevated status" and that there are no plans to raise it.  \par \par \uc2 As reported, data released by the Commerce Department showed that U.S. companies hired at a faster pace in October than the market had expected. Nonfarm business payrolls rose by 126,000 in the month, compared with economists' forecasts of a 55,000 increase. The unemployment rate fell to 6.0% from the previous month, lower than the market's prediction of 6.1%.  \par \par \uc2 The rise in September payrolls was revised up to 125,000 from 57,000, while the 41,000 fall in August was revised upward to a gain of 35,000.  \par \par \uc2 Late Friday in New York, the euro was at $1.1540, up well over a cent from the intraday and new six-week low of $1.1378 and well up from $1.1410 late Thursday in New York.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at 109.30 yen, down from 110.27 yen late Thursday and a high of 110.44 yen after the jobs report was released. The dollar was also at 1.3616 francs, down from 1.3771 francs late Thursday and 1.3803 francs earlier Friday, a new six-week high.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.6742, up from $1.6663 late Thursday and nearly two cents up from the low Friday of $1.6569.  \par \par \uc2 Following the sharp plunge in weekly jobless claims last week of 43,000, expectations of strong data were already running high. The numbers, while clearly above market expectations, don't appear strong enough for some.  \par \par \uc2 "Expectations were so elevated ... and the market was long of dollars going into it," said a dealer at a large U.S. investment bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 Aside from the terror alert rumor, dealers attributed the euro's turnaround to the solid support it received at the lows from Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bids, notably the tenacious defense of a large options-related barrier at $1.1375 from a large Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 There was no sign of Japanese monetary authorities coming into the market to stem the yen's appreciation, though that might change if the dollar falls below 109.00 yen, dealers say.  \par \par \uc2 The head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at a Japanese bank in London said he thinks the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is more likely to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling yen for dollars with the dollar anywhere between 107.70 yen and 109.00 yen.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 1033 1865 20714 4164  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJON000020031107dzb70000w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
