\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Options: Vols Spike Up On Sharp Spot Moves  \b0\par\par\uc2 652 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 7 November 2003\par 20:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Sharp moves in the dollar's spot exchange rates Friday lifted volatilities implied by one-month euro/dollar and dollar/yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  options from low levels traded during Tokyo hours.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar surged across the board early Friday on surprisingly strong U.S. employment data, hitting new six-week highs against the euro and Swiss franc. But it quickly reversed course and headed sharply lower on talk, since denied, that the U.S. government was about to raise the country's terror alert status. This caught the market long on dollars and dealers scrambled to cover these positions, which pushed the dollar down through layers of stop-loss sell orders.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers reported aggressive buying interest for euro downside protection at $1.1300, considered a critical level for the options market.  \par \par \uc2 "If that (support) goes, it will change the whole outlook for the derivatives market," said a senior options dealer at a Japanese bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 But a test of that level seems unlikely for some time now that the euro is back above $1.1500. Its rebound was partly attributed to an impenetrable options-related barrier at $1.1375 placed by a large Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The reversal in the dollar's fortunes also saw the greenback sink more than one full yen to as low as Y109.05, dangerously close to potential Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  territory. The head of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  sales at a Japanese bank in London says Japanese monetary authorities are prepared to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  selling yen for dollars anywhere between Y107.70-Y109.00.  \par \par \uc2 Around 2 p.m. EST (1900 GMT) Friday, in the over-the-counter market, one-month at-the-money (ATM) dollar/yen options implied volatility of 9.50%/9.90%, up from 8.90%/9.30% earlier Friday in Tokyo and 9.10%/9.50% in New York Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Volatilities implied by one-month ATM euro/dollar options were higher at 9.80%/10.10%, up from 9.70%/10.00% and 9.60%/9.90%.  \par \par \uc2 One-month ATM euro/yen options were steadier, implying volatility of 9.65%/9.95% compared with 9.60%/10.00% and 9.75/10.05%.  \par \par \uc2 One-month risk-reversals quarter delta risk-reversals last favored dollar put/yen call options by 0.90%/1.30%, up from 0.60%/0.90% and 0.70%/1.10%, reflecting the market's bearish sentiment toward the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Euro call/dollar put risk reversals were also slightly up at -0.10%/0.30%.  \par \par \uc2 Around 2 p.m. EST (1900 GMT), the dollar was at Y109.14, more than a full yen down from Y110.27 late Thursday in New York. The euro was at $1.1521, up from $1.1410 late Thursday in New York. It was also at Y125.77, little changed from Y125.80.  \par \par \uc2 As reported, data released early Friday by the Commerce Department showed that U.S. companies hired at a faster pace in October than the market had expected. Non-farm business payrolls rose by 126,000 in the month, compared with economists' forecasts of a 55,000 increase. The unemployment rate fell to 6.0% from the previous month, lower than the market's prediction of 6.1%.  \par \par \uc2 The rise in September payrolls was revised up to 125,000 from 57,000, while August was also revised upward.  \par \par \uc2 Following the sharp plunge in weekly jobless claims last week of 43,000, expectations of strong data were already running high. The numbers, while clearly above market expectations, don't appear to be strong enough for some.  \par \par \uc2 "Expectations were so elevated ... and the market was long of dollars going into it," said a dealer at a large U.S. investment bank in New York.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 1077 4127 44427  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031107dzb7000wn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
