\par\par \b \uc2 NY Fed: G7 Communique Accelerated Dlr Depreciation In 3Q  \b0\par\par\uc2 606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 6 November 2003\par 22:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones News Service\par \uc2 DJ\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar's 2.3% depreciation on a trade-weighted basis in the third quarter was accelerated by the Group of Seven communique on exchange rate policies released Sept. 20, according to the Federal Reserve.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 monetary authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  didn't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets during the quarter, said Dino Kos, vice president of the Federal Reserve Bank of New York in the bank's quarterly report to the U.S. Congress report published Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Kos made no mention of Japanese monetary authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  via the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's account with the New York Fed on Sept. 30, when it sold yen for dollars in a bid to smooth the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rapid appreciation. The New York Fed conducts \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  on behalf of the U.S. Treasury Department. In addition, it acts as an agent for foreign central banks. It is standard practice for the New York Fed not to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  carried out on behalf of foreign central banks.  \par \par \uc2 However, in July and August the dollar had actually appreciated 2.2% on a trade-weighted basis amid rising confidence in the strength of the U.S. economy, Kos noted. The surge in Treasury bond yields - the 10-year yield by as much as 95 basis points - reflected the market's faith in good U.S. growth prospects.  \par \par \uc2 This focus on growth differentials lifted the dollar, particularly against the euro, which was blighted by the weak growth outlook for the euro zone. Yields on the 10-year German bund fell below comparable Treasury yields for the first time since February.  \par \par \uc2 But as September approached, optimism surrounding the U.S. economy started to fade as U.S. economic indicators - particularly weekly jobless claims data and the employment report for August - failed to meet bullish market expectations. Speculative traders increased their bets against the dollar the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  started to slide, despite the support offered by Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 This weakening trend was exacerbated by the G7 communique calling for "more flexibility in exchange rates, for major countries or economic areas to promote smooth and widespread adjustments in the international financial system, based on market mechanisms."  \par \par \uc2 This, according to Kos, "resulted in a marked depreciation of the dollar on Monday Sept. 22," and the yen appreciating to a near-three year high later in the week.  \par \par \uc2 The G7 statement also triggered a sharp increase in option-implied volatilities, although taken over the quarter as a whole, they remained within the ranges of recent years.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar depreciated by 2.3% on a trade-weighted basis in the quarter, Kos said. Specifically, the dollar fell 6.9% against the yen, 1.2% against the euro and 0.4% against the Canadian dollar.  \par \par \uc2 At the end of the third quarter, the current value of U.S. monetary system euro and yen holdings totaled $18.6 billion for the Federal Reserve's System Open Market Account and $18.6 billion for the Treasury's Exchange Stabilization Fund.  \par \par \uc2 A significant portion of the U.S. monetary authorities' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves is invested in European and Japanese securities. Foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  reserves are also invested at the Bank for International Settlements in facilities at other official institutions.  \par \par \uc2 As of Sept. 30, the Fed and Treasury's direct holdings of foreign government securities totaled $18 billion, split evenly between the two institutions. Foreign government securities held under repurchase agreements were also split evenly, for a total of $3.8 billion.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJ00000020031106dzb6000l1\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
