\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: US Jobs Data Key To Cementing Dollar Gains   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 944 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 2 November 2003\par 23:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones Emerging Markets Report\par \uc2 EM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Finally, it's shaping up to be a good week for the dollar.   \par \par \uc2 Underpinned by the massive surge in U.S. third quarter gross domestic product growth and a less confrontational stance from the U.S. Treasury toward Asian governments on alleged \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation, the dollar's near-term prospects look brighter than they have for some time.   \par \par \uc2 If Friday's U.S. employment report for October shows signs that the recovery is being accompanied by real job creation, the dollar should strengthen further against its major rivals. Certainly, the conditions for that - a technically favorable picture for the dollar following the surge through key chart points Friday and market positioning - are in place, analysts say.   \par \par \uc2 Other U.S. economic data due for release this week include October national manufacturing numbers and preliminary third quarter productivity figures. But the focus is firmly fixed on the jobs report, with the market looking for evidence that September's 57,000 rise in nonfarm payrolls wasn't a fluke.   \par \par \uc2 "The dollar will probably benefit from an accumulation of good news, but it has to be good jobs news," said John McCarthy, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ING Financial Markets in New York.   \par \par \uc2 Economists surveyed by Dow Jones Newswires and CNBC expect nonfarm payrolls to have risen by 35,000 in October. This would be an encouraging sign that employment is picking up, although not at as fast as a rate of 150,000 a month that many economists say is needed to allow the economy to grow at its true potential.   \par \par \uc2 The U.S. economy expanded at a stunning rate of 7.2% in the third quarter from the year-earlier period, the fastest pace in nearly 20 years. This will slow in the coming quarters as the one-time impact of recent tax cuts fades, but there's little doubt it's supporting the dollar now.   \par \par \uc2 "Post-GDP, we're seeing signs of life," in the dollar, said Phil Suttle, global head of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at JP Morgan in New York. That's not to say analysts think this is the beginning of the end of the dollar's decline, but with the market having bet so heavily against the dollar, some degree of correction is overdue.   \par \par \uc2 "Positioning in the market is still reasonably constructive for the dollar but not overwhelmingly so," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Friday, the dollar hit two-week highs against the euro - with the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trading as low as $1.1566 - and the Swiss franc of CHF1.3421. It also hit 10-day highs of Y110.24 against the yen. Dealers pointed to the GDP numbers and Congressional testimony by Treasury Secretary John Snow last Thursday as the main catalysts.   \par \par \uc2 Against some nervous expectations, Snow didn't accuse Japan of specifically manipulating the yen nor did he comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  aimed at stemming the yen's rise and limiting the impact it has on the competitiveness of Japanese exports. Japan sold Y2.7 trillion in October to smooth the yen's rapid appreciation, bringing the total amount sold by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year to a record Y16.4 trillion. That's around $140 billion.   \par \par \uc2 "There's a bit of a relief trade that stems from (Snow's testimony)...and that's marginally helpful for the dollar," said Robert Lynch, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York.   \par \par \uc2 Late Friday in New York, the dollar was at Y109.90, up sharply from Y108.71 late Thursday, and against the Swiss franc was at CHF1.3408, up from CHF1.3325 late Thursday. The euro was at $1.1585, down from $1.1635, while the pound was lower at $1.6951 from $1.6960.   \par \par \uc2 The euro, which has been strengthening lately more by default than anything else, seems set for a correction lower. Analysts point out that important technical support at $1.1650 has been shattered, and there is a chance of the next key support at $1.1550 will also give way. This would open up a move down to around $1.13, which analysts say is a better level to start building up long euro positions again.   \par \par \uc2 The euro zone economy is also underperforming and the public deficit problems faced by several of the bloc's biggest countries, including France and Germany, will be highlighted by the Tuesday meeting of European finance ministers. "There are few convincing reasons to buy the euro," said ING's McCarthy.   \par \par \uc2 Another major event for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets this week will be the Bank of England's interest rate-setting meeting on Thursday. Economists increasingly expect the Bank's Monetary Policy Committee to raise the benchmark repurchasing rate by at least 25 basis points from 3.5% currently, thereby becoming the first major global \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to begin the monetary tightening cycle.   \par \par \uc2 The pound has already benefited from these expectations, soaring to a five-year high against the dollar earlier this week at $1.7081. On Friday, the euro fell to a fresh six-month low against sterling of GBP0.6827.   \par \par \uc2 But given that interest rate futures in the U.K. are currently pricing in more than 125 basis points of tightening by the end of next year, it's questionable just how much higher the pound can go on the strength of buying based on the fact that interest rates are set to head even higher compared to 45-year lows in the U.S.   \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201-938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com   \par \par \uc2 1141 4137   \par \par \uc2 Dokument EM00000020031103dzb200002\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
