\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Oct 31  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,993 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2003\par 10:16\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Despite signs Japan's economy is picking up pace, price and labor market data issued Friday show deflation remains a problem and jobs growth has yet to feel much of a kick from the export-led rebound. Japan's core consumer price index fell for the 48th straight month in September, slipping 0.1% from a year earlier, while the jobless rate stood unchanged at 5.1% from August. Other data showed spending by Japanese wage earners fell 1.9% in September in real terms from a year earlier, reversing a surprise 2.2% rise in August. ("=WRAP: Japan Data Show Lingering Deflation, Flat Job Mkt" ran at 0425 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The Bank of Japan's policy board Friday voted unanimously to hold monetary policy steady, as expected, despite concerns expressed by some of the harm caused to the economy by the yen's appreciation since August. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  in its semiannual outlook and risk report forecast another year of deflation despite slightly stronger economic growth, implying the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will keep its zero interest rate policy through 2004. ("BOJ Policy" first take ran at 0327 GMT; "BOJ Board/Forecasts" ran at 0617 GMT; WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow may have provided some fodder for dollar bulls Thursday by not castigating China and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday for their \b \highlight22\uc2 interventionist forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies, but technical analysis say this merely provides a more attractive trading opportunity. ("\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: As US Econ Rebounds Charts Point To S$, NT$ Rise" ran at 0357 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's home construction bubble swelled alarmingly in September, bringing closer a rise in official interest rates, perhaps as early as next week. Approvals to build new homes surged 7.5% in September from August, fanned by a 2.1% increase in bank lending for housing, its strongest monthly expansion in 19 years. ("=WRAP: Australia's Housing Bubble Expands, Hike Closer" ran at 0542 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: "Made In India" no longer causes noses to wrinkle in East Asia's boardrooms. Although western companies are still the biggest players by far in India's three-year-old outsourcing boom, a number of heavyweights in East Asia's electronics, technology and automotive industries have recently moved parts of their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  here. And that trend, say business organizations and companies in China, Japan, Taiwan and South Korea, will likely rise sharply. ("=INDIA OUTSOURCING: East Asian Cos Buying Into Boom" ran at 0806 GMT; this is the last in a three-part series)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: The E.U. can help push Beijing toward commonly shared targets and goals, but China will decide its own \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, European Commission President Romano Prodi, who is Beijing for the sixth annual E.U.-China summit, said Friday. ("EU Prodi: Up To China To Decide Its Own \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Regime" ran at 0744 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Visiting E.U. Trade Commissioner Pascal Lamy Friday countered increasingly strident U.S. calls for a loosening in the yuan peg to the dollar with a "friendly" call for China to link the yuan to a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket that includes the euro, yen and dollar. ("EU Lamy/Yuan/Dollar" first take ran at 0527 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA ECONOMY WATCH: Developing countries in Asia and elsewhere are shortchanging themselves by chasing bilateral free trade agreements and turning their backs on the World Trade Organization's multilateral approach. It's a message the WTO has been pushing for years, but it's also a theme that's moving up the agenda of the World Bank as it pursues its mission of reducing global poverty. (=Asia Economy Watch column will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong suppliers still have enough competitive advantages over those in mainland China to command a price premium, a new survey shows, in positive news for Hong Kong's struggle to maintain its role as the key business gateway to China. ("HK Suppliers Still Command Premium Over China - Survey" ran at 0425 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN CREDIT: Credit rating agency Standard & Poor's is taking on its domestic rivals in Japan, sharply increasing coverage of Japanese companies and their debt. The rating agency's move has been welcomed by many. But some credit analysts say S&P's broader coverage may hinder more than help investors, partly because the agency's ratings on a number of companies are too low. ("=JAPAN CREDIT: S&P's Push Into Japan Draws Criticism" ran at 0039 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's inflation was slightly higher than forecast in October as food supply was still tight due to Typhoon Maemi, but consumer demand remained limited indicating economic recovery may still be some way off. ("=WRAP: S Korea Inflation Tame Ahead Of Key Rate Decision" ran at 0622 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Central banker turned New Zealand opposition leader Don Brash rates his biggest challenge before the 2005 general election as convincing the electorate that the National Party is still the country's most potent political force on the center-right. ("=INTERVIEW: NZ's Brash Aims To Restore National's Primacy" ran at 0407 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Business confidence appears to be the only casualty so far in Reserve Bank of New Zealand Governor Alan Bollard's war of words against the country's overheating property market. ("=DATA VIEW: NZ Bollard Aims For Housing, Hits Confidence" ran at 2248 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Unemployment in Singapore rose to a 17-year high of 5.9% in the third quarter, but the economy created new jobs for the first time in two years although not fast enough to cut the official unemployment levels. ("=WRAP: Singapore Jobless Rate At 17-Yr High, But May Fall" ran at 0559 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: The Hong Kong government releases a slew of statistics for September this afternoon: budget deficit, money supply, Exchange Fund balance sheet and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  board account, and visitor arrivals. (stories will run from around 0900 GMT onwards)  \par \par \uc2 =RBI WATCH: On the eve of the Reserve Bank of India's biannual monetary and credit policy announcement Monday, analysts expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its key repurchase agreement on hold amid lingering inflation concerns and an ongoing economic rebound. But it will likely reduce the less-sensitive bank rate in a structural alignment with a late-August repo rate cut to affirm that monetary policy is still on an easing tilt. (=RBI Watch column will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand manufacturing activity for September recorded the fastest growth for 2003 as manufacturers readied for the Christmas shopping season, figures issued Friday show. ("NZ Sep Manufacturing Activity Surges; Biggest Rise In '03" ran at 1131 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =AUSTRALIA CORP BONDS: Australian utility ElectraNet Pty. Ltd. plans to return to the domestic market this year to start refinancing bonds issued after the company's privatization in 2000, and may eventually also tap the market to help fund its network expansion plans. ("=Australia Corp Bonds: ElectraNet Plans Wrapped Deal" ran at 0426 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thailand's exports and manufacturing production grew at a faster-than-expected pace in September as the economy continued to expand, the Bank of Thailand said Friday. ("DATA SNAP: Thai Sep Export Value Hits Record High" ran at 0832 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: India must cut its import tariffs and other trade barriers for the government to achieve its target of exports accounting for 1% of global trade by 2007, says the Confederation of Indian Industry's trade adviser. ("=INTERVIEW:India Must Dismantle Trade Barriers -Indus Grp" ran at 0245 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar continued its climb against the yen in Asia Friday, boosted by the combination of exceptionally strong U.S. economic data and the Japanese authorities' confidence that Washington won't oppose their yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0541 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian budget airline Virgin Blue will list before Christmas after founder Richard Branson Friday agreed to sell down his stake to help fund an ambitious U.S. expansion. Virgin Blue will raise A$500 million from the initial public share offer, which is being coordinated by Goldman Sachs JB Were and Credit Suisse First Boston. ("=WRAP: Australia's Virgin Blue To List Before Christmas" ran at 0614 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: PICC Property & Casualty Co., China's largest general insurer, priced its initial public offering at HK$1.80 a share, at the top end of the offer's indicative price range, making it the biggest share offer in Hong Kong this year, a source close to the deal said Friday. Huge demand from retail investors was rewarded with an increase of their proportion of the IPO to 50% of the shares on offer from 10%. ("China PICC P&C/IPO" first take ran at 0018 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: DBS Group Holdings and United Overseas Bank, Singapore's top two lenders, are both expected to post a 25% on-year rise in third-quarter net profit as provisions shrink due to the brighter economic outlook. However, their operating profits may not excite, with loans and margins showing only modest improvement, analysts said. DBS, Southeast Asia's largest bank, will likely report a net profit of S$249.2 million for the July-September quarter, while rival UOB will likely post a net profit of S$292 million. ("=Singapore DBS, UOB 3Q Net Likely +25% As Provisions Ease" ran at 0359 GMT; results expected after 0900 GMT; combined WRAP will around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: As it prepares for a US$2 billion initial public offering, probably Asia's biggest this year, China Life Insurance Co. is removing the historical burden of negative interest rate spreads from its shoulders. The company, China's largest life insurer, is seeking to list its shares in Hong Kong and the U.S. by year end. ("=China Life Sheds High Return Burden; Seeks US$2B IPO" ran at 0706 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's KT Corp. swung to a third-quarter loss from a year earlier profit, due to a one-time charge stemming from a major reduction in its work force. ("=WRAP:KT Corp Swings To 3Q Loss;Hefty Chg From Staff Cuts" ran at 0815 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: LG Card, South Korea's largest credit card firm, posted a net loss of KRW270 billion in the third quarter from net profit a year ago, as hefty provisions for delinquent card assets continued to hurt its bottom-line. as heavy provisions for delinquent card assets continued to hurt its bottom-line. ("S Korea LG Card/Results" first take ran at 0440 GMT; WRAP will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Minister of Information and Communication Daeje Chin said he welcomes more foreign investment in the country's telecommunications sector, because it will be a key driver in spurring overall economic growth. "The reason why China's economy is expanding so rapidly is because of the rise in foreign capital. For South Korea's economy to grow 1%, we need capital of 6 trillion won," he told Dow Jones. ("=INTERVIEW:Korea Min Urges More Foreign Invest In Telcos" ran at 0514 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: John Fairfax Holdings said Friday that it expects to meet market expectations for first half profit even though important advertising segments in Australia - employment and real estate - remain weak. ("=WRAP: Australia's John Fairfax Tips A$170M-Plus 1H EBIT" ran at 0622 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Commonwealth Bank of Australia, the country's second-largest bank by market value, said Friday that continued strong housing lending and deposits growth had made for a strong first quarter. ("=WRAP: Commonwealth Bank Australia Reports Healthy 1Q" ran at 0604 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: All Nippon Airways (ANA) was hit by turbulence in the wake of SARS and the Iraq war, but deep cost cuts helped Japan's No. 2 airline minimize the damage to its bottom line for the first half. (WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Taiwan's largest commercial carrier, China Airlines, said Friday its nine-month net profit was at NT$408.15 million, or NT$0.15 a share, down sharply from NT$2.25 billion, or NT$0.80 a share, in the year-ago period of 2002, according to an income statement filed to the Taiwan Stock Exchange. A finance department official blamed the SARS outbreak in the second quarter for the poor results, but said business has returned to normal. ("Taiwan's China Air/Earnings" first take ran at 0203 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: A Chinese shipping conglomerate, a mainland carmaker and Hong Kong's stock exchange operator are analysts' leading picks for inclusion in Hong Kong's benchmark index, following a November reshuffle that could lead to the ejection of two long-standing Chinese constituents. ("=China Shrs Tipped For Ejection from Hang Seng Index" ran at 0720 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Sluggish advertising spending and a stumbling foray into the Internet business have prevented Hong Kong publishing firm Next Media from translating the vast popularity of its flashy newspaper Apple Daily into investor returns over the past few years. But as hopes for an economic recovery look set to rejuvenate the media and advertising markets, media mogul Jimmy Lai's publishing flagship is once again tempting investors to take another bite of the apple, with its Taiwan expansion adding extra zest. ("=Asian Stock Focus: Next Media Invites Bite Of The Apple" ran at 0311 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Toyota Motor said it'ss considering launching an auto loan service in China in an effort to boost its sales in the fast-growing auto market. ("Toyota Mulls Starting Auto Loan Service In China" ran at 0345 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Hana Bank said Friday it has signed a final contract to buy a controlling stake in China-based Qingdao International Bank. ("S Korea Hana Bk/Qingdao" first take ran at 0243 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China's largest steelmaker, Baoshan Iron & Steel Co. (Baosteel), said Friday its net profit for the third quarter rose 18% on year, underpinned by a similar improvement in revenue. ("("China/Baosteel/Earnings" first take ran at 0000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Ratchaburi Electricity Generating Holding, Thailand's largest private power producer, Friday announced an on-year improvement in its third-quarter net profit, due chiefly to higher sales and lower interest expenses. ("Thai Ratchaburi" first take ran at 0139 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Shenzhen Development Bank said Friday U.S. investor group Newbridge Capital wants an international arbitrator to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  between it and the Chinese bank's biggest shareholders in a dispute about control of the institution. ("China Shenzhen Devt Bk/Newbridge" first take ran at 0018 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Software giant Oracle Corp., whose traditional clients are the big corporations of the world, has started to court medium-sized companies for new business, according to a senior company executive. (INTERVIEW will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: State Street Global Advisors is treading carefully into China, seeking opportunities in developing exchange-traded funds as well as in managing corporate pension funds, a senior executive said. (INTERVIEW will run around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: NIIT Ltd., an Indian software consultancy and training company, plans to start software development in China by December to tap into the expanding local industry there, said its chief operating officer P. Rajendran, adding that NIIT aims to finalize a tie-up with a Chinese partner in a few weeks to distribute its software. ("=INTERVIEW:India NIIT Plans Software Svcs Launch In China" ran at 0648 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's electronics maker Venture Corp. believes it will benefit greatly from a global trend to outsource electronics manufacturing to lower-cost Asia. ("=INTERVIEW: Singapore's Venture Bullish On Outsourcing" ran at 0652 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 JAKARTA: State-owned Telekomunikasi Indonesia (Telkom) said Friday its nine-month net profit fell 42% on year from the year-ago figure that was boosted by a special gain. Revenue from its cellular \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  surged, lifting Telkom's operating revenue 28% on year. (("Indonesia Telkom/Earnings" first take ran at 0728 GMT; WRAP will run by 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =TECHNICAL TALK: Shares of Singapore's Cycle & Carriage were higher at midday Friday, with technical charts suggesting they may hold onto their gains. ("=TECHNICAL TALK:Singapore Cycle & Carriage May Keep Gains" ran at 0452 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Steven Cain, the new head of Coles Myer's supermarket unit, Friday promised an overhaul for Australia's biggest retailer as it steps up efforts to win back customers from archrival Woolworths. ("=Coles Myer's New Supermarket Boss Talks Up Change" ran at 0447 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Fertilizer and pharmaceutical firm CK Life Sciences, controlled by Hong Kong tycoon Li Ka-shing, reports third-quarter earnings at some point during the day. The company posted a loss of HK$11.1 million in the year-earlier period. said it swung to a net profit of HK$253,000 in the third quarter, from a net loss of HK$11.1 million a year earlier, thanks to an "increase in sales and returns from treasury management." ("HK CK Life Sciences/Earnings" first take ran at 0514 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China's largest offshore oil producer CNOOC Ltd. releases third-quarter production data this afternoon, followed by a press conference at 0915 GMT. (production data will run around 0915 GMT; WRAP will run around 1130 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: Access to the U.S. for agricultural products and Australia's farm marketing monopolies including wheat exporter AWB Ltd. remain key differences in negotiations leading to a proposed free trade agreement between the two countries. ("Australia/US Talks Still Hung Up On Farm Trade" ran at 0123 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 62550  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031031dzav000f9\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
