\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Snow Injects Doubt Into One-Way Yen Trade  \b0\par\par\uc2 745 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2003\par 19:40\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--At last, there's some two-way moves in the dollar's exchange rate with the yen.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 After heading steadily lower against the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent months - most notably since the Group of Seven industrialized nations called for increased flexibility in global exchange rate policies - the dollar reversed course Friday and popped back above Y110.00 for the first time in over a week.  \par \par \uc2 Some of the credit for that can be given to U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow, who in his semiannual Congressional testimony on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market manipulation Thursday, declined to directly accuse Japan of manipulating the yen. Along with exceptionally strong U.S. gross domestic product data Thursday, this was the catalyst for many traders to unwind their short dollar and long yen positions.  \par \par \uc2 This is certainly logical. The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets had been nervous ahead of Snow's testimony that he might crank up the pressure on Japan and others - China and Taiwan, for example - to let their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  float more freely. But the tone was conciliatory not hostile, and there was no finger-pointing or confrontational rhetoric. Snow's softly-softly approach suggests U.S. displeasure with the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's yen-selling market \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  and China's tightly controlled \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  peg, while very real, isn't turning sour.  \par \par \uc2 "There's a bit of a relief trade that stems from that...and that's marginally helpful for the dollar," said Robert Lynch, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BNP Paribas in New York, adding that the dollar could climb up to Y110.50 and above in the coming sessions.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  analysts at ABN Amro say Snow's less confrontational stance on Asian governments' \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies "removes much of the political pressure" on the dollar against Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . They reckon the dollar can climb up to Y111.00 next week, before resuming it downward path, albeit at a more gradual rate of decline than recent months.  \par \par \uc2 Official efforts from the Japanese government look set to only make that more likely. Already in October, Japan sold Y2.7 trillion, according to \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data, to stop the yen's rally from getting out of control. Market participants reckon Japan, given the relatively soft treatment it got from Treasury this week, to continue that policy.  \par \par \uc2 Early afternoon Friday, the dollar was at Y110.20, a high for the week and well up from Y108.71 late Thursday in New York. The euro was also surging against the yen, trading up at Y127.76 from Y126.41  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Rally May Not Last \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But few \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  watchers expect this run to mark the beginning of the end of the dollar's decline against the yen, despite the favorable economic arguments in its favor.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Data Thursday showed that the U.S. economy expanded at a whopping 7.2% in the third quarter, well above expectations and certainly stronger than anything Japan can hope to offer. And while Japan's economy is showing real signs of recovery it remains fragile.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, the sheer weight of capital flowing into Japan to take advantage of even that tentative rebound in the economy means the yen remains poised to strengthen further, even in the face of continued efforts by Japan's finance ministry to slow the move.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Noting this and the weight of flows, Koji Aiyoshi, senior economist at Mizuho Corporate Bank in New York, questions how much higher the dollar can go. "There's still huge upward pressure on the yen...so I'm not sure how long it (the dollar's rally) will continue," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dealers say Friday that Japanese exporters caught out by the dollar's slide below Y108 are now rushing back into the market to sell dollars at these more attractive levels.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "This first round of Japanese exporter sellers are extremely anxious to get on board and unload some of their stale (dollar) longs," said one West coast-based dealer, expecting more export selling to emerge above Y110.50 then Y111.00-20.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Thanks to Snow for disappointing the market and giving the (Japanese) exporters this fine opportunity to salvage some profits," he quipped.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031031dzav0002x\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
