\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar yields to yen strength before Snow hearing.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Christina Fincher  \par \uc2 578 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2003\par 12:09\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 30 (Reuters) - The dollar slid back towards a three-year low against the yen on Thursday as traders waited to see if U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow would criticise Japan for its efforts to curb yen strength.  \par \par \uc2 Snow testifies in a Senate hearing at 1500 GMT on exchange rate policy. Any explicit mention of Japan's recent yen selling/dollar buying forays would underscore concern Washington is looking for a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "Snow will be under pressure to comment on Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation in the question and answer session even if he doesn't in the speech itself," said David Mann, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Standard Chartered.  \par \par \uc2 "The risk is the dollar could weaken on any such comments."  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was down a third of a percent at 107.98 yen by the European midsession, a whisker above three-year lows hit on Wednesday. It also fell a quarter-percent against the euro, lifting the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to session peaks just above $1.1700  \par \par \uc2 The euro toyed with eight-week lows against the yen in early trade before recovering to stand little changed on the day.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. gross domestic product data for the third quarter is due at 1330 GMT and is expected to show growth of six percent or more. Weekly jobless claims are also due for release.  \par \par \uc2 AUSSIE SURGES  \par \par \uc2 Buoyant gold and commodity prices and the prospect of higher interest rates lifted the Australian dollar above US$0.7090 for the first time in six years.  \par \par \uc2 The Reserve Bank of Australia meets on November 4, two days before the Bank of England, and some suspect the RBA may be the first major \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise rates this cycle.  \par \par \uc2 "Higher rates are still the story here," said Jim Webber, chief economist at TD Securities. "Speculation is growing that the Reserve Bank of Australia will hike rates sooner rather than later."  \par \par \uc2 Higher yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have been on a roll against the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week after the U.S. Federal Reserve left rates unchanged and reiterated a dovish outlook.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling, which has also benefited from bets on higher interest rates, held close to five-year highs against the greenback set in the previous session, while the New Zealand and Canadian dollars also held within sight of recent multi-year peaks.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 INTERVENTION\b0 \highlight\uc2  CONCERNS  \par \par \uc2 Although the greenback was weighed down against the yen by speculation that Snow would criticise Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , few traders were ready to sell aggressively, believing the Japanese authorities would step in again to prop up the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 According to market sources, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market from early Tuesday to late Wednesday. Many traders say the Bank of Japan probably bought the dollar around and below 108 yen when it was hitting new three-year lows.  \par \par \uc2 Japan has thrown more than 13.5 trillion yen at the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets so far this year, fearing a rise in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  would jeopardise Japan's export-led recovery.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar has declined for several months on the belief that Washington is seeking a weaker \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  - despite its official strong-dollar policy - to help its exports and to curb a growing U.S. current account deficit.  \par \par \uc2 Its fall accelerated after the Group of Seven leading industrial countries met in September, as the group's statement calling for flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  was interpreted as criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031030dzau002jn\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
