\par\par \b \uc2 MOF Hayashi says Japan to act on  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  volatility.  \b0\par\par\uc2 124 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 30 Oktober 2003\par 09:28\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 30 (Reuters) - Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi said on Thursday that Japan would continue to act against volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets and has plenty of resources available for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "The yen's rise since the G7 (in late September) has been sudden," he told reporters at a regular news conference. "As I have said, we will continue to act appropriately against volatility.  \par \par \uc2 "We have plenty of resources available for \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," he said when asked about recent media reports that the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  may sell some holdings of foreign bonds to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise cash for \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 "There is no need to raise extra cash at present," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031030dzau0021m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
