\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus: No  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Red Card For  \highlight22\uc2 Japan \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 551 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 31 Oktober 2003\par 00:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 There was no red card given today to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for record \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow, though several members of Congress felt one was deserved. The Treasury report on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy among key trading partners allows for a formal listing of a partner country for practicing \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulation. And even China, the real focus of the hearing and report, escaped without a formal listing as a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  manipulator.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Blame global politics. Blame economics. Blame common sense.  \par \par \uc2 Today's hearing was all about the U.S. election next November and blaming someone else for the poor job market performance in the now nearly two-year-old recovery. Unfortunately for the Senate Banking members, the most watched real-time financial cable TV news group, CNBC, chose not to show it. Other more vital programming like Power Lunch took precedence. The fact is CNBC knows what is prime time and what is not. Snow testimony on the dollar, yen and yuan are not. I doubt NBC anchor Tom Brokaw will pick it up either for the most widely watched evening news program in the U.S.  \par \par \uc2 When asked about \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  Snow said he would prefer that the market set exchange rates and assured that this message has been delivered to Japan and that the issue is under discussion through normal bilateral channels. He also noted that dollar/yen has managed to fall significantly and this was proof that the current relationship allowed for adequate flexibility. In other words, Snow showed Japan a yellow card not a red card. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still in the game and the window to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  more ahead, to smooth volatility, remains open.  \par \par \uc2 Does this mean Japan has the green light to whack the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market with dollar/yen buying and run the pair above 110 or 115? Absolutely not. Indeed \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's whole approach to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with the exception of the day it \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  through the Fed, has been toned down since the Dubai G7 meeting. Indeed data from the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  this week should confirm that the amount of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  being conducted by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is declining significantly, even as the pair is near a three-year low.  \par \par \uc2 Japan is not walking away from the bid. It is very likely that it was a key buyer of the two-year U.S. Treasury note auction Tuesday (Wednesday's spot settled today with the notes). Japan will get itself into a pickle with the U.S. if \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  returns to what was seen earlier this summer as an attempt to put a floor under dollar/yen. But smoothing \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , even sizable \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to slow the move down should be expected to continue as long as the dollar trends lower and as long as the Bush administration is perceived as wanting the dollar to move gradually lower.  \par \par \uc2 -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com  \par \par \uc2 (David Gilmore is an economist and partner at \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics, Essex, Conn., which advises institutional \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -market participants on a real-time basis. Neither the author nor \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics trades in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. Opinions expressed are those of the author, and not of Dow Jones Newswires.)  \par \par \uc2 1057 4166  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031030dzau0004o\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
