\par\par \b \uc2 FXAnalytics Focus:Check Expectations Before Snow  \highlight22\uc2 FX \highlight\uc2  Talk  \b0\par\par\uc2 1,148 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 29 Oktober 2003\par 18:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By David Gilmore \par    \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Foreign Exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Analytics \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Mr. Treasury Secretary please beat up on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's unprecedented \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  so my position can go another one to three big figures in my favor. Well that is the state of sentiment at the moment even as dollar/yen hovers at a three-year low under 108.00. But Secretary John Snow will surely head into the meeting deliberately seeking to avoid initiating a dollar sell-off versus the yen or fresh speculation of a near-term shift in China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime. In other words, Snow's goal is to fly under the radar screen in terms of the dollar impact (bond market also vulnerable to dollar weakness and reduced official buying from Asian central banks of U.S. Treasurys) while making political mileage out of sounding tough on fair trade relations with Asia, namely China, but to a lesser extent Japan, and non-Japan Asia. After all there is an election next year and job losses, along with corporate crowing, demands a pro-jobs take on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regimes in Asia, no matter how misguided or otherwise. Film is rolling and voters are watching.  \par \par \uc2 A bit of background information is in order on just what the hearing is all about. Snow has been asked by the Senate Banking Committee to testify on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy manipulation by trading partners in conjunction with the U.S. Treasury's annual report on exchange rate policies of foreign countries to determine whether or not any country is manipulating its exchange rate and it results in an unfair trade advantage. The report is required under the Omnibus Trade Act of 1988 and is presented to Congress yearly with an update at six months.  \par \par \uc2 Why then is it so "new" to the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market? Congress only recently has turned China's yuan policy into an election issue...scapegoating China's low wage manufacturing advantage into an election issue or claim that U.S. jobs are being exported east. For the most part Japan is left out of the job-stealing accusations but does come up in light of its record \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year...aimed at protecting market share in U.S. markets.  \par \par \uc2 In prior years it was rare for the Treasury Secretary to present the report and even rarer for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to follow it (last report update in June is available on the Treasury Web site). It is equally rare that the U.S. Treasury concludes that any country formally manipulates exchange rates to the detriment of the U.S. economy. Maybe South Korea was named once...vaguely recall this. But the typical conclusion in this report is (quoting from the 2002 report updated in June): "This report reveals a wide variety of exchange rate policies used by major trading partners of the United States. Based on a broad review Treasury concluded that no major trading partners of the United States manipulated exchange rates under the terms of the Section 3004 of the Act during the period."  \par \par \uc2 But because the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue is a political hot button, the report will surely suggest at unfair \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy in China and perhaps South Korea and Taiwan, without charging any with manipulation. If any nation is charged with unfair manipulation the Treasury is required to undertake negotiations with the manipulating countries that "have large and material global current account surpluses and significant bilateral trade surpluses with the United States unless such negotiations would have a serious detrimental impact on vital national economic and security interests."  \par \par \uc2 China's global current account surplus is not that large as it runs a deficit with Asia and conceivably could run a current account deficit in coming years. China has also launched an initiative to buy American (see Wednesday's Washington Post) - as in passenger jets and other capital equipment. China is also a huge buyer of U.S. Treasurys, agency debt, corporate debt and stocks as a direct result of the yuan peg. Snow surely is not eager to bite the hand that feeds Treasury and funds the current account deficit in a large way. Moreover, China and the U.S. are already under negotiations over the yuan peg as a result of Snow's trip to Asia earlier this month. Clearly China's role in global security is paramount, especially regarding U.N. Security Council activity on Iraq and in checking nuclear initiatives in North Korea. And then there are the protectionists in Congress eager to pounce. Why fan this flame?  \par \par \uc2 Oh, and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is surely a concern that the Treasury shares with some in Congress and with U.S. manufacturers. Indeed Snow has said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be minimized. But this is clearly a sensitive issue for U.S.-Japan relations, otherwise it would have spilled over in Dubai or Thailand in the last two months. Japan's U.S.-friendly, Iraq-war-backer, post-Iraq-war donor, Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi is facing an election (Diet election Nov. 9). This is not time for Snow to lambaste \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , particularly in the ultra-sensitive Japanese political culture. And dollar/yen is down a lot this year. Sure it would be down more if not for record Y13.5 trillion in \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But most of the dollars \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's government buys through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  end up in U.S. Treasurys (government debt). Thursday's testimony is not the place or time to rip \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its arguably unfair, overboard \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  practices.  \par \par \uc2 One thing the Snow testimony and Treasury report have achieved...\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is itself flying under the \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  radar screen, hoping to avoid exacerbating the criticism of its \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy Thursday. Indeed the nightly musings on why the yen is overvalued by every \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  official under the sun have actually stopped. Oddly the Bank of Japan's Toshihiko Fukui and the Cabinet Secretary Yasuo Fukuda have been the few to speak out on the yen, even as it hit a three-year high versus the dollar. It is not too different from what happened ahead of the September G7 meeting in Dubai.  \par \par \uc2 Thursday's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market should be better remembered for the U.S. third quarter gross domestic product print than what Snow or the Treasury \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  report on \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation reveals (not much). So between ripper of GDP result and an underwhelming testimony/report, it might not be a bad idea to cover dollar/yen shorts and sell higher.  \par \par \uc2 -By David Gilmore, \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign Exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  Analytics; 860-767-9102; fxa@fxa.com  \par \par 