\par\par \b \uc2 BC-MARKETS- \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Shinichi Kishima  \par \uc2 563 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 28 Oktober 2003\par 01:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Oct 28 (Reuters) - The dollar was pinned near three-year lows against the yen on Tuesday as speculation swirled that U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow could condemn Japan's attempts to weaken the yen at a Senate hearing later this week.   \par \par \uc2  Speculation that Washington might take such action, fuelled by a Group of Seven (G7) statement last month calling for flexible exchange rates, was reignited on Monday when Snow said he reiterated to China of the need for more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.   \par \par \uc2  In addition, downward momentum was building up on the dollar as it has been unable over the last few weeks to sustain a bounce above the psychological 110-yen threshold, prompting Japanese exporters and investors to give up on their strategy of waiting for a significant rebound before selling dollars.   \par \par \uc2  "What was initially speculation about U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy after G7 and inflow of foreign investment flows into the Tokyo stock market is turning into a broader move," said Mitsuru Sahara, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at UFJ Bank.   \par \par \uc2  "Exporters are lowering the levels for their dollar-selling orders and Japanese investors are starting to repatriate some of their foreign bond holdings."   \par \par \uc2  At 0015 GMT, the dollar stood at 108.40/43 yen , down marginally from Monday's late New York levels and within a quarter yen of its recent three-year lows.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar was slightly firmer against the euro, hovering at around $1.1730 to the single European \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  , versus $1.1750 late on Monday.   \par \par \uc2  SNOW TESTIMONY   \par \par \uc2  Traders said wariness of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities, who have made no secret of their concern that the yen's strength could choke off the nation's export-led economic recovery, was putting a brake on the dollar's decline for now.   \par \par \uc2  "At these levels, you'd have to be cautious about \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said a dealer at a major Japanese bank.   \par \par \uc2  Traders said the biggest focus in the market was Snow's testimony at a Senate hearing on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies scheduled for Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  He recently repeated the long-standing U.S. Treasury mantra that a strong dollar was in U.S. interest and that exchange rates are determined by the market.   \par \par \uc2  But traders said senators would likely press him to elaborate on his - and the G7's - remarks on "flexible" exchange rates, which have been interpreted in the market as a criticism against the Chinese yuan's virtual peg against the dollar and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's heavy \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold down the yen. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has conducted a record 13.5 trillion yen ($124.5 billion) in yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in just the first nine months of the year. It is due to unveil its \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  figures for October on Friday, probably adding to the tally.   \par \par \uc2  Most of the scheduled events before Snow's testimony were expected to receive only muted reaction, traders said.   \par \par \uc2  While third quarter U.S. gross domestic product figures on Wednesday were forecast to show strong growth of around six percent on an annualised basis, traders said the market had already largely discounted such numbers.   \par \par \uc2  The U.S. Federal Reserve's rate-setting Federal Open Market Committee meeting on Tuesday was expected to yield no change in monetary policy, although the market would closely scrutinise any statement from the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  for hints of its future policy. ($1=108.43 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031028dzas00035\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
