\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar slips vs yen before Snow testimony.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Gertrude Chavez  \par \uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2003\par 22:14\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Oct 27 (Reuters) - The dollar slipped against the yen on Monday, hurt by an overnight rally in Japanese stocks and amid expectations U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow will implicitly encourage a weaker greenback at this week's Senate testimony on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2 , analysts said.   \par \par \uc2  But the greenback rose versus the euro, drawing support from stronger economic prospects in the United States ahead of third-quarter gross domestic product data, scheduled for Thursday. A Reuters consensus survey shows analysts expecting 6 percent U.S. growth in the third quarter.   \par \par \uc2  "There has been a divergence in the dollar's performance. Against the yen, the dollar is weaker because markets are widely expecting Snow will implicitly support a weaker dollar at his Senate testimony this week," said Larry Brickman, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Bank of America in New York, referring to the Senate's statutory semi-annual report on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  on Thursday.   \par \par \uc2  Snow confused the markets last week about the U.S. government's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, saying the Bush administration supports a strong dollar, but noted at the same time that open markets should determine exchange rates. Despite the contradiction in his remarks, markets believed Snow was leaning towards a weaker dollar.   \par \par \uc2  Late Monday afternoon in New York, the euro was down 0.4 percent to $1.1735. Against the yen, the dollar was down 0.5 percent to 108.56 yen. Against the Swiss franc the dollar was at 1.3184 francs, up 0.4 percent on the day. The pound was virtually flat at $1.6942.   \par \par \uc2  The dollar has been on a losing streak against the yen and euro for much of the year, despite Japan's purchases of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  in an effort to weaken the yen.   \par \par \uc2  Other analysts said investors will be reluctant to sell the yen ahead of the U.S. Treasury's report.   \par \par \uc2  "My sense is that markets will be hesitant to sell the yen aggressively ahead of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation report. For dollar-yen, resistance should be 108.80 yen," said Marc Chandler, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at HSBC in New York.   \par \par \uc2  Analysts also said the Nikkei's recovery overnight further buoyed the yen against the dollar after the Japanese stock market's freefall last week.   \par \par \uc2  The Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strengthened to a near three-year high against the dollar as the Nikkei gained 1.15 percent after a more than 6 percent drop last week.   \par \par \uc2  As the yen firmed, the threat of Japanese yen-weakening \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  loomed larger.   \par \par \uc2  Such worries were given an added twist by a report that the Japanese government is considering selling a portion of its roughly 50 trillion yen of U.S. Treasuries and other foreign bond holdings to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  in order to finance \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, The Nihon Keizai Shimbun reported on Monday.   \par \par \uc2  The greenback reacted little to a slight fall in U.S. home sales to a seasonally-adjusted annual rate of 1.145 million.   \par \par \uc2  FED MEETING   \par \par \uc2  Markets are also bracing for the Fed's Federal Open Market Committee policy meeting on Tuesday. Analysts expect the FOMC to leave interest rates unchanged, but will watch for clues about whether the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is becoming more optimistic about the recovering economy.   \par \par \uc2  "Any hint of higher rates would be viewed as a dollar positive," because it would reflect expectations that the U.S. economy is beginning to pick up the pace more strongly, said David Leaver, senior trader with Gain Capital in Warren, New Jersey.   \par \par \uc2  But analysts said the Fed's statement of risks is more likely to emphasize the continuing risk of disinflation, reinforcing the recent decline in expectations for a near-term Fed rate hike. (Additional reporting by John Parry and Wayne Cole in New York and Justyna Pawlak in London).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031027dzar005cm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
