\par\par \b \uc2 FACTBOX-Quotes on China's  \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange \highlight\uc2  policy.  \b0\par\par\uc2 842 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 27 Oktober 2003\par 09:04\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Oct 27 (Reuters) - The following is a selection of quotes on China's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy by politicians, central banks and business leaders since the beginning of October.  \par \par \uc2 Since the mid 1990s, China has kept the yuan in a range of 8.2760 to 8.2800 per dollar. That has attracted criticism from European countries and the United States, who argue that the Chinese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  is undervalued and costing them exports and jobs.  \par \par \uc2 Pressure has risen since September, when the Group of Seven - the United States, Japan, Germany, Britain, France, Italy and Canada - called for greater \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  flexibility, a comment that was seen as a criticism of Asia's policy of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  from rallying and its exports competitive.  \par \par \uc2 That prompted the yen to rally to three-year highs and it dragged many other Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher. However, many Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  have since unwound that rally.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  issue was not raised at the APEC leaders summit in Bangkok earlier this month.  \par \par \uc2 Oct 26: PEOPLE'S BANK OF CHINA GOVERNOR ZHOU XIAOCHUAN  \par \par \uc2 "China would perfect the renminbi (yuan) exchange rate mechanism and maintain the basic stability of the renminbi exchange rate at a reasonable and balanced level."  \par \par \uc2 Oct 24: CANADIAN FINANCE MINISTER JOHN MANLEY  \par \par \uc2 "The Chinese come up often, of course, but many of the southeast Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are essentially tied to the yuan, so it affects them as well.  \par \par \uc2 "As you know, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  quite a bit in the exchange markets to affect the yen, so there's also a factor there which the U.S. has raised with the Japanese."  \par \par \uc2 Oct 22: U.S. TREASURY SECRETARY JOHN SNOW  \par \par \uc2 "We remain convinced that the right answer for China is relying on freely fluctuating exchange rates set in the marketplace."  \par \par \uc2 "I certainly want to keep that dialogue going that we've initiated already on the subject of moving to a more flexible exchange rate and putting in place the financial infrastructure, the interim steps, that make more flexibility possible.  \par \par \uc2 Oct 21: CHINESE PRESIDENT HU JINTAO  \par \par \uc2 "What we are doing now is to study ways to gradually realise the convertibility of the renminbi on the capital account."  \par \par \uc2 Oct 21: JAPANESE FINANCE MINISTER SADAKAZU TANIGAKI  \par \par \uc2 "It's a matter that China must wisely judge for itself."  \par \par \uc2 Oct 19: PEOPLE'S BANK OF CHINA GOVERNOR ZHOU XIAOCHUAN  \par \par \uc2 "With a view to discourage 'hot money' speculation, it is wise to re-endorse stability.  \par \par \uc2 "I should stress here that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  has the capability to sterilise the liquidity impact of inflow of 'hot money' with additional monetary policy instruments."  \par \par \uc2 "The narrowing of the band (in 1997) should not be seen as a change from float to fixed regime."  \par \par \uc2 Oct 16: RESERVE BANK OF AUSTRALIA GOVERNOR IAN MACFARLANE  \par \par \uc2 "The United States is being particularly heavy-handed and is not likely to be very helpful."  \par \par \uc2 "The concentration of the United States on the bilateral trade balance is not really a sensible thing to base policy on."  \par \par \uc2 "It's all a matter of doing it in an orderly, sensible way."  \par \par \uc2 Oct 16: WORLD BANK ECONOMIST MILAN BRAHMBHATT  \par \par \uc2 "A policy of maintaining a stable exchange rate against some other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  like the U.S. dollar leads to some loss of control over the country's monetary policy."  \par \par \uc2 "This is the somewhat the situation in China, where the build-up of reserves has tended to support fast domestic credit growth and the potential for overheating in some sectors, which has become a matter of concern for policy makers."  \par \par \uc2 Oct 14: U.S. SENATE MINORITY LEADER TOM DASCHLE  \par \par \uc2 "We are losing hundreds of thousands, in fact, now millions of manufacturing jobs, many of them going to China, as a direct result of this \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation."  \par \par \uc2 Oct 14: U.S. FEDERAL RESERVE GOVERNOR BEN BERNANKE  \par \par \uc2 "I do think that a purely floating exchange rate would provide some dangers in the current financial system in China."  \par \par \uc2 "Particularly one risk is...that if there were some significant capital outflows...the yuan might actually depreciate, worsening the competitive situation."  \par \par \uc2 Oct 12: IMF DEPUTY DIRECTOR SHIGEMITSU SUGISAKI  \par \par \uc2 "It's difficult to find a persuasive (case) that the renminbi is substantially undervalued."  \par \par \uc2 Oct 9: U.S. TREASURY SECRETARY JOHN SNOW  \par \par \uc2 "We reiterate our long-standing policy on the strong dollar. We're not departing one iota from a strong dollar policy.  \par \par \uc2 "The value of the dollar, as that of any other \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , is best reflected in market forces. We're for the use of competitive market forces."  \par \par \uc2 Oct 7: CHINESE PREMIER WEN JIABAO  \par \par \uc2 "In the course of further financial reform, China will seek an appropriate renminbi exchange rate regime and improve the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime according to its economic development level, economic situation and balance of international payments."  \par \par \uc2 Oct 3: U.S. PRESIDENT GEORGE W. BUSH  \par \par \uc2 "The manufacturing sector is concerned about the playing field being level. This administration will work to level that playing field."  \par \par \uc2 "We've got to have free trade policy that includes fair trade. Fair trade means \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy is fair."   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031027dzar0034r\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
