\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Oct 21  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,347 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 21 Oktober 2003\par 11:14\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Pacific Rim leaders called for a resumption of multilateral talks to remove the North Korean nuclear threat, but made no reference on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, according to a statement read by Thai Prime Minister Thaksin Shinawatra at the end of a 21-nation economic summit Tuesday. The two issues weren't mentioned in the written copy of the communique issued after the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation summit, although they dominated talks on the sidelines. ("APEC Leaders" first take ran at 0740 GMT; WRAP will run after 0930 GMT; further stories will run through the day -events later today include Japanese Prime Minister Koizumi's press conference at 0900 GMT and Chinese President Hu Jintao's press conference at 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: The U.S. was successful in its bid to bring North Korea on the table of Pacific Rim leaders' talks but made no heedway securing a commitment by Asian economies that they will stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to keep the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  down. Even U.S. corporate executives and economists were critical of a rapid \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy change in the region. (WRAP on APEC \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  dispute developments will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Finance Minister Sadakazu Tanigaki Tuesday continued Japan's softly-softly approach on getting China to revalue the yuan, choosing a low profile campaign that might help make Tokyo's own manipulation of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates look less hypocritical. ("Japan Tanigaki" first take ran at 0202 GMT; WRAP will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: General Motors' Chief Financial Officer Tuesday strongly criticized \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's efforts to weaken the yen, saying the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have given Japanese auto exporters an unfair advantage in the U.S. market. ("=WRAP:GM's Fincl Chief Says \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX Interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  Unfair" ran at 0703 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: GMAC Commercial Mortgage \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , K.K. has securitized Y35.84 billion of nonrecourse loans on 30 real estate assets in the largest commercial mortgage-backed securitization of its kind in Japan. ("GMAC Commercial Mtge Japan Breaks Ground With Y35.8B CMBS" ran at 0844 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The Bank of Korea said after a monthly meeting with economists early Tuesday that there's a need to raise interest rates immediately, and aggressively, once the country's economy recovers. ("Bk Of Korea/Rates" first take ran at 0244 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: China is marketing a US$1 billion international bond at 55 basis points over corresponding U.S. Treasuries and US$500 million in five-year euro-denominated bonds at 10 to 13 basis points over the mid-swap rate, according to a market source. ("=China/Marketing Bonds -2: Euro Bonds Seeing Hefty Demand" first take ran at 0741 GMT; WRAP will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's major life insurers will buy more foreign bonds hedged against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risks over the next six months, but at a slower pace than in the previous half year. ("=Japan Life Insurers To Slow Pace Of Foreign Bond Buying" ran at 0421 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ISLAMABAD: Moody's Investors Service raised a key sovereign credit rating for Pakistan, which the country's finance minister said should make a coming Eurobond issue more attractive to investors and cut borrowing costs for the government. ("=WRAP: Moody's Upgrade May Help Pakistan Bond Pricing" ran at 0909 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: The U.S. is undoubtedly in a period of recovery, with the underlying drivers of the world's biggest economy in good shape, leading Australian academic and Reserve Bank board member Warwick McKibbin said Tuesday. McKibbin also said the domestic economy is "chugging along quite nicely" and the coalition government's forecast of 3.25% growth for the current fiscal year ending June 30, 2004 is both "plausible" and "achievable." ("=INTERVIEW/McKibbin" ran at 0344 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 KUALA LUMPUR: Malaysian finance ministry investment arm, Khazanah Nasional Bhd., is planning to issue two new packages of covered warrants for shares of several companies in December, a financial market source said Tuesday. ("=Malaysia Khazanah To Sell More Covered Warrants-Source" ran at 0359 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: Partly state-owned Philippine National Bank has mandated JP Morgan to sell as much as $140 million in subordinated notes that will boost the bank's supplementary, or Tier 2, capital, an industry source privy to the deal told Dow Jones Tuesday. The subordinated notes will have a term of 10 years, non-callable after five years. ("=INTERVIEW: Philippine Natl Bk" first take ran at 0238 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: With moves in short-term U.S. interest rates a major influence on the dollar, technical analysis of three-month Eurodollar futures gives added insight into the outlook for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: 3-Mo Eurodlr Technicals Key For Dlr's Outlook" ran at 0432 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China recorded an easing in retail sales growth in September as the country's consumer price index outpaced expectations by rising 1.1% in the same period, the National Bureau of Statistics, said Tuesday. ("China Retail Sales Growth Eases In Sep;CPI Rise Continues" first take ran at 0504 GMT; DATA VIEW will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: People's Bank of China completely neutralized its sale Tuesday of 10 billion yuan of short-term \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  bills with a like-sized purchase of treasury bonds. ("China PBOC Neutralizes Tightening By Buying Tsy Bonds" ran at 0742 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The Reserve Bank of New Zealand is expected to leave interest rates unchanged Thursday but also signal that the next move in the Official Cash Rate is more likely to be up than down. ("=NZ \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  To Talk Tough But Leave Cash Rate At 5%" ran at 0210 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: The Taiwan government has submitted a plan to the Legislature for a special NT$500 billion five-year project aimed at maintaining a high profile for the island amid growing dominance by its cross-Strait rival on the world stage, a senior official of its top economic planning agency said. The plan will include a trade fair, organized to coincide with the Beijing Olympics in 2008 and intended to steal some of the thunder from China. ("=INTERVIEW: Taiwan To Spend NT$500B To Stay Competitive" ran at 0407 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Visitors to New Zealand rose sharply in September, in a continuing recovery from SARS, but tourist numbers from several Asian countries remain weak, official data released Tuesday show. ("DATA SNAP: NZ Tourism Continues Recovery From SARS" ran at 2151 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar lost ground against the yen in Asia Tuesday, as Japanese exporters wasted no time in cashing in on the greenback's short-covering induced surge to two-week highs overnight. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0531 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 ILSAN, South Korea: Shareholders of South Korea's Hanaro Telecom Tuesday approved a plan to issue new shares worth $500 million to American International Group and Newbridge Capital of the U.S., ending a long duel with LG Group over control of the indebted high-speed Internet access provider. At Tuesday's meeting at Hanaro's headquarters in Ilsan, north of Seoul, shareholders representing 87.7% of total Hanaro shares outstanding were present, of which 75% voted in favor of the AIG-Newbridge investment. ("=WRAP:Korea Hanaro Hldrs OK $500M Invest By AIG,Newbridge" ran at 0858 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Nissan Motor President Carlos Ghosn said Tuesday he hopes to reduce the company's exposure to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates in order to limit the impact of the yen's recent rise. ("=INTERVIEW/Nissan" first take ran at 0439 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's competition watchdog fired a shot across the bow of National Australia Bank on Tuesday, warning it will have a say in any takeover bid for AMP Ltd. In a blunt, four-sentence statement sent to the stock exchange, the Australian Competition and Consumer Commission said any proposal from NAB will be judged against Section 50 of the Trade Practices Act, which prohibits mergers or acquisitions likely to substantially reduce competition in a major market for goods and services. ("=WRAP: Australia ACCC To Have Say In Any NAB Bid For AMP" ran at 0532 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: NTN Corp. and Nidec Corp. say their joint venture will more than double production capacity of bearing units for use in hard disk drives, to coincide with rapidly growing demand. ("NTN/Nidec -2: China JV To Spend Y2B To Boost Capacity" first take ran at 0301 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Hong Kong mobile operator CSL is scouring new markets in southern China, possibly thorough extending alliances with China Mobile or China Unicom to tap fresh earnings streams beyond Hong Kong's cutthroat telecom market. ("=WRAP: Telstra's HK Unit Scours New S China Markets" ran at 0725 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China Kejian Corp. said Tuesday Nanjing Hezong Investment has agreed to buy out one of its key shareholders, a move that will indirectly give the investment company the largest stake in the Chinese mobile phone maker. ("China Kejian: Change In Largest Hldr; No Impact On Opers" ran at 0312 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australia's QBE Insurance Group said Tuesday it is considering up to 11 acquisitions. Chief executive Frank O'Halloran told reporters about eight of these were in Australia and the Asia Pacific region. The company, which does most of its business outside Australia and is one of the biggest underwriters in the Lloyds of London market, has a strong record of growth through acquisitions. ("=WRAP: Australia's QBE Considering 11 Acquisitions" ran at 0614 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China has approved the sale of a 1.22% stake in China Minsheng Banking to the International Finance Corp., the private investment arm of the World Bank Group. (story will likely run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Private equity interest in both China acquisitions and state bank nonperforming loans will help drive the expansion of O'Melveny & Myers law offices in China, OMM Chairman Arthur B. Culvahouse Junior said in a recent interview. ("=INTERVIEW: O'Melveny & Myers Tips Rapid China Expansion" ran at 0137 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Mayne Group agreed Tuesday to sell its ailing hospitals portfolio for A$813 million, ending a tumultuous foray into an industry that led to financial heartache at the Australian health care company. ("=WRAP: Australia's Mayne Group Sells Ailing Hospitals" ran at 0719 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Australian building materials group Boral Tuesday upgraded its annual profit guidance, citing the housing boom and upswing in nonresidential construction. ("=WRAP: Australia's Boral Boosts Pft Guidance, Shares Gain" ran at 0732 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Hynix Semiconductor is likely to post a much narrower loss for the third quarter ended September, helped by higher chip prices and cost savings. But some don't rule out the possibility of an operating profit in the third quarter for the company, its first such profit in six quarters. Results due out Wednesday. ("=Hynix 3Q Loss Likely To Narrow; Chip Prices, Sales Rise" ran at 0532 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Chinese independent power producer Huaneng Power will report third-quarter earnings later today. (earnings expected after 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Mainland Chinese steel company Maanshan Iron & Steel said Tuesday its nine-month net profit rose more than five times to 1.69 billion yuan, buoyed by stronger steel prices this year. ("China's Maanshan Iron 9-Mo Net Surges On Steel Price Rise" ran at 0054 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: U.S.-based Cadence Design Systems' president and chief executive Ray Bingham expects "big returns" from the electronic design company's latest investment in China, but financial rewards aren't uppermost in his mind - for now. (INTERVIEW will run around 0945 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Rents have bottomed out for properties owned by Ascendas Real Estate Investment Trust, but depending on growth in Singapore's economy, it will take at least another year before they begin to recover, the manager of the Singapore industrial property trust said. ("=INTERVIEW: Singapore A-REIT Rents Have Bottomed Out - CEO" ran at 0646 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: CapitalMall Trust, one of three Singapore Real Estate Investment Trust, or REITs, will report third-quarter results. (earnings expected around 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: Philippine semiconductor and electronics companies run the risk of missing out on the projected surge in global electronics demand next year due to recent low investment, said an industry executive at a seminar Tuesday. ("Philippine Semiconductor Cos May Miss Demand Surge -Exec" ran at 0852 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: Minority shareholders in takeover target Richmond, New Zealand's biggest meat exporter, will appeal a court ruling giving suitor PPCS Ltd., a meat cooperative, majority control of the company, Richmond said Tuesday. ("NZ Bell Group To Appeal Court Judgment On Richmond, PPCS" ran at 2008 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: Indian automobile companies likely recorded strong profit growth in the fiscal second quarter ended Sept. 30 on a combination of higher sales and lower costs. Tata Motors, the country's largest auto company, reports results Oct. 29; India's No. 2 auto company Ashok Leyland releases results a day earlier. ("=Indian Truck, Car Cos' FY 2Q Net Pft Likely To Surge" ran at 0350 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: China's crude oil imports hit an all-time monthly high of 9.8 million metric tons in September, up 44% from August, fueled by high economic growth and the buildup of strategic crude oil stocks. ("=WRAP: China Crude Oil Imports Above Government Forecasts" ran at 0431 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: A fire Monday at a hydrocracker unit at South Korea's largest refinery, owned by SK Corp., will cut gasoil and kerosene supply while creating a surplus of residual fuel oil. The disruption to middle distillate supplies comes at an unfortunate time, given current rising export demand for these products from Korea. However, in the case of fuel oil, markets are oversupplied. ("WRAP: SK Corp's Hydrocracker Outage Tips Product Balance" ran at 0530 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031021dzal000f2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\sh