\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Oct 20  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 Oktober 2003\par 11:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan and the U.S. confirmed that there is no change to their basic views on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  when the leaders of the two nations met here Friday, the top bureaucrat at Japan's Finance Ministry said Monday. \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will thus continue to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets if it feels that movements are too volatile, Administrative Vice Finance Minister Masakazu Hayashi told reporters, echoing remarks made by other Japanese officials earlier Monday. ("REPEAT:Japan Hayashi: No Change To US,Japan \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Stances" ran at 0855 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Terror and security threats overshadowed pressing global economic issues at the Asia-Pacific summit on Monday as the leaders of the U.S. and South Korea went behind closed doors over a new initiative to break the divided peninsula's nuclear standoff. And, as U.S. President George W. Bush used the annual gathering to promote his war on terror, other leaders at the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum pressed ahead with the 21-member group's core theme - promoting free trade.("APEC Leaders Open Meeting With Focus On Terrorism, Trade" ran at 0047 GMT; "Some APEC Leaders Say Mtg Sidetracked By Other Issues" ran at 0454 GMT; other "APEC" stories will run through the day)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Bank of Japan Governor Toshihiko Fukui reiterated Monday that the foundations for a moderate economic recovery in Japan are being laid. A positive cycle of economic recovery will likely emerge in the future but it will take time for a self-sustaining recovery in domestic demand, he said at the opening of a BOJ branch managers' meeting Monday. Fukui also underlined the BOJ's commitment to keep its ultra-easing monetary policy for some time. ("BOJ Fukui" first take ran at 0057 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: After losing ground early Monday in Asia, the dollar rose sharply on a report that U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow expects local interest rates to rise in the next few months as the economy recovers. ("=REPEAT&CORRECT: \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: Dlr Rebounds On Snow Comments" ran at 0452 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: The U.S. Federal Reserve will likely tighten monetary policy sometime in 2004 as the U.S. economic recovery gathers pace, the deputy director of the International Monetary Fund's research department said Monday. But the Fed can keep policy steady for the time being as inflation is low, the U.S. labor market is still weak, and there is a lot of excess capacity, David Robinson said Robinson said at a briefing on the IMF's most recent world economic outlook. ("=WRAP: IMF Adviser Expects Fed To Tighten In 2004" ran at 0552 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: While the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market remains skeptical about the U.S. government's commitment to its strong-dollar policy, options traders are being forced to review their own weak-dollar policies. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Option Views: Vol Drop Challenges Weak-Dollar Policy" ran at 0314 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DEBT: Japan will attempt to place a record Y120 trillion of government bonds in financial markets next fiscal year starting April, requiring the Finance Ministry to plan its move carefully to avoid causing a market panic, according to analysts. ("=ASIA DEBT: JGB Selling Task To Top Y120 Tln Next FY" ran at 0650 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Asahi Mutual Life Insurance, a major Japanese life insurer, plans to buck a trend among Japanese insurers by selling about Y300 billion in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -hedged foreign bonds over the next six months. ("=INTERVIEW: Asahi Life Plans Y300 Bln Foreign Bond Sales" ran at 0752 GM)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: Industrial & Commercial Bank of China, China's largest bank, reportedly has hired Credit Suisse First Boston to advise it on using a bond sale to dispose of a portion of its bad loans, according to CSFB. (story may run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The International Monetary Fund said Monday that it expects the South Korean economy to start to recover toward the end of this year led by export growth and stabilizing domestic consumption, but made no adjustments to its recent economic forecasts. Kenneth H. Kang, IMF's new resident representative in Korea, reiterated the Fund's revised forecast of 2.5% growth for Korea's economy this year. ("IMF Official: S Korea Econ To Start Recovering End-'03" ran at 0249 GMT; and "CORRECT: IMF, S Korea To Hold Annual Mtg Nov 5-18" ran at 0628 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hong Kong's unemployment rate fell to a lower-than-expected 8.3% in July-September as the city's economy improved due to a revival in tourism and related industries. Separately, the number of personal bankruptcies declared in September was just 1,683, the Official Receiver's Office said Monday, in a sign that the economy is rebounding from its second-quarter rut. ("DATA SNAP: HK Jul-Sep Jobless Rate Eases On Tourism Surge" first take ran at 0827 GMT; bankruptcies story will run around 0930 GMT; Data View will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH:: For Malaysia, the gathering last week of Islamic leaders was much more than an occasion to show off its infrastructure and hospitality. As host of the Organization of the Islamic Conference summit, Malaysia also grabbed another opportunity to promote itself as Asia's premier Islamic banking hub. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 CENTRAL BANK\b0 \highlight\uc2  WATCH: Malaysia As Islamic Banking Center" ran at 0701 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 CANBERRA: This week is shaping up as one of the most politically lucrative in John Howard's seven-and-a-half years as Australian Prime Minister. He will play host to U.S. President George W. Bush and China's President Hu Jintao over consecutive days. Roughly a year out from an election, the political mileage for Howard appears substantial, just when the major opposition Labor Party was starting to fix the spotlight back on prickly domestic matters such as health and education. ("=Australia PM's Standing To Flourish On Bush, Hu Visits" ran at 0329 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Inflation in Australia likely remained weak in the third quarter but expectations that interest rates will rise before the end of the year are set to remain unaltered, economists said. Consumer price index data for the third quarter due out Wednesday. ("=PREVIEW: Australian 3Q CPI Likely Weak, But Rates Up" ran at 0659 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's robust housing market paused for breath in September as some home buyers and investors appeared to have heeded a warning about higher interest rates from the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Alan Bollard. ("NZ's Sept House Price" ran at 2330 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MANILA: The Philippines again blew its budget deficit target in September, overspending for the second month in a row to pump prime a struggling economy. Separately, trade data for August showed a slowdown in imports in the wake of a failed mutiny in July that undermined investor confidence even though the event blew over quickly and without any bloodshed. (WRAP will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MUMBAI: The Reserve Bank of India implemented a package of measures to staunch a rising tide of system liquidity that is threatening the stability of the bond and \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets. (story may run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: Dollar bulls saw red in Asia Monday, after a report that U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow expects higher U.S. interest rates boosted the U.S. unit more than a yen from its early session lows. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0532 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China Mobile is expected to post a third-quarter net profit of about CNY9 billion later today, up 6% from the previous quarter, likely indicating organic growth helped by post-SARS recovery despite stiff competition from China Unicom and China Telecom's Little Smart. (earnings will run after 0900 GMT; WRAP will run by around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China Resources Power Holdings is expected to raise up to HK$2.58 billion through an initial public offer of shares in Hong Kong next month, a person familiar with the deal said Monday. The company is offering 920 million shares, or 25% of its enlarged share capital, to be priced between HK$2.20 and HK$2.80 a share. The roadshow starts Monday and trading is expected to begin Nov. 12. ("China Resources Power/IPO" first take ran at 0101 GMT; WRAP will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australian & New Zealand Banking Group could unveil details of a possible NZ$6.2 billion acquisition of National Bank of New Zealand as early as Friday. Australia's fourth-largest bank is also expected to use its annual results briefing this Friday to warn of a tougher banking environment into the next year, given a likely slowing in credit growth. ("=NZ Deal May Overshadow Australia's ANZ Profit Result" ran at 0829 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sharp Corp. is considering raising output capacity for high-resolution small liquid crystal display panels at one of its plants in central Japan, in response to strong demand for such products for use in mobile phone handsets with built-in cameras and other equipment, a company spokesman said Monday. ("Sharp May Lift Small LCD Panel Output As Demand Strong" ran at 0244 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: China Development Financial Holding reportedly plans to double the capital of its wholly owned asset management unit to NT$10 billion in preparation for entering Taiwan's nonperforming loan resale market. The firm, Taiwan's fourth-largest listed financial group in terms of equity, is expected to approve the plan when it meets later today. Company has scheduled announcement around 0930 GMT. (story will run around/after 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =CHINA WATCH: For three years now, not a single new A-share company has made its debut on the main board in Shenzhen, a former Chinese fishing village turned boom town across the border from Hong Kong. But changes may be on the way. ("=CHINA WATCH: Stock Listing Revival Possible In Shenzhen" ran at 0554 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =TECH STOCK FOCUS: Investors in Japanese industrial electronic companies such as NEC Corp. have had reason to celebrate in recent months, as some of the stocks more than doubled in price in four months, exceeding the 45% jump in a bullish share market. But it might be time now to put away the party hats and champagne, because those shares don't appear to have much upside left. ("=Tech Stock Focus: Party Over For Japan Indus Elec Shares" ran at 0217 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: A Cisco Systems Inc. technology support outsourcing deal with Beijing-based Information Technology United Corp., or ITUC,, may spell the beginning of the end of India's dominance in global outsourcing market, an ITUC executive said Monday. (INTERVIEW will run around 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: Private equity interest in China acquisitions and state bank nonperforming loans will help drive the expansion of O'Melveny & Myers LLP law offices in China, OMM chairman Arthur B. Culvahouse Jr said. (INTERVIEW will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIAN STOCK FOCUS: Usually low-profile northeast-concept stocks have chalked up gains in recent weeks, as China's leaders turn their attention to revitalizing the rust-belt provinces in the northeast. ("=Asian Stock Focus:China's Northeast Stks Enjoy Attention" ran at 0324 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA DRAM REPORT: Spot prices of computer memory chips rebounded strongly Monday after a two-month decline, encouraged by demand in China and a strong fourth-quarter outlook from the world's two largest chipmakers, Intel and Samsung Electronics. (=Asia DRAM Report will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korea's Samsung Heavy Industries said Monday it has received orders to build nine liquefied natural gas carriers for a total of $1.38 billion from BG Group PLC of the U.K. and Qatar's Rasgas. ("S Korea Samsung Heavy/Orders" ran at 2321 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Citing a rising Australian dollar and higher debt payments, Australian nonferrous metals and plumbing supplies exporter Crane Group Monday said annual earnings could fall as much as 10% compared with its earlier forecast of a flat result. ("=WRAP: Australia's Crane Tumbles After Profit Warning" ran at 0645 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thai Kasikornbank, Thailand's third-largest lender, third-quarter net profit rose slightly to THB1.93 billion from THB1.88 billion a year ago. Its nonperforming loans at the end of September stood at 15.65% of total lending compared with 17.23% at end-June. ("Thai Kasikornbank/Earnings" first take ran at 0134 GMT; "Thai Kasikornbank/NPL" ran at 0132 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: MobileOne will likely post steady third-quarter net profit of around S$34.8 million versus $34.7 million last year. Analysts say steady earnings would still be commendable for Singapore's No. 2 mobile operator, given growing pressure in the domestic mobile phone market and its market share slipping to 31% from 34%. (earnings story will run around 0900 GMT; WRAP, to be filed on merit, will run around 1100 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Hotelier Shangri-La Asia has secured a management contract that will give the group its first presence in India, and it will aim to seal additional similar deals in the country by the end of the year, the company's top executives said. ("=INTERVIEW: HK Shangri-La Sees India Deal As 1st Of Many" ran at 0756 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Rents at Ascendas Real Estate Investment Trust Ltd.'s properties may have bottomed but they won't pick up for "at least 12 months," a top executive managing the Singapore industrial property trust said. (INTERVIEW will run around 1030 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Shandong Xinhua Pharmaceutical said Monday its net profit fell 13% on year in the nine months ended September 49.6 million yuan as prices of its products fell and raw material costs rose. ("China Shandong Xinhua Pharm 9-Mos Net CNY49.6M, Down 13%" ran at 0306 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SHANGHAI: China Construction Bank posted a pretax profit of 8.79 billion yuan for the first three quarters of this year, which was an increase of CNY1.44 billion from a year ago. The unlisted bank is one of China's four major state-owned commercial banks. ("China Construction Bk" first take ran at 2339 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Chinese mobile carrier China Unicom said that at the end of September, it had 13.34 million subscribers to its Code Division Multiple Access, or CDMA, network service compared with 12.05 million CDMA users at the end of August. ("China Unicom/Sep" first take ran at 0006 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's big consumer electronics makers mostly look set to report stronger profits for the fiscal first half, as they continued to benefit from solid demand for digital gadgets. ("=JAPAN SECTOR OUTLOOK:Consumer Elec Cos 1H Pfts May Shine" ran at 0620 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 PERTH: Epic Energy's US$1.8 billion sale of its Australian pipeline assets has been delayed by a month as the U.S.-controlled group tries to satisfy the confidentiality concerns of more than a dozen possible bidders. ("=Epic Energy's Australian Pipeline Assets Sale Delayed" ran at 0500 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Four key shipping executives from Swiss-registered trading company Glencore resigned on Friday, leaving market players speculating where the quartet may be heading, shipping sources said. ("=Glencore's Tanker Shipping Quartet Jump Ship - Sources" ran at 0824 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031020dzak000dh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
