\par\par \b \uc2 WEEK AHEAD-Tokyo stocks seen marking time ahead of earnings.   \b0\par\par\uc2 By Risa Maeda   \par \uc2 840 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2003\par 04:25\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 19 (Reuters) - Japanese stocks are expected to consolidate this week as investors take profits on shares of brokers, banks, retailers and other high-flyers that have helped lift the Tokyo market to 16-month peaks.   \par \par \uc2 Companies reporting strong earnings and outlooks could attract buyers, however, as big investors seek to park large pools of funds that have pushed turnover above one trillion yen ($9.10 billion) in every session since September 30.   \par \par \uc2 While Monday kicks off the busiest week of the U.S. earnings season, Japan Inc's is just beginning.   \par \par \uc2 Kao Corp (4452.T), Japan's top household products maker, and industrial electronics giant NEC Corp (6701.T) will be among the first blue chip firms to issue July-September results this week.   \par \par \uc2 "As for the indices, their topside will likely be limited, mirroring Wall Street's recent hesitance to rise further," said Toshihiko Matsuno, senior strategist at SMBC Friend Securities.   \par \par \uc2 "But for individual firm's earnings, it will be a different story," he said. "Traders and investors will jump on the bandwagon for anything exciting."   \par \par \uc2 After rising 2.33 percent last week, the Nikkei average is expected to range between 10,500 and 11,300. Friday's close of 11,037.89 was the highest since June 13, 2002.   \par \par \uc2 Investors in Japan were encouraged by gains on Wall Street early in the week as strong earnings fanned hopes for a global economic recovery, but profit-taking set in on Friday.    \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par The Dow Jones Industrial Average  ended down 0.71 percent on Friday\par while the tech-laden Nasdaq Composite         dropped 1.94 percent.\par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 STRONG EARNINGS   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese firms are expected to report strong results for the quarter, so much of the focus will be on their forecasts for October-March.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "A lot of positive surprises in coming weeks would make it easier for the Nikkei to test last year's high of 12,000," said Kenichi Hirano, strategist at Tachibana Securities.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "But that looks pretty unlikely given that companies have been eking out profits mainly by restructuring."   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Investors will be looking at how the market reacts to the expected contrast in results of electronics giants NEC and Sony Corp (6758.T), both due to report on Thursday.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEC, Japan's largest maker of mobile phones, said in July it expected a quarterly net profit of 11.3 billion yen, against a loss of 6.2 billion yen a year earlier, due to strong handset sales and good results from its chip unit.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts expect NEC to raise its October-March forecasts from a net profit of 18 billion yen on sales of 2.6 trillion yen.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sony, the world's largest consumer electronics maker, is expected to report a big decline in operating profit. The average of forecasts from five analysts is for an operating profit of 33.3 billion yen, down 34.1 percent from a year earlier.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Weak sales at Sony's mainstay electronics division are expected to dent earnings, and analysts say results from its music and movie division have been disappointing.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another concern for the market is the strong yen, a negative for companies like Sony that are heavy exporters.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar, now trading just above 109 yen, has fallen by about 10 percent against the Japanese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  over the past three months despite heavy \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 On Friday, the dollar retreated after visiting U.S. President George W. Bush pressed Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi to let the market determine the value of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bush is expected to make his case again in coming days at the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation forum in Bangkok.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. manufacturers say an overly weak yen and Chinese yuan are hurting their exports and killing jobs.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "While \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  dealers are talking about the possibility of the dollar's fall to 105 yen, we hope to see it stabilise at and above 109-110 yen," said Koji Muneoka, head of domestic trading at HSBC Securities.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A strong yen reduces the competitiveness of Japanese products on global markets and cuts into the value of earnings from abroad when converted to yen.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 DIARY   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 MONDAY, OCT 20   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Annual summit meeting of Asian-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) forum begins in Bangkok.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Bank of Japan's quarterly branch managers' meeting.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - First day of two-day Tokyo global auto conference.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Firms to announce earnings include Hoya Corp (7741.T).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TUESDAY, OCT 21   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Second day of two-day Tokyo global auto conference.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Firms to announce earnings include Yahoo Japan Corp (4689.Q).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 WEDNESDAY, OCT 22   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Firms to announce earnings include Kao, NEC Electronics Corp (6723.T) and Matsui Securities (8628.T).   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 THURSDAY, OCT 23   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to release September trade data.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Government to release August all-industries, tertiary sector indices.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Firms to announce earnings include Sony and NEC.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 FRIDAY, OCT 24   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 - Firms to announce earnings include Toshiba Corp (6502.T), Nikko Cordial Corp (8603.T) and Mitsubishi Securities Co Ltd (8615.T). ($1=109.84 yen) (Additional reporting by Daisuke Wakabayashi).    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031019dzaj000xd\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
