\par\par \b \uc2 Bush set to pressure China over  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2  policy.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Entous  \par \uc2 538 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 Oktober 2003\par 00:05\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK, Oct 19 (Reuters) - Under pressure from U.S. manufacturers who say China is costing them business and jobs, President George W. Bush will make a personal appeal to Chinese President Hu Jintao on Sunday to begin letting the market determine the value of the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 But just as in Japan earlier in the week, Bush was unlikely to win any firm commitments that China would let the value of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  rise, a change which would help U.S. exporters compete in markets flooded with cheap Chinese goods.  \par \par \uc2 North Korea's nuclear weapons programme will also top the agenda for the hour-long meeting with Hu during Bush's visit to Thailand, where the leaders are attending a summit of the Asia Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) forum.  \par \par \uc2 "The president attaches a tremendous amount of importance to the role that China can play in seeking a peaceful resolution to the North Korean nuclear issue," a senior administration official said.  \par \par \uc2 Before meeting with Hu, Bush will meet the Thai prime minister, a close Asian ally in the U.S. war on terrorism.  \par \par \uc2 Washington is expected to reward Bangkok's support by speeding up trade talks and designating Thailand a major non-NATO ally, making it easier for it to buy U.S. military equipment.  \par \par \uc2 Bush has come under mounting pressure from members of Congress and from U.S. manufacturers - a critical base of support in the run-up to next year's presidential election - to get China and Japan to stop trying to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 In China's case, the administration wants Beijing to loosen its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, which pegs the Chinese yuan to the dollar. U.S. officials argue that the fixed peg renders Chinese exports cheaper, making it hard for American companies to compete.  \par \par \uc2 But some of Bush's economic advisers are wary of pushing Beijing too hard, warning that a sudden upward revaluation of the yuan could trigger a banking crisis.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely but its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Bush has made clear his preference for less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities to weaken the yen against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 But he appeared to make little progress on the issue in talks on Friday with Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, who made no commitments during their talks.  \par \par \uc2 Instead, Koizumi stressed that steps were needed to counter exchange rate volatility, U.S. and Japanese officials said.  \par \par \uc2 So far China has shown little willingness to bend to U.S. pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , though Bush appears undeterred.  \par \par \uc2 "The president is making it clear to the Chinese that we need progress on this, that we've got to see the Chinese moving towards a free-floating exchange rate," a senior Bush administration official told reporters in Manila on the eve of the talks.  \par \par \uc2 But he acknowledged: "Are we going to get it tomorrow? Probably not."  \par \par \uc2 The official played down the long-term impact of the rift, noting: "China itself says that, ultimately, it wants to move towards a free floating exchange rate. They say that is the goal.  \par \par \uc2 "So now I guess we're just quibbling over the time frame," he said.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031018dzai0089i\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
