\par\par \b \uc2 WRAP: Sakakibara: US "Playing Dangerous Game" With Dlr  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Ron Harui  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 705 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2003\par 12:58\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The Bush administration is "playing a dangerous game" with the its strong-dollar policy, as it seeks to accommodate domestic political pressure in the run-up to the U.S. presidential elections next year, a former Japanese Finance Ministry official said Friday.  \par \par \uc2 The U.S. continues to publicly adhere to its long-standing strong-dollar policy, but its push last month for Group of Seven countries to endorse greater flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates has encouraged a weaker dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "Their position is contradictory. It's a major problem," said Eisuke Sakakibara, a former vice finance minister for international affairs.  \par \par \uc2 President George W. Bush is embracing the cause of U.S. manufacturers located in Democratic strongholds such as Ohio, Michigan and California in order to win the presidential elections next November, Sakakibara told Dow Jones Newswires in an interview.  \par \par \uc2 "It's elections. He has to, at least, give some lip-service to those constituencies," he said. "But, at the same time, if the U.S. officially shifts their policy away from the strong dollar, there's a real danger of a collapse of the dollar, so this is a dangerous game."  \par \par \uc2 To prevent any hard-landing of the dollar in the future, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  should \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, either alone or together with other Group of Seven nations, Sakakibara said.  \par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which has responsibility for Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, "should (fight dollar weakness), particularly the overshooting," he said. "They should certainly stop an overshooting below Y100, which could hurt the Japanese economy."  \par \par \uc2 Sakakibara - known as "Mr. Yen" for his influence over exchange rates when he was Japan's point man on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  - said that Japan's economy could be damaged significantly if the dollar plunged to Y90 or Y80, though he added the economy can weather a dollar slide as far as the low-Y100s.  \par \par \uc2 In the near-term, the dollar will be rangebound "between Y107 and Y112 for the next two weeks," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "Then slowly the dollar will weaken to Y105 or even lower by year end," Sakakibara added.  \par \par \uc2 At 1056 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y109.66.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par      MOF Tolerates Some Yen Rise \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Despite sentiment among some market players that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's Finance Ministry has shifted its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tactics to 'smoothing' \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operations\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  from defending a 'line in the sand,' Sakakibara brushed aside those notions, saying the ministry hasn't changed its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Still, recent \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  "is sort of defensive," he said. "Whether it's smoothing or defending the line is very difficult to distinguish."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sakakibara's comments come as Bush meets Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi in Tokyo this evening. Bush has said he'll discuss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  with Koizumi. Neither of the two leaders plan to hold a press conference after their meeting but U.S. and Japanese government officials will brief reporters on the talks at 1300 GMT.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sakakibara, who is currently director of Keio University's Global Security Research Center, said the yen's rise since early August was caused by a change in the market's perception of Japan's economy.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Until July, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market participants were uncertain about the economy's recovery. But since then markets have grown confident that growth will pick up, prompting foreign inflows into Japanese assets and pushing up the yen, he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar slid to a fresh three-year low of Y108.28 a week ago.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The Japanese recovery is just starting, so it's not a bad time to buy Japanese assets at this moment," he said.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the finance ministry recognizes that some yen appreciation is inevitable if the nation's economy and stock markets strengthen, it will likely continue its yen-selling \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curb any future excessive \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rate movements, Sakakibara added.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Underscoring its resolve to cap the yen's rise this year, the Bank of Japan, acting on behalf of the ministry, sold an unprecedented Y13.483 trillion in the first nine months, and is suspected to have sold several hundred billion yen more in October.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Ron Harui, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2942; ron.harui@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031017dzah000gr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
