\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW: DKW Strategist Says Bet On Yen Over Euro  \b0\par\par\uc2 583 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2003\par 12:19\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The yen is likely to rise against the euro in the medium term, which should please European officials concerned about Japan's recent efforts to keep its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weak, says Jesper Dannesboe, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Dresdner Kleinwort Wasserstein.  \par \par \uc2 "I'm telling the speculative clients and fund managers that it's still safe to be overweight yen, because it hasn't really moved much yet, and it's highly unlikely that it will fall back," the London-based strategist told Dow Jones Newswires earlier this week.  \par \par \uc2 "The odds are so heavily in favor of the yen appreciating," Dannesboe said on a trip here to visit Tokyo clients. "Euro/yen has made a trend reversal, and is going to trend lower."  \par \par \uc2 Dresdner's long-term forecast is for the euro to fall to Y124 in 12 months and Y122 in 15 months. At 1010 GMT Friday, the euro was trading at Y127.39.  \par \par \uc2 What clients find most interesting, said Dannesboe, is the current "political game" going on in terms of \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  spent more than Y13 trillion \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets in the first nine months of the year, of which more than Y4 trillion was spent in September alone. But the yen has risen about 7% against the dollar since mid-September, as the Finance Ministry appears to have refrained from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  too heavily after the Sept. 20 meeting of top Group of Seven monetary officials in Dubai.  \par \par \uc2 "The new story is that Europeans have finally started to talk tough on the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," Dannesboe said. "We got these comments out from the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 , where they said it's not fair that the euro was running up against everything."  \par \par \uc2 European policymakers have long desired to see the euro rise against the dollar, thus easing inflationary pressure. But while a strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  can help keep prices stable, it could also damage Europe's export-led growth by making the region's goods less competitive in overseas markets - a scenario Europe can ill afford.  \par \par \uc2 "In Europe, it's very, very difficult to find anything positive to say in terms of future growth," said Dannesboe. "Clearly there's been a change in Europe. Euro/yen can't rally anymore. This is the new story, really."  \par \par \uc2 The euro's valuation is still high relative to that of the yen, he said. Japan's unit labor costs are growing at a slower pace than Europe's, meaning the Japanese export sector has become more competitive in relation to that of Europe in local \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  terms, he said.  \par \par \uc2 "If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  had not \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , you could be pretty sure that euro/yen would be much lower, and this is the point Europeans are making," he said.  \par \par \uc2 The yen, said Dannesboe, is one of the easiest \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to predict, because it follows the Japanese business cycle.  \par \par \uc2 International fund managers tend to be underweight Japanese assets, he said, as "they've been burned so many times, because every time they got really bullish the recovery didn't last for long."  \par \par \uc2 Six months ago, when Dannesboe last called on clients around the world, he noticed that "no one really wanted to talk about Japan."  \par \par \uc2 But now, by contrast, "when you get an upturn, everyone wants to get back to the benchmarks," which is positive for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , he said.  \par \par \uc2 -By Lisa Twaronite, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; lisa.twaronite@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Kirsty Mackenzie  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031017dzah000fh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
