\par\par \b \uc2 Dow Jones Asia-Pacific Top Stories Sked For Oct 17  \b0\par\par\uc2 2,031 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2003\par 11:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 Following are the top Asia-Pacific stories featured on Dow Jones Newswires as of 0900 GMT, along with items expected later in the day. Please direct any questions or comments to asianewseds@dowjones.com or by phone on 65-6415-4020.  \par \par \uc2 MACRO/\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /FIXED INCOME  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: U.S. President George W. Bush kicks off his Asia tour Friday in Japan, and all eyes are on what if anything he says about U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy and \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's aggressive recent \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  campaign. (briefings on Bush/Koizumi talks to start around 1300 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BEIJING: China's economy bounced back stronger than expected in the third quarter, recovering well from a SARS-induced slowdown in growth in the previous three months, the government said Friday. ("DATA SNAP: China's January-September GDP CNY7.911 Tln" ran at 0210 GMT; Data View will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Eisuke Sakakibara, the former senior Finance Ministry official who earned the nickname "Mr. Yen" due to his expert manipulation of the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, talks to Dow Jones about \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  and the Japanese economy just as U.S. President Bush is in town in Tokyo.(INTERVIEW will run after 0900 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =JAPAN CREDIT: Fixed interest-rate exposure in Japan's swap market will produce better returns than government bonds in coming weeks as domestic banks rebuild swap portfolios in the second fiscal half. ("=JAPAN CREDIT:Swaps To Beat JGBs As Banks Boost Positions" ran at 0802 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  ASIA: Although U.S. calls for more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  are focused mainly on China and Japan, South Korea has been the most blatant manipulator of its exchange rate in recent weeks. Seoul could be the next target of Washington's efforts to discourage Asian nations from weakening their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to spur exports - policies that widen the U.S. current-account deficit with the rest of the world and could damp the pace of growth in the U.S. economy. ("=\b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Asia: US Could Shift Its Focus To Korean \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Policy" ran at 0408 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE: Singapore's economy is again growing, riding a global cyclical rebound, but structural changes in the domestic economy and the emergence of China as a powerful competitor will stump growth rates, the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  said Friday. Showing signs of the rebound from the ravages of the SARS outbreak and the Iraq war, non-oil domestic exports rose an above-expected 25.9% on year to S$10.5 billion, the fastest pace of growth since August 2000, data show Friday. However, the recovery underway has "yet to broaden and become self-sustaining," the \b \highlight22\uc2 Monetary Authority\b0 \highlight\uc2  of Singapore said in its semiannual macroeconomic review Friday. ("=WRAP: Strong Singapore Exports Point To Modest Recovery" ran at 0814 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean Finance Minister Kim Jin-Pyo expects economic implications of President Roh Moo-Hyun's call for a referendum to gauge the public's trust in his administration to be minimal. ("S Korea Fin Min -2: Possible To Minimize Effect On Econ" first take ran at 2307 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: South Korean capital investment is weak due to a delay in the global economic recovery, lackluster domestic consumption and a slump in the local informaton and technology sector, according to a statement by the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  and Economy on Friday. ("S Korea Fin Ministry:Capital Invest Weak Due To IT Sector" first take ran at 0110 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thailand's Finance Ministry is still considering taxing speculative \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  inflows to help curb upward pressure on the baht. ("Thai Fin Min/Tax" first take ran at 0350 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Thailand's finance ministry will exempt new baht-denominated government bonds from a 15% withholding tax, initiating the country's participation in the Asian Bond Fund II, a senior finance ministry official said Friday. ("Thai MOF/Bonds/Tax" first take ran at 0331 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Market participants welcomed the Asian Bond Fund II as a step towards developing the regional bond market but indicated that it's too early for the initiative to have a major impact in the market. (story will run after 0930 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Japan's largest life insurer, Nippon Life Insurance plans to buy Y200 billion to Y300 billion of foreign bonds hedged against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk over the next six months. ("=INTERVIEW:Nippon Life Eyes Y200B-Y300B Foreign Bond Buys" ran at 0433 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: The South Korean banking industry is likely to recover next year as provisioning levels fall but economic risks such as the weakening ability of households and companies to repay debt will pose a threat, said an analyst at Standard & Poor's Ratings Service. ("=INTERVIEW:Economic Risks To Limit S Korean Bks' Recovery" ran at 0645 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =INDIA DEBT WATCH: An unprecedented rally in Indian government bond prices has many convinced that the market is overpriced. So the outlook is for - more buying. ("=India Debt Watch: Overpriced Bonds May Not Retreat Yet" ran at 0819 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's competition watchdog, the Commerce Commission, has signed a cooperation memorandum with its counterparts in Australia and the U.K. aimed at combating cross-border breaches of competition and consumer law and is negotiating a similar agreement with the U.S. ("NZ, Australia, UK Regulators Sign Cooperation Memorandum" ran at 2125 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: New Zealand's housing market could trigger interest rate rises before the end of 2004, Deutsche Bank said Friday in a quarterly outlook for the country's economy. ("NZ Housing Market Could Trigger \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Action - DB" ran at 1200 GMT Thursday)  \par \par \uc2 =\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2  MARKET ROUNDUP: The dollar ticked slightly higher against the yen in Asia Friday in a choppy but rangebound session ahead of President George W. Bush's meeting here with Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi. ("Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2 " ran at 0546 GMT; Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 Currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  comment usually runs by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 EQUITIES  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: In a sign that the global technology turnaround is gaining momentum, Samsung Electronics, the world's largest memory-chip maker, said third-quarter net income rose nearly 7% to 1.84 trillion won, boosted by a surge in sales of mobile-phone handsets and increasing demand for its mainstay semiconductors. ("=WRAP: Samsung Elec 3Q Net Boosted By Handsets, Semicon" ran at 0434 GMT; second WRAP will run by around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Sony Corp. and South Korea's Samsung Electronics are close to a deal to form a joint venture to make liquid crystal display panels for flat-screen televisions, a move showing Sony's desire to catch up in the rapidly growing flat TV market. ("=WRAP: Sony, Samsung Near Deal On JV For Flat TV-Use LCDs" ran at 0904 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SYDNEY: Qantas Airways acknowledged Friday that a restructuring now underway will make it easier for the Australian carrier to break off parts of its business and list them on the stock market. Although that doesn't seem to be the plan for the time being, Chief Executive Geoff Dixon conceded that the restructure does make the "floating" of different units a possibility. ("=WRAP: Qantas Raises Possibility Of Listing Business Ops" ran at 0853 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Struggling Japanese supermarket store operator Daiei Friday reported dismal earnings for the first half ended Aug. 31, citing unusually cool summer weather and deflationary pressure. Daiei's group operating revenue fell 15% on year to Y992.08 billion, while group net profit tumbled 98% to Y2.30 billion - year-earlier profit was inflated by a Y170 billion special profit due to a debt waiver from its main lenders. ("Daiei" first take ran at 0455 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGALORE, India: Wipro Ltd., one of India's largest software services firms, reported Friday a slightly higher-than-expected second-quarter net profit on growing business volume and easing pricing pressures. Its net profit rose 4% on year to 2.30 billion rupees in the quarter ended Sept. 30. ("=WRAP:India's Wipro 2Q Net Pft Up 4%, Strong Outlook" first take ran at 0713 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: Mutual funds on average rose in all markets in the third quarter of 2003, with funds investing in Thai shares proving the best performers globally, according to data provider Lipper Inc. In its survey of the latest quarter's results, Lipper said that funds investing in Thailand gained 34.7% on average during the three months ended Sept. 30. Funds that invest in shares in India and Japan also ranked among the top performers, posting average returns of 27.3% and 22.2%, respectively. ("=Funds Invested In Asia +17% Avg; Thai Funds Lead -Lipper" ran at 0323 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FUND VIEW: Global fund managers are pouring more money into Asian equities while selling Latin American stocks, according to reports from several U.S.-based market research firms. ("=Asia Fund View: Asia Drawing Invest From Latin America" ran at 0838 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 =ASIA FUND VIEW: Sri Lankan shares, trading at fresh highs and up around 70% so far this year, have room for further upside, backed by strong earnings potential, said Gehan Rajapakse, general manager of Eagle NDB Fund Management Co. (=Asia Fund View will run around 1000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Krung Thai Bank said Friday its net profit for the third quarter ended Sept. 30 rose sharply to 4.41 billion baht from THB2.68 billion a year ago after a revaluation of its assets. ("Krung Thai Bk/Earnings" first take ran at 0154 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI: Flat-panel maker HannStar Display has raised the maximum size of its planned Global Depositary Receipt issue by one-third to the equivalent of 800 million common shares, subject to shareholders' approval, the company said. ("Taiwan's HannStar Raises Planned GDR Size To 800M Shrs" ran at 0434 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 MELBOURNE: Australian telecommunications company Telstra said Friday the performance of its BigPond Internet service is improving as it braces for rising costs associated with a worm virus that has crippled customer e-mail delivery for the last two weeks. ("Australia Telstra: E-mail Problem Abates, Weighs Up Costs" ran at 0122 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 SEOUL: Korean Air will sell 9.6 million shares of Hanjin Shipping starting Friday to help the latter spin off from Hanjin Group. ("Korean Air To Sell 9.6M Hanjin Shipping Shrs From Friday" ran at 0244 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 HONG KONG: China Minmetals, China's largest importer and exporter of metals and steel products, said Friday it will acquiring controlling stakes in two Hong Kong companies for a total of HK$576.4 million. The move, China Minmetals said, will strengthen its international market presence and further develop its business. ("China Minmetals To Buy Majority Stakes In 2 HK-Listed Cos" first take ran at 0444 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 WELLINGTON: The independent directors of takeover target Richmond, New Zealand's biggest meat exporter, said Friday they won't appeal against a court ruling giving suitor PPCS Ltd. control of the company. ("NZ's Richmond Decides Not To Appeal Against Court Ruling" ran at 0000 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 NEW DELHI: India's telecom companies will likely report mixed results in the fiscal second quarter with significant profit growth for Bharti Tele-Ventures and shrinking income for Videsh Sanchar Nigam. India's telecom operators are scheduled to announce second-quarter results by the end of the month, starting with Bharti on Oct. 23. ("=Mixed 2Q Performance Likely For India Telecom Cos" ran at 0554 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 ENERGY/COMMODITIES  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO: Electricity generated by Japan's 10 major power utilities in the April to September period fell 1.5% on year to 454.07 billion kilowatt-hours, as low temperatures during this summer slashing demand for air conditioning, an industry body said Friday. ("Japan Apr-Sep Pwr Output Falls 1.5% On Yr On Cool Summer" ran at 0234 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 BANGKOK: Unocal Thailand, a local unit of U.S. energy giant Unocal Corp., said Friday it and its partners plan to invest around US$10 billion over the next 20 years in the Erawan and Unocal 2 and 3 gas fields offshore Thailand. The planned investment will boost natural gas volume to be sold to Thailand's oil and gas conglomerate PTT PCL. ("Unocal Plans To Invest US$10 Bln In Thai Gas Fields" first take ran at 0823 GMT)  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031017dzah000cr\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
