\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar static, ponders Bush visit to Asia, US data.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Natsuko Waki  \par \uc2 692 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2003\par 11:48\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 17 (Reuters) - The dollar held on Friday near 1-1/2 week highs against the euro hit during the previous session on robust U.S. data, with markets waiting to see what President George W. Bush's Tokyo visit would bring.  \par \par \uc2 Bush arrived in Tokyo earlier at the start of a six-day sweep through Asia. Bush is set for talks with Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi which are expected to put pressure on \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to hold back from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to curb yen strength.  \par \par \uc2 U.S. officials are expected to brief reporters at around 1300 GMT following the meeting.  \par \par \uc2 This may overshadow the October University of Michigan consumer sentiment survey, due at 1345 GMT. But if the expected rise in the index to 88.0 from 87.7 a month ago materialises, it could boost hopes for a sustained recovery in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 "\b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  will give a plenty of sound bites in the next four to five days. The pace of Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ' appreciation against the dollar is getting somewhat unsustainable so we have to see whether Bush's visit will lead to a further rise," said Jim McCormick, head of \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  research at Lehman Brothers.  \par \par \uc2 "Euro/dollar has always been a proxy for a U.S. growth story. The dollar's link with growth is breaking down because of (Washington's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2 ) policy and external financing issues, but as long as we are in a zone of U.S. growth the euro will have hard time getting new highs."  \par \par \uc2 At 0930 GMT, the dollar was flat on the day at $1.1593 , having risen closer to Thursday's 1-1/2 week high of $1.1563. It was a touch softer at 109.70 yen but kept its distance from last week's three-year lows at 108.23.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rallied on Thursday following a strong showing in a manufacturing index in the Philadelphia Fed's business survey for September.  \par \par \uc2 BOOST FOR KOIZUMI?  \par \par \uc2 Earlier this week, Japan said it will provide about $1.5 billion in grants over the near term to help rebuild Iraq.  \par \par \uc2 "We don't believe the Japanese aid for Iraq is a coincidence. Japan will do their bit to help the U.S. and the U.S. will do their bit to help Japan, as friends do," said Steven Pearson, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Halifax Bank of Scotland Treasury Services.  \par \par \uc2 "Bush and Koizumi are likely to hit on a mutually acceptable formula for the yen and will continue to encourage China to move towards a more flexible exchange rate."  \par \par \uc2 Others also said Bush could give \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  some leeway on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and provide a boost to Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi, who faces a general election in November.  \par \par \uc2 "If he says he understands Japan's concerns over the yen's rise, the dollar will be bought back," said Takashi Toyahara, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Despite heavy Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  this year, the dollar has dropped around 10 percent in the past three months. The fall accelerated after the Group of Seven industrial nations called for more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies in September.  \par \par \uc2 Reflecting recent strong demand for the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Friday that foreign investors poured a record 809.4 billion yen ($7.37 billion) of funds into Japanese stocks and bonds in the week that ended last Friday.  \par \par \uc2 OPTIMISTIC DATA  \par \par \uc2 Friday's University of Michigan report comes at the end of a fairly data-positive week for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In addition to the strong Philly Fed survey, Thursday's labour market data showed progress in the weakest part of the U.S. recovery story.  \par \par \uc2 The weekly U.S. jobs report showed first-time jobless claims declining to 384,000, the lowest since February.  \par \par \uc2 Also, U.S. consumer prices rose 0.3 percent in September, the same as in August, indicating tame inflation. Industrial production for September rose 0.4 percent, with capacity utilisation at 74.7 percent.  \par \par \uc2 However, business inventories fell 0.4 percent in August, after a 0.2 percent decline a month earlier.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031017dzah001le\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
