\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar hugs tight ranges as Bush lands in Tokyo.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Burton Frierson  \par \uc2 650 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2003\par 09:56\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 LONDON, Oct 17 (Reuters) - The dollar held on Friday near 1-1/2 week highs against the euro hit during the previous session on robust U.S. data, as markets waited to see what President George W. Bush's Tokyo visit would bring.  \par \par \uc2 The greenback also kept to tight ranges against the yen after Bush arrived in Tokyo, where he is expected to put pressure on officials to hold back from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  aggressively to dampen the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.  \par \par \uc2 The Bush visit may overshadow the October University of Michigan consumer sentiment survey, due at 1345 GMT. But if the expected rise in the index to 88.0 from 87.7 a month ago materialises, it could boost hopes for sustained recovery in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 Some are betting Bush, who sent the dollar tumbling earlier this week after he said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  values should be set by markets, will tone down his comments in face-to-face meetings, but markets are likely to remain on edge during his Asia trip.  \par \par \uc2 "If there is a shock it would be that he is very strident in his views on the dollar, but we had the comments earlier in the week that he is going to tell the Japanese he doesn't agree with \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Lee Ferridge, head of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategy at Rabobank.  \par \par \uc2 "The Michigan report should be strong today...generally the data this week has been positive."  \par \par \uc2 At 0735 GMT, the dollar was flat on the day at $1.1593, with 1-1/2 week highs set at $1.1563 on Thursday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the dollar was a touch lower at 109.73, but keeping its distance from last week's three-year lows at 108.23.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar rallied on Thursday after the Philadelphia Fed's business survey showed its main manufacturing index soared to 28.0 in October from 14.6 percent in September, higher than median forecasts of 16.0 taken in a poll last week.  \par \par \uc2 BOOST FOR KOIZUMI?  \par \par \uc2 Some players said that Bush could surprise the market by giving \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  some leeway on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and providing a boost to Koizumi, who faces a general election in November.  \par \par \uc2 "If he says he understands Japan's concerns over the yen's rise, the dollar will be bought back," said Takashi Toyahara, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities in Tokyo.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are aimed at curbing the yen's rise versus the dollar, seen as harmful to the nation's export-led economic rebound.  \par \par \uc2 Despite heavy Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar has dropped around 10 percent in the past three months, from above 120 yen. The fall accelerated after the Group of Seven leading industrial nations called for flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates following its meeting last month.  \par \par \uc2 Reflecting recent strong demand for the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Friday that foreign investors poured a record 809.4 billion yen ($7.37 billion) of funds into Japanese stocks and bonds in the week that ended last Friday.  \par \par \uc2 OPTIMISTIC DATA  \par \par \uc2 Friday's University of Michigan report comes at the end of a fairly data-positive week for the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 In addition to the strong Philly Fed survey, Thursday's labour market data showed progress in the weakest part of the U.S. recovery story.  \par \par \uc2 The weekly U.S. jobs report showed first-time jobless claims declining to 384,000, the lowest since February.  \par \par \uc2 Also, U.S. consumer prices rose 0.3 percent in September, the same as in August and above expectations of a 0.2 percent gain, indicating tame inflation. Industrial production for September, meanwhile, matched expectations, rising 0.4 percent, with capacity utilisation at 74.7 percent.  \par \par \uc2 However, business inventories fell 0.4 percent in August, after a 0.2 percent decline a month earlier. Forecasts had called for inventories to be unchanged.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031017dzah001d8\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
