\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar sits tight ahead of Bush's visit to Tokyo.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kazunori Takada  \par \uc2 505 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 Oktober 2003\par 07:50\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Oct 17 (Reuters) - The dollar was glued to tight ranges on Friday as the market waited to hear what U.S. President George W. Bush had to say on \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he meets Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 Bush, due to arrive in the Japanese capital later in the day, sent the dollar tumbling earlier in the week when he said the value of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  should be determined by markets, which traders read as a criticism of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's repeated yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 At the same time, Bush expressed support for a strong dollar, but many in the market considered the comment as merely lip service to a discarded policy.  \par \par \uc2 Most traders expect Bush to stick to the same rhetoric in Tokyo.  \par \par \uc2 "The two elements are contradictory, since leaving it to the market would mean an approval of the dollar's steep fall (against the yen)," said Katsunori Kitakura, manager of the treasury department at Chuo Mitsui Trust and Banking.  \par \par \uc2 "If he says both, I think the market will gradually push up the yen," he said.  \par \par \uc2 BOOST FOR KOIZUMI?  \par \par \uc2 Some players said that Bush could surprise the market by giving \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  some leeway on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  and providing a boost to Koizumi, who faces a general election in November.  \par \par \uc2 "If he says he understands Japan's concerns over the yen's rise, the dollar will be bought back," said Takashi Toyahara, \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Nomura Securities.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  are aimed at curbing the yen's rise versus the dollar, seen as harmful to the nation's export-led economic rebound.  \par \par \uc2 Despite heavy Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the dollar has dropped around 10 percent in the past three months, from above 120 yen. The fall accelerated after the Group of Seven leading industrial nations called for flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates following its meeting last month.  \par \par \uc2 Reflecting recent strong demand for the yen, \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  data showed on Friday that foreign investors poured a record 809.4 billion yen ($7.37 billion) of funds into Japanese stocks and bonds in the week that ended last Friday.  \par \par \uc2 As of 0523 GMT, the dollar was steady at 109.83 yen , flat from late U.S. trade. It was briefly dragged down as a major U.S. operator sold the euro for yen.  \par \par \uc2 The euro fell more than half a yen to under 127 yen, before edging back up to 127.46 yen.  \par \par \uc2 The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was little changed versus the dollar at $1.1602.  \par \par \uc2 After stopping over in Japan, Bush will fly to Bangkok for the October 20-21 Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation (APEC) summit, where he will meet Chinese President Hu Jintao.  \par \par \uc2 Bush has said he would press Japan and China at the APEC meeting to loosen up their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies as a way to lower trade barriers.  \par \par \uc2 "If he does that, it would weigh on east Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , including the yen," said Kitakura of Chuo Mitsui. ($1=109.84 yen).   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031017dzah000vw\par }\page 