\par\par \b \uc2 New Taiwan Dollar Ends Down On Falling Japanese Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 350 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2003\par 11:03\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TAIPEI (Dow Jones)--The New Taiwan dollar finished lower against its U.S. counterpart Thursday after a similar drop in the Japanese yen, dealers said.  \par \par \uc2 The local unit typically tracks the yen because of close export competition between Taiwan and Japan.  \par \par \uc2 At 0800 GMT, the U.S. dollar ended at NT$33.950, up from NT$33.900 Wednesday.  \par \par \uc2 Trading volume totaled US$859 million, up from US$809 million in the previous session.  \par \par \uc2 The intraday range for the pair was NT$33.870-NT$34.052.  \par \par \uc2 "For some reason the Japanese yen spiked later in the afternoon, it might have been some \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the BOJ (Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 )," said a dealer at an overseas bank.  \par \par \uc2 At 0800 GMT, the U.S. dollar was trading at Y110.08, compared with its low for the day of Y109.52.  \par \par \uc2 One dealer said trade on Friday will depend on what happens to the yen in overnight trade.  \par \par \uc2 Traders also expect U.S. officials attending the Asia-Pacific Economic Cooperation meetings in Bangkok to press Asian countries to allow their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen, which would also affect the local unit.  \par \par \uc2 In the money markets, the overnight interbank rate was last quoted at 1.022%, up from 1.020% Wednesday after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold NT$50 billion in government paper to offset NT$13.5 billion of similar paper maturing, as well as to soak up liquidity.  \par \par \uc2 At the same time the 30-day bill rate was flat for the fourth day in a row at 0.886%.  \par \par \uc2 Taiwan government bonds ended broadly lower due to strength in the local bourse, which ended up 1.9% at a near 18-month high of 6035.74.  \par \par \uc2 The most active government 10-year bonds last yielded 2.765% compared with 2.735% Wednesday. One dealer said the yield is unlikely to break 2.8% any time soon, as "buying will likely emerge" near that level.  \par \par \uc2 -By Dan Nystedt, Dow Jones Newswires; (8862) 2502-2557; dan.nystedt@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Andrew Bullard  \par \par \uc2 1061 1860  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031016dzag000j3\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
