\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2  VIEW: Sterling Best Poised To Cash In On Weak Dlr  \b0\par\par\uc2 796 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2003\par 19:37\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Jamie McGeever \par    A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--Sterling surged to four-month highs against the dollar Thursday, and there doesn't seem to be much standing in the way of it building on those gains soon.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 Amid an environment of deeply negative dollar sentiment, a combination of solid economic prospects in the U.K. and the strong likelihood that interest rates there will rise sooner rather than later suggest the pound has further room to appreciate. Only the fact that sterling has gained so much ground in such a short time, which could precipitate a wave of profit-taking, looks like limiting its potential.  \par \par \uc2 "Technically, the view may be less bullish, but I think we'll edge above $1.70 in the weeks and months to come," said Jason Bonanca, global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at CSFB in New York. His three-month forecast for sterling is $1.70, but doesn't discount a rise to as high as $1.73 in the interim.  \par \par \uc2 At 1:00 pm EDT Thursday, the pound was trading at $1.6774, up a full cent on the day and easing back from the four-month high of $1.6818 hit earlier in the session.  \par \par \uc2 Its ascent has been rapid. In early September, sterling was as weak as $1.56, at the time a four-month low. Should it climb above $1.6904, it will make a fresh high for the year and should it hit $1.70. That would be the first time since October 1998 - a time of massive volatility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets - that the pound has scaled such peaks.  \par \par \uc2 Since the Group of Seven leading industrial nations last month issued its call for more "flexibility" in exchange rates - which should ideally be set by "market-based mechanisms" - dealers have turned even more bearish on the dollar. The thinking among many \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  investors is that global policy makers have given their tacit approval for a weaker dollar which they believe will help adjust the severe imbalances in the global economy. They include the yawning U.S. current account deficit and weak domestic growth and demand in Europe and Asia.  \par \par \uc2 With the market selling dollars en masse, they need to buy an alternative \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 . With the euro's prospects blighted by government deficits, and anemic growth and demand, and the yen driven by its own unique dynamics - particularly the propensity of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to stop the yen from getting too strong - sterling is fast emerging as among the most viable.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     Rate Hike In The Cards? \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Rebecca Patterson, global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at JP Morgan in New York, thinks the pound will hit $1.70 by the end of this month. "This is more a reflection of our bearish dollar view than anything else," she said, but added that the Bank of England is poised to begin the G7 interest rate tightening cycle, maybe as early as next month.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The BoE's benchmark interest rate of 3.5% may be a 48-year low, but it offers investors a higher return than the U.S. (1%), euro zone (2%) and Japan and Switzerland (both close to zero).  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Strong economic data and BoE comments reinforce this (yield) advantage," wrote Steven Englander, chief \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Barclays Capital in New York, in a research note.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 And it's about to get bigger. "At some point, reducing the present degree of monetary stimulus will be necessary in order to keep inflation on track to meet the target," BoE governor Mervyn King said Tuesday, referring to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's 2.5% inflation target set by the government. "The timing of any such decision will reflect our judgment of the outlook for inflation."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Inflation in the U.K. as measured by the retail price index excluding mortgage repayments (RPIX) is running at an annual 2.8%. Rising rates will help this target be reached, King said this week, in comments that were interpreted as a remarkably clear indication from a central banker that rates are set to rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Annual economic growth in the U.K. was recently revised upwards to 2.0%, again sharply better than the euro zone, while unemployment is at its lowest level since 1975.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But for sterling to continue its march higher, it will have to overcome "congestion" of resistance barriers around $1.69 and again around $1.70, said Stephen Cox, chief technical analyst at Dow Jones Newswires in New York.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Jamie McGeever, who covers \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets in New York, has also reported on \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for Dow Jones Newswires in Rio de Janeiro and London.)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    -Jamie McGeever; Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938 2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031016dzag00083\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
