\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Govt Bonds End Weak Day Mixed On Stocks, Yen  \b0\par\par\uc2 667 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2003\par 08:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japanese government bond prices ended steady to slightly lower Thursday after spending most of the session at weaker levels, weighed by a fall in the yen and stronger stock prices, traders said.  \par \par \uc2 Paring of dollar gains against the yen during afternoon trading helped JGB prices recover from intraday lows.  \par \par \uc2 But bond market activity overall was light as investors stayed on the sidelines ahead of tomorrow's meeting in Tokyo of Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi and U.S. President George W. Bush. The two are likely to discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent round of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop the yen appreciating against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 The price of lead December bond futures ended 0.05 lower at 137.15 while the yield on new 10-year JGBs was unchanged at 1.420%.  \par \par \uc2 "JGB investors have been paying much attention to equities and the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Susumu Kato, chief strategist at Lehman Brothers.  \par \par \uc2 The Nikkei 225 Stock Average closed 125.20 points higher at 11025.15, indicating investors continue to favor stocks on expectations that Japan's recovery will boost company earnings.  \par \par \uc2 The yen, meanwhile, lost ground. At 0610 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y109.60, up from Y109.15 in Tokyo late Wednesday. A stronger yen boosts demand for JGBs because an appreciating \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  increases deflationary pressure and forces counterparties in certain \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -linked structured debt products to buy JGBs to maintain hedging on those products.  \par \par \uc2 Lehman's Kato said most market participants don't expect the two leaders will consider any major changes in their countries' respective \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  policies. But market participants are trading cautiously just in case.  \par \par \uc2 Investors bought JGBs yesterday after the 10-year yield briefly rose above 1.5% for the first time in more than a month.  \par \par \uc2 But today "fundamentals aren't so good," said Kazuhiko Sano, chief strategist at Nikko Citigroup.  \par \par \uc2 Traders said long-end JGBs are unlikely to gain much ground until after a Y500 billion auction of 20-year JGBs next Tuesday.  \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    Prices at 0600 GMT: \par  \par    Tiffe Jun 3-Mos Euroyen Price:    99.870  Up 0.005 \par    SGX Jun 3-Mos Euroyen Price:      99.865  Dn 0.005 \par    TSE Dec 10-Yr JGB Future Price:  137.15   Dn 0.05 \par    SGX Dec 10-Yr JGB Future Price:  137.17   Up 0.02 \par    10-Yr 1.6% JGB No. 253 Yield:      1.420% Unchanged \par  \par    \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Looking ahead to the auction, Lehman's Kato expects the new 20-year bonds will have a coupon of 1.9% or 2.0%, higher than a coupon of 1.8% on 20-year bonds issued in October.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The higher coupon should attract life insurers and pension funds, which like the super-long bonds because they better match their liabilities.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I don't think there are any risks in the auction. But before the auction, dealers must reduce their positions," he said. That hedging activity will weigh on the super-long zone.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Nikko Citigroup's Sano said the smaller size of the auction also means there should be little difficulty in selling the bonds.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Ministry of Finance\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has said that from October it will increase the frequency of 20-year bond auctions to monthly from bimonthly, but reduce the individual auction amounts to Y500 billion from Y800 billion.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the swap market, activity followed the movements in JGBs, although aggressive receiving in the 10-year zone pushed that rate to a traded level as low as 1.4375% from 1.46% at midday and 1.4425% yesterday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Paying demand was evident in short- and medium-term zones with 4-year swaps traded as high as 0.575% from 0.565% yesterday.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In the super-long zone, the curve widened; the 10-20-year spread edging out by 0.75 basis points to 56 basis point.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -By Iain McDonald, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2941; iain.mcdonald@dowjones.com  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Hiroshi Miwa contributed to this report)  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 -Edited by Hugh Lawson and Kirsty Mackenzie  \f28 \par \par \uc2 1037 4153  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031016dzag0005n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
