\par\par \b \uc2 Bush vows to seek 'level playing field' in Asia trade.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Adam Entous  \par \uc2 618 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2003\par 21:15\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 SAN BERNARDINO, Calif., Oct 16 (Reuters) - President George W. Bush left for Asia on Thursday promising to increase pressure on U.S. trading partners to create a "level playing field" for American exporters, a critical base of support for Bush in the run-up to next year's election.  \par \par \uc2 Bush will prod the leaders of Japan and China to let the market determine the value of their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , and vowed to expedite bilateral free-trade negotiations with Australia, Thailand and other "friends" after World Trade Organization talks derailed last month.  \par \par \uc2 While he expressed optimism that the U.S. economy is "coming around," Bush said: "We need a level playing field when it comes for trade and a level playing field will help us create jobs here in America."  \par \par \uc2 "Free trade must be two ways," Bush added in San Bernardino, California, before departing for Japan to meet Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi. He will later travel on to Bangkok for the Oct. 20-21 annual summit of the Asia Pacific Economic Cooperation forum, where he will meet with Chinese President Hu Jintao.  \par \par \uc2 The administration wants Beijing to loosen up its \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  regime, in which the Chinese yuan is pegged to the dollar. U.S. officials argue that the fixed peg renders Chinese exports cheaper, making it hard for American companies to compete.  \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  floats freely but its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market. Bush has made clear his preference for less \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Japanese authorities to weaken the yen against the dollar, telling Asian journalists in an interview earlier this week that "markets ought to be determining respective \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."  \par \par \uc2 But, at the same time, Bush restated the U.S. policy of wanting a strong dollar - a policy many investors see as hollow given the administration's tolerance of the dollar's slide, most recently to three-year lows against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 In the interview, the full transcript of which was released by the White House on Thursday, Bush said he hoped to revive global trade negotiations. "I wouldn't condemn the WTO (Doha) round to failure yet," he said.  \par \par \uc2 "The sense I get is a lot of countries feel this was a missed opportunity" and that U.S. trade negotiators believe "there is a framework to get the process restarted and moved forward," Bush added.  \par \par \uc2 In the meantime, Bush said he would seek bilateral free-trade agreements, including one with Thailand. Asked if a deal could be signed during his state visit in Bangkok, Bush said: "I don't know. We'll see."  \par \par \uc2 Bush said he also hoped to complete a free-trade pact with Australia before the end of the year.  \par \par \uc2 Bush is under mounting pressure from U.S. manufacturers and lawmakers to confront China.  \par \par \uc2 Senate Minority Leader Tom Daschle, a South Dakota Democrat, has urged the administration to start a probe of China's actions under the World Trade Organization, and a small group of lawmakers proposed legislation to repeal China's trade privileges in the United States.  \par \par \uc2 But some of Bush's economic advisers are wary of pushing Beijing too hard, warning that a sudden revaluation of the yuan \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could trigger a banking crisis.  \par \par \uc2 The United States wants China to move to a more flexible exchange rate, but Deputy Commerce Secretary Sam Bodman said this week that concerns about the damage its fixed \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  does to U.S. manufacturers may be exaggerated.  \par \par \uc2 So far, China has rebuffed calls from U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow to allow the yuan to trade more freely, and other Bush administration officials played down prospects for a breakthrough when Bush meets with Hu.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031016dzag002sx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
