\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE 3-US Treasury's Snow-All areas need flexible  \highlight22\uc2 currency \highlight\uc2 .  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Anna Willard  \par \uc2 723 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 Oktober 2003\par 01:16\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2  NEW YORK, Oct 15 (Reuters) - U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow said on Wednesday the Bush administration's push for flexible exchange rates was not targeted at any particular country and should apply to every region of the world.   \par \par \uc2  Speaking a day after President George W. Bush said he favored a strong dollar - saying at the same time that exchange rates should be set by financial markets - Snow also tried to drive home his backing for a strong U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  Bush also said he would discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  on his trip to Asia later this week.   \par \par \uc2  Snow, asked on Bloomberg Radio if the flexible exchange rate policy applies to Europe, said: "This is a universal policy. It applies to everywhere. We think a system of market-based exchange rates makes sense for everywhere."   \par \par \uc2  The Group of Seven leading economies in September called for flexible exchange rates worldwide. The euro surged against the dollar after the statement, even though the comments were seen as a signal to \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to stop \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  and to China to float its exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2  European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  Governing Council member Nout Wellink said on Wednesday financial markets had misunderstood the statement, saying it was predominantly about east Asian \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  and not the euro.   \par \par \uc2  BACKING OFF ON CHINA?   \par \par \uc2  In a sign the Bush administration may be easing pressure on China, Snow said he agrees with Chinese officials who said Beijing needs to take "interim steps" before it frees up the yuan.   \par \par \uc2  "We recognized that they (China) can't go there tomorrow, but they agreed with the broad idea that they want to move to a flexible exchange rate and, in the interim, are taking the steps, like reducing capital controls, fixing the banking problem that would make that possible," Snow said in a transcript of an interview with CNBC.   \par \par \uc2  He said China's economy must be "ready" before a flexible exchange rate is implemented - including letting foreign nationals invest in insurance, financial services and banks and liberalizing exchange controls.   \par \par \uc2  U.S. manufacturers, a critical support base for Bush in next year's elections, complain their exports are struggling because Beijing's pegged exchange rate makes Chinese goods cheaper on international markets.   \par \par \uc2  U.S. officials have put pressure on China to float the yuan, and leading manufacturing groups have congratulated the government for this stance.   \par \par \uc2  But Snow's acknowledgment that China may need time, coupled with comments from Deputy Commerce Secretary Sam Bodman, seem to indicate U.S. officials are worried about pushing China.   \par \par \uc2  Bodman, in Baghdad, told Reuters "there may be an overly excited...response to the problems caused by the Chinese exchange rate."   \par \par \uc2  On Tuesday, Federal Reserve Governor Ben Bernanke cautioned against pushing China, saying a \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  float could be dangerous without reforms to China's banking system. He also said the yuan could actually depreciate if it was floated.   \par \par \uc2  And on Wednesday, Lawrence Lindsey, a former topic economic aide in the Bush administration, said China is not politically or economically ready for a flexible exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2  Snow also warned against introducing tariffs against China if it did not take on a more flexible \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .   \par \par \uc2  "Punitive arrangements that hurt the opportunities for good trade and for real market openings ought to be avoided, I think. They...could be dangerous," he told CNBC.   \par \par \uc2  Some U.S. lawmakers have proposed legislation which, if adopted, would slap tariffs on Chinese imports if Beijing fails to move toward a more flexible exchange rate.   \par \par \uc2  ECONOMY DOING BETTER   \par \par \uc2  Snow is in New York for one day to meet Wall Street executives and economists. He said they shared his view that the economy is doing better.   \par \par \uc2  "There was a consensus there that this economy is really beginning to move strongly in the right direction," Snow said.   \par \par \uc2  "The consensus was that we're going to see growth in the third quarter of well over 4 percent, for the second half over 4 percent and around 4 percent for next year."   \par \par \uc2  He said with growth at that level, there should be a "nice pickup" in employment.   \par \par \uc2  The labor market has lagged other areas of the economy which have been showing signs of recovery although earlier this month, the government said U.S. employers added jobs in September, for the first time in eight months.   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031015dzaf0030m\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
