\par\par \b \uc2 Asia  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2 :Dlr Surges Vs Euro, Up Vs Yen On US Econ Hopes  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Miyako Takebe  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 816 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2003\par 07:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--The dollar posted strong gains against the euro and rose more modestly against the yen in Asia Tuesday, boosted by buybacks triggered by hopes for further upbeat U.S. economic data.  \par \par \uc2 Remarks by European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg, made Monday in Moscow, added to the pressure on the euro - which plunged by more than one cent against the dollar.  \par \par \uc2 Duisenberg was reported to have said that last month's call by the Group of Seven for greater flexibility in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy wasn't aimed specifically at the euro. The G7 statement has been interpreted by many investors as a move to let the dollar depreciate to help correct the U.S. current account deficit. Traders said Duisenberg's comment was taken as an expression of concern over a stronger euro.  \par \par \uc2 But the dollar's gains Tuesday were due more to the market giving the greenback some respite from its recent selloff.  \par \par \uc2 "The dollar is being bought back a little now, given that there have been some minor signs of improvement in the U.S. economy," said Minoru Shioiri, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  manager at Mitsubishi Securities.  \par \par \uc2 Stronger U.S. stock markets and recent improvements in U.S. microeconomic data are fueling expectations of a pickup in the economy, traders said, adding that continued signs of recovery were expected from data due this week, such as the New York Fed Manufacturing index, retail sales and jobless claims.  \par \par \uc2 "The general picture should remain one of improvement," UBS said in a research note.  \par \par \uc2 Shiori added that the dollar's gains were limited against the yen given current supply and demand conditions. "It's the non-yen \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  which are benefiting."  \par \par \uc2 Indeed, European and U.S. funds bought the dollar back heavily against the euro from the outset of Asian trading, dragging the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  off a high of $1.1716 to a low of $1.1605 - with thin trading conditions accelerating the fall.  \par \par \uc2 The decline was "orderly until the $1.1650 stops were taken out," after which it "became a little choppy," said a trader at a Japanese bank, who added the next target would a minor options barrier at $1.1600, followed by $1.1550.  \par \par \uc2 But while dollar buybacks may continue Tuesday, the market remains fixated on U.S. President George W. Bush's visit to the Tokyo later this week and whether that will make it harder for the Japanese authorities to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 , traders said.  \par \par \uc2 At 0515 GMT, the dollar was quoted at Y109.30, above Y108.92 late Monday in New York. The dollar was at Y108.46, late Monday in Singapore. Tokyo markets were closed Monday for a national holiday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was at $1.1607, lower than $1.1689 late Monday in New York. The euro was at $1.1758 in Singapore Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Against the yen, the euro was quoted at Y126.86, down from Y127.30 in New York late Monday. The euro was at Y127.44 in Singapore late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 Sterling was at $1.6568, down from $1.6665 in New York Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar got a further lift from buybacks later in the session, as European players entering the market helped lift the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to a high of Y109.95.  \par \par \uc2 But \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders have said the dollar could come under pressure ahead of Bush's Friday and Saturday Tokyo trip, as Japan may be inclined not to upset Washington - which favors a more hands-off approach to the market - by \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken the yen.  \par \par \uc2 Some market players believe that the Finance Ministry and the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will let up on \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2 , resulting in a rise in the yen - as was the case when U.S. Treasury Secretary John Snow visited Japan last month.  \par \par \uc2 But a senior Finance Ministry official said Tuesday he sees no reason for the yen to strengthen due to Bush's visit, as U.S. officials have repeated their commitment to a strong dollar.  \par \par \uc2 "U.S. Treasury Secretary Snow and others have made clear that their stance on the strong dollar remains unchanged," Vice Finance Minister for International Affairs Zembei Mizoguchi told reporters.  \par \par \uc2 Still, market players are avidly watching for any signs from Bush which might reinforce the market view that the U.S. is aiming for a gradually weaker dollar.  \par \par \uc2 -By Miyako Takebe, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-5255-2929; miyako.takebe@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 -Edited by Alastair Gale  \par \par \uc2 The chart of late Tokyo \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates that accompanied Asia \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been replaced with an enhanced \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  fixture that is updated hourly.  \par \par \uc2 Subscribers can find this table:  \par \par \uc2 -On Telerate pages 4046 and 1087.  \par \par \uc2 -On Dow Jones Newswires by searching the code N/IFX  \par \par \uc2 -On Reuters by entering FRX or INT  \par \par \uc2 -On Bloomberg by entering NI FRX  \par \par \uc2 1074  \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020031014dzae0005r\pa