\par\par \b \uc2 Dollar Reverses Course, Hits Lows Vs European Majors  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Jamie McGeever  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 593 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2003\par 18:22\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK (Dow Jones)--The dollar is sharply reversing course midday Tuesday, heading south against its major rivals as dealers come to the conclusion that the unit's gains since Friday have been overdone.  \par \par \uc2 Shortly after 12.00 p.m. EDT (1600 GMT), the dollar skidded to intraday lows against the euro, Swiss franc and sterling, while only strong bids form Japanese accounts - which look suspiciously as if they're on behalf of Japanese monetary authorities - are preventing the yen from running away too.  \par \par \uc2 There appears to be little fresh fundamental news behind the dollar's turnaround, rather a general feeling the dollar was simply overbought. And the failure of encouraging corporate earnings reports to filter into further equity market gains - the Dow Jones Industrials Average is off 16 points and bond yields are heading lower Tuesday - isn't helping dollar sentiment either.  \par \par \uc2 "What we're seeing is a (consolidation) bid (to the euro) after the big selloff overnight," said Thomas Molloy, trader at Bank Leumi in New York.  \par \par \uc2 "The market got way ahead of itself on the euro," agreed David Leaver, senior trader at Gain Capital in Warren, N.J., referring to the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's slide overnight Tuesday to as low as $1.1583.  \par \par \uc2 Midday Tuesday in New York, the euro was at $1.1724, up from $1.1644 at the session open and $1.1689 late Monday in New York. The euro's intraday high Tuesday is $1.1735.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at Y109.29, down from Y109.73 earlier Tuesday, but still up from Y108.92. late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The euro was strengthening against the yen too, trading at Y128.10, up from Y127.78 at the open and Y127.30 late Monday.  \par \par \uc2 The dollar was at a session low of CHF1.3192, down from CHF1.3289 and CHF1.3239, while sterling was at its high for the day at $1.6735, up from $1.6599 and $1.6665.  \par \par \uc2 Dealers report strong appetite to buy euros from Middle Eastern accounts and global central banks who may be swapping some of their dollar reserves for euros.  \par \par \uc2 And official bids continue to prop the dollar up against the yen.  \par \par \uc2 "We believe they're still in the market, just below Y109.30," said the senior dealer at a Japanese bank in New York, referring to the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervenes\b0 \highlight\uc2  in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets on behalf of the country's \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 .  \par \par \uc2 It had all started much brighter for the dollar Tuesday, however, as growing optimism over the U.S. economic recovery prompted dealers to cover some of the massive short dollar positions in the market.  \par \par \uc2 And developments in Europe, like European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  President Wim Duisenberg's comments at the weekend implying that the euro's recent gains were overdone and a weak German business sentiment survey Tuesday also contributed to the dollar's buoyancy.  \par \par \uc2 All this, in a market where bets against the dollar are stretched to extreme levels, and it's little surprise the dollar appeared to claw back some of its recent heavy losses. The question is whether this recovery is sustainable.  \par \par \uc2 "It's sustainable for a while, as long as U.S. earnings numbers come in on the firm side and as long as U.S. economic indicators suggest things are improving," said Steven Englander, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at Barclays Capital in New York.  \par \par \uc2 -By Jamie McGeever, Dow Jones Newswires; 201 938-2096; jamie.mcgeever@dowjones.com  \par \par \uc2 1074 1863 28038 4164  \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020031014dzae00026\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
