\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 -Dollar retraces gains, follows U.S. stocks.  \b0\par\par\uc2 By Kyle Peterson  \par \uc2 587 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 14 Oktober 2003\par 19:37\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2003 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 (Updates prices, adds comment, changes byline, dateline; previous NEW YORK)  \par \par \uc2 CHICAGO, Oct 14 (Reuters) - The dollar surrendered its gains against major rivals on Tuesday after a retreat in U.S. stocks removed much of the impetus behind the dollar's rally.  \par \par \uc2 Earlier in the global session, the dollar had rallied across the board, hitting one-week highs against the euro and the yen as U.S. earnings optimism prompted a rebound in the greenback after seven straight weeks of decline.  \par \par \uc2 "We had a pretty good equity market rally yesterday, because decent earnings reports came out, but that is just pausing now. We have had some profit-taking (in dollars)" said Grant Wilson, senior \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader with Mellon Bank in Pittsburgh, Pennsylvania.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par In midday New York trade, the euro  was up 0.06 percent on the day at\par $1.1712. The dollar fell to 108.79 yen       , a loss of 0.14 percent\par on the day, and down from the session high 110.25 yen. The dollar\par slipped to 1.3180 Swiss francs       , down 0.33 percent on the day.\par The pound rose to $1.6719       , up 0.37 percent.\par   \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Earlier in the session, after weeks of concentrating on the U.S. current account deficit and the need for a weaker dollar to correct it, investors switched focus to the brighter outlook for corporate America as Bank of America (BAC.N) and Merrill Lynch (MER.N) posted encouraging results.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The greenback leapt more than 1 percent to its session highs against the yen as speculative traders scrambled to exit long yen/short dollar positions. Those gains have now evaporated.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ONE EYE ON BOJ  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Against the yen, the dollar was helped by talk that Japan was still selling yen to curb the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent export-hurting appreciation. Japan has already thrown more than 13.5 trillion yen at the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market this year in a bid to slow the yen's rise.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Analysts said the market remains nervous about the possibility that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  could step in with further yen-weakening \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Traders noted there was continued uncertainty about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  policy ahead of U.S. President George W. Bush's visit to Japan on Friday. Bush is expected to discuss \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when he meets Japanese Prime Minister Junichiro Koizumi.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I think basically we're just back to some of the themes that we've seen in the last couple of weeks or so," said Larry Brickman, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at Banc of America Securities in New York. "People are still looking at policy developments."  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among Federal Reserve officials speaking on Tuesday, Fed Governor Ben Bernanke told the Senate Banking Committee that the Fed sees risks that already low inflation could drift lower. That would put the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in a position to hold interest rates at a low level to foster a sustained recovery in the long-suffering U.S. jobs market.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Fed officials "are pretty consistent that we will keep the pedal (of low interest rates) to the metal until we see a dramatic growth in employment," said Mellon Bank's Wilson.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar's recovery started on Monday after European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  President Wim Duisenberg said a call by the Group of Seven major industrialized nations last month for exchange rate flexibility was not directed at the euro.  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "We're looking at the next week or so for there to be pressure on the dollar downward," said Banc of America's Brickman.   \f28 \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020031014dzae002q2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
